onl with a pin line, and `custody.json` has `routing.maker` with declaredAt matching.\n\nNow: if that pin line lacks `writer`, my detector flags `routing.maker.declaredAt` as `ledger-no-writer`. That would make the base test fail.\n\nHmm, but maybe the fixture was built with a ledger that DID stamp writer? No — base ledger didn't.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider. Maybe the fixture's custody-events.jsonl lines DO have `writer` because... hmm. Actually the bar says at base `fill_logger_times` rewrites logs. And T1 is about the new ledger stamping writer. The base ledger at 3df1ce5 — does it have writer? The bar T1 says \"Every line that any custody_ledger.py command appends (old and new commands) carries writer\". So at base it did NOT. So the fixture built at base has no writer.\n\nThis means: with the new emitter, job-rejected would be flagged as hand-time. That can't be right if the base test must still pass.\n\nUnless... the hand-time detection is scoped differently. Let me re-read T2:\n\n\"On held-out planted fixtures P-T2a (ledger lines with no writer), P-T2b (writer: \"hand\"), P-T2c (a time in custody.json with no ledger line) and P-T2d (a tool line overridden by a later hand line), the emitter with no switch exits 3 with refusal kind hand-time, names every hand-time field and its file:line, and writes nothing.\"\n\nSo P-T2a has ledger lines with no writer and expects refusal. But job-rejected also has ledger lines with no writer and expects exit 0 (per base test). Contradiction, unless job-rejected's events lines are all... hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the held-out fixtures P-T2a etc. are *modified copies* where they ADD a no-writer line that actually carries a time value. In P-T2a, the planted no-writer line is the one that carries a hand time. But job-rejected's no-writer pin line — hmm, still carries `at`.\n\nAlternatively: maybe the intent is that only lines that set a *time field used by the record* count. The pin line sets routing.<seat>.declaredAt. So it counts.\n\nHmm, let me look at this from a different angle. Perhaps I'm wrong that job-rejected's events lack writer. Let me consider: the base test `live-match` appends lines WITHOUT writer:\n\n```\nj = job_copy(\"live-match\")\nappend_text(j / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\", \"\".join(json.dumps(e) + \"\\n\" for e in (\n    {\"event\": \"freeze\", \"at\": \"2026-10-20T10:00:00Z\", \"bar_sha256\": ...},\n    {\"event\": \"approve\", \"role\": \"specialist\", \"by\": \"Fixture Specialist\", \"at\": \"2026-10-20T10:10:00Z\"})))\nrc, out = emit(j, TMP / \"live.json\")\nrl = json.loads((TMP / \"live.json\").read_text())[\"predicate\"] if rc == 0 else {}\nres((f\"{S} · a ledger that matches the live event log is written and says so\", rc == 0 and\n     rl[\"bar\"][\"approvals\"][1][\"x-plumbline\"][\"time_source\"].startswith(\"logged live\"), ...))\n```\n\nThis test appends freeze and approve lines without writer and expects exit 0! So under my new hand-time detection, these no-writer lines would be flagged → exit 3 → this base test FAILS.\n\nHmm. So either:\n(a) I must change this base test (but \"same results\" rule), or\n(b) the hand-time detection must not flag lines the emitter *itself* synthesizes... no.\n\nWait. This is a real tension. Let me re-read the constraint: \"Every test that exists at the base must still pass with the same results; every new test must pass.\" And \"Fixture-only or test-only fixes are not acceptable.\"\n\nHmm, but also the commission says this part covers \"tests/validate_schemas.py coverage\". So I'm expected to update tests/validate_schemas.py. Maybe the \"same results\" means the same PASS/FAIL results — i.e., every base test case still PASSES (not that its exact assertions are frozen). If I change a base test's input (add --allow-hand-times) but it still passes, is that \"same results\"? The result is PASS. Hmm.\n\nActually, I think the realistic interpretation: the harness runs the test suite and requires all test