 me collect only first and last. Actually `mk[\"_calls\"]` — let me collect for first and last:\n```\ncalls = mk.get(\"_calls\") or []\nif calls:\n    first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)\n    last = max(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)\n    collect_call_hands([first, last], hand)  # dedupe via dict\n```\nBut if first==last, fine.\n\nHmm, but `collect_call_hands` adds both sent and received entries for each call. For `first`, sent is used (startedAt); received isn't used (unless first==last). For `last`, received is used (producedAt); sent isn't used. So collecting both for each adds extra entries. \"No more and no fewer\"...\n\nOK let me be surgical. I'll add only the relevant entries:\n- startedAt uses first.sent (or created/date). If first.sent is None → hand entry for `.sent_at` (the one from hand_time_entries matching sent_at).\n- producedAt uses last.received. If last.received is None → hand entry for `.received_at`.\n\n`Call.hand_time_entries()` returns entries for both sent_at and received_at (each if missing). I can filter by field suffix.\n\nLet me write:\n```\ndef call_hand(c, which):  # which = \"sent_at\" or \"received_at\"\n    for e in c.hand_time_entries():\n        if e[\"field\"].endswith(\".\" + which):\n            return e\n    return None\n```\n\nThen:\n```\nif calls:\n    first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)\n    last = max(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)\n    e = call_hand(first, \"sent_at\")\n    if e: hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n    e = call_hand(last, \"received_at\")\n    if e: hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n```\n\nBut wait — `started` might be None if no date/created either. Then min fails. The read_log already refuses calls with `at is None`. For started, if sent None and created None and date None, started None. But read_log only checks `at`. Hmm. Let me guard: filter calls with started not None for first, at not None for last.\n\nActually `mk` already computed first/last internally (`first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)`, `last = max(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)`). So calls have started and at (else it would've errored). Fine.\n\nFor passes: the pass's at = call.received. If received None → the call's `.received_at` hand entry. So:\n```\nif call is not None:\n    e = call_hand(call, \"received_at\")\n    if e: hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n```\n\nFor controls: same.\n\nOK this is cleaner.\n\nBut hold on — `Call.hand_time_entries()` for sent_at when `self.sent is None`: it checks `if self.created: ... elif self.date: ...`. So if sent is None but both created and date exist, it emits response-created for sent_at. And for received when received is None: if date → server-header, elif created → response-created. Good.\n\nNow, there's a case: a call has sent_at present but received_at missing. Then only `.received_at` entry. Good.\n\n### x-plumbline arrays and spend\n\nLet me compute calls_sum. I need all calls (maker + evaluators). Let me accumulate `all_calls = []` as I go: maker calls + each evaluator's calls.\n\nIn build:\n```\nall_calls = []\n```\nAfter maker_history: `all_calls += mk.get(\"_calls\") or []`.\nIn evaluator loop: `all_calls += list(ev_calls[e.get(\"id\")].values())` — but ev_calls[id] is a dict name->call. Hmm, `eh.get(\"calls\", {})` is named dict. `all_calls += list((eh.get(\"calls\") or {}).values())`.\n\nActually `ev_calls[e.get(\"id\")] = eh.get(\"calls\", {})`. So `all_calls += list(ev_calls[id].values())`.\n\nThen:\n```\ncalls_sum = round(sum(call_cost(c) for c in all_calls), 6)\n```\nHmm, rounding. V5 allows 0.0001 tolerance. Let me not round aggressively; just sum floats. Actually round to avoid float noise: `round(sum(...), 6)`.\n\nspend line:\n```\nsp_lines = [e for _, e in lines if e.get(\"event\") == \"spend\"]\nsp = sp_lines[-1] if sp_lines else None\nkey_delta = float(sp.get(\"key_delta_usd\") or 0) if sp else 0.0\ntotal = max(calls_sum, key_delta)\nxp[\"spend\"] = {\"calls_sum_usd\": calls_sum, \"key_delta_usd\": key_delta, \"total_usd\": total,\n               \"tolerance_u