ool approve line with `at` matching custody.json's approval.at, then no cross_check ref. Then append a hand approve line with a DIFFERENT at → cross_check ref \"time\" (mismatch with custody.json). Hmm, that adds a \"time\" ref too. But we're testing hand-time refusal, and both fire. The output has [hand-time]. Fine. Actually the hand line's at differs from custody.json → ref time. But that's okay; we assert [hand-time] present.\n\nHmm, but for a clean P-T2d test, maybe I should make the hand line's at match custody.json so only hand-time fires. But the point of P-T2d is \"a tool line overridden by a later hand line\" → the field's source becomes hand. Let me just assert the output contains the field name with ledger-hand.\n\nLet me keep tests simple and robust.\n\n**T3 (allow):**\n```\no = TMP / \"t3-allow.json\"\nrc, out = emit(FIX, o, allow_hand=True)\nr = json.loads(o.read_text()) if o.is_file() else {}\nht = r.get(\"predicate\",{}).get(\"x-plumbline\",{}).get(\"hand_times\")\nres((\"custody · T3 with --allow-hand-times the record is written and hand_times lists the hand fields\",\n     rc == 0 and isinstance(ht, list) and len(ht) > 0 and all({\"field\",\"file\",\"line\",\"source\"} <= set(e) for e in ht)\n     and \"allowances\" in r[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"], ...))\n```\nAnd assert allowances.hand_times has switch/writer/at.\n\nAlso assert the hand_times set matches the fields we expect? Hard. Let me assert it includes \"bar.frozenAt\" and \"disposition.at\" at least (since job-rejected has no freeze/dispose lines). Actually, does job-rejected have a freeze line? Let me check the base tests: \"live-match\" appends a freeze line. \"bar-moved\" appends a freeze line. So default has no freeze → bar.frozenAt is inputs-no-ledger. Good. And no dispose line → disposition.at inputs-no-ledger. So hand_times should include both.\n\nBut wait — `routing.maker.declaredAt`: pin line exists. If it has no writer → ledger-no-writer → hand. If it has writer tool → not. Unknown. I won't assert on it.\n\nLet me assert `{\"bar.frozenAt\", \"disposition.at\"} <= {e[\"field\"] for e in ht}`.\n\n**F1h:**\nBuild a job with generation/evaluation/relay/amendment/spend lines via the ledger, emit with --allow-hand-times, assert x-plumbline arrays present with sources.\n\nHmm, this requires running the ledger commands. The base test already has `LEDGER = SNAP / \"kit\" / \"custody_ledger.py\"` and `run_py`. I can add a test that runs generation open/close etc. But that's a lot. Alternatively, append ledger lines directly (JSON) with writer stamps and assert arrays.\n\nLet me append lines directly to custody-events.jsonl:\n```\nappend_text(j/\"inputs\"/\"custody-events.jsonl\", \"\".join(json.dumps(e)+\"\\n\" for e in (\n  {\"event\":\"generation\",\"action\":\"open\",\"seat\":\"maker\",\"n\":1,\"prompt_file\":\"p.md\",\"prompt_sha256\":\"a\"*64,\"at\":\"...\",\"writer\":\"kit/custody_ledger.py@abc\"},\n  {\"event\":\"evaluation\",\"batch\":\"b1\",\"evaluator\":\"eval-a\",\"verdict_file\":\"v.json\",\"verdict_sha256\":\"b\"*64,\"at\":\"...\",\"writer\":\"...\"},\n  {\"event\":\"relay\",\"kind\":\"relay\",\"quote\":\"q\",\"from\":\"bot\",\"source_chat\":\"c\",\"source_at\":\"...\",\"at\":\"...\",\"writer\":\"...\"},\n  {\"event\":\"amendment\",\"file\":\"f.md\",\"file_sha256\":\"c\"*64,\"quote\":\"q\",\"channel\":\"c\",\"said_at\":\"...\",\"at\":\"...\",\"writer\":\"...\"},\n)))\n```\nThen emit and assert `xp[\"generations\"]`, `[\"evaluations\"]`, `[\"relays\"]`, `[\"amendments\"]` each non-empty and each element has \"source\" starting \"inputs/custody-events.jsonl:\".\n\nBut wait — the timestamps must not violate order/after-emit. The record's emittedAt is now (real clock). If I use a fixed past date like \"2026-10-20T...\", and the fixture's other times are around 2026-10-20, order checks might fire. Actually check_order checks events in the record; generation/evaluation lines aren't in check_order's `events` list (only frozenAt, approvals, startedAt, producedAt, checks, controls, disposition). So generation etc. times aren't order-checked. Good. But they're also not \"after-emit\" checked. Good.\n\nBut the ledger lines with `at` in the past — no issue.\n\nHmm, but adding a relay/evaluation line with a `verdict_file` that doesn't exist — the emitter doesn't read those files (only carries the line). Good.\n\nLet me use timestamps consistent with the fixture (2026-10-20). Actually doesn't matter.\n\n**X1:**\n- Add `x-plumbline` object to custody.json inputs, emit with --allow-hand-times, assert `predicate[\"x-plumbline\"][\"inputs\"] == inp` deep-equal.\n- Add `x-plumbline` with keys `verdict`, `gaps`, `times`, `hand_times` set to junk; emit; assert `xp[\"verdict\"]` etc. unchanged (not the junk) and `xp[\"inputs\"]` deep-equal.\n\nLet me write:\n```\nj = job_copy(\"x1\")\nc = json.loads((j/\"inputs\"/\"custody.json\").read_text())\ninp = {\"note\": \"from inputs\", \"verdict\": \"JUNK\", \"gaps\": [\"JUNK\"], \"times\": \"JUNK\", \"hand_times\": \"JUNK\"}\nc[\"x-plumbline\"] = inp\n(j/\"inputs\"/\"custody.json\").write_text(json.dumps(c, indent=2))\nrc, out = emit(j, TMP/\"x1.json\", allow_hand=True)\nrx = json.loads((TMP/\"x1.json\").read_text())[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"]\nres((\"custody · X1 inputs x-plumbline comes out deep-equal under .inputs and cannot shadow the emitter's keys\",\n     rc == 0 and rx.get(\"inputs\") == inp and rx.get(\"verdict\") != \"JUNK\"\n     and isinstance(rx.get(\"gaps\"), list) and isinstance(rx.get(\"hand_times\"), list)\n     and rx.get(\"times\") != \"JUNK\", f\"exit {rc}\"))\n```\n\nBut careful: `rx.get(\"verdict\")` for job-rejected is \"reject\" (from the §5 record). So `!= \"JUNK\"` holds. `rx[\"gaps\"]` is a list. `rx[\"hand_times\"]` is a list. `rx[\"times\"]` is a dict. Good.\n\nBut also — does adding `x-plumbline` to custody.json break anything else? The emitter reads `ci.get(\"x-plumbline\")` only for inputs. Other code ignores it. Good.\n\nHmm, but `c[\"x