one, appending another (tool) → last wins. If the existing one is hand/no-writer, my appended tool line overrides → not hand. Good.\n\nHmm, but if the existing freeze line is already tool-written, then bar.frozenAt already not hand. Either way the test passes.\n\nLet me compute bar hash: `hashlib.sha256((FIX/\"custody\"/\"bar.md\").read_bytes()).hexdigest()`. But I'm editing a copy `j`, and `j/custody/bar.md` is the same. Use `(j/\"custody\"/\"bar.md\")`.\n\nOK. But this test requires the freeze `at` to equal custody.json's frozenAt. Let me get it.\n\nActually, there's a risk: if the fixture's frozenAt is not RFC or missing. Let me guard with a conditional.\n\nHmm, let me not over-engineer. Let me focus on robust core tests.\n\n### Now let me think about whether job-rejected actually has a pin line with a writer or not, because it affects the T3 hand_times assertion.\n\nI don't know the fixture contents. Let me be conservative in assertions: only assert `{\"bar.frozenAt\", \"disposition.at\"} <= fields`, since those definitely come from custody.json with no ledger line (based on base tests appending freeze/dispose lines to test them). Actually, is there a dispose line by default? The base test doesn't append dispose lines anywhere. And `disposition.at` comes from custody.json. So yes, inputs-no-ledger. And freeze: base tests append freeze lines, implying none by default. So bar.frozenAt inputs-no-ledger. Good.\n\nBut wait — what if the fixture DOES have freeze/approve/dispose lines (with no writer)? Then they'd be ledger-no-writer hand times, still in hand_times with field bar.frozenAt. So the assertion `{\"bar.frozenAt\"} <= fields` holds either way. Good.\n\nFor `disposition.at`: if there's a dispose line (no writer) → ledger-no-writer; if not → inputs-no-ledger. Either way field is disposition.at. Good.\n\nSo assert both fields present. \n\n### Let me now also double-check: will my hand-time detection flag `passes.pass-a.at` or controls? In job-rejected, passes link to harness calls with received_at. So no. But if a control's call is linked and has received_at, no hand. Good.\n\nBut what about `startedAt`/`producedAt`? Maker log calls have sent_at/received_at? The base fixture's maker log — the base test `same-family` modifies it. It likely has sent_at/received_at (since base emitter reads them for startedAt/producedAt and expects exit 0 with no gap). Actually base emitter uses `first.sent` and `last.received`; if they were None, it'd fall back to date/created and still work, but `xp[\"times\"]` would say... hmm. The base test \"fixture job emits with no gap\" — no gap means all required fields sourced. startedAt from log (even server time) is sourced. So maybe the log lacks sent_at/received_at and uses server headers.\n\nIf the maker log calls lack sent_at/received_at, then under my rule they'd be hand times (`<log>#call.sent_at` etc.). That's fine — with --allow-hand-times they're listed. But it adds to hand_times. My assertion only checks subset, so fine.\n\nBut T4 (held-out) requires a tool-built job to have EMPTY hand_times. The tool-built job uses or_call.py which writes sent_at/received_at. So empty. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: T4's job is built by tools including or_call.py \"with its mocked transport writing the harness logs\". or_call.py writes logger sent_at/received_at. So no hand times from logs. And ledger lines all have writer. And custody.json values all have ledger lines. So empty hand_times. \n\nBut there's a catch: does the tool-built job have `disposition.at` with a dispose line? T4 lists \"dispose\" as a command used. So yes, dispose line (tool) → disposition.at not hand. Good. And freeze line → bar.frozenAt not hand. And approve lines → approvals not hand. And pin line → routing not hand. And generation/evaluation lines → arrays (not hand fields). So empty. \n\nBut what about `startedAt`/`producedAt`? or_call.py logs with sent_at/received_at → not hand. Good.\n\nWhat about `passes.<id>.at` / `controls[<id>].at`? These come from eval calls (or_call.py) with received_at → not hand. Good.\n\nSo T4 passes.\n\n### Wait, one more: `routing.<seat>.declaredAt` in the tool-built job.\n\nThe pin command writes `declaredAt` to custody.json AND a pin line. pin_for uses live.at. So not hand if writer tool. Good.\n\n### Now, the `x-plumbline.spend` for T4: no spend command listed, so no spend line. calls_sum from or_call logs. But do the logs have total_cost? The mocked transport's chat.json might include usage/cost. Not sure. If not, calls_sum=0. total=max(0,0)=0. V5 is verifier's concern with its own fixtures. For T4, spend presence isn't checked (only hand_times empty). So fine.\n\nHmm, but V5 says \"Each spend-ledger settle line matches one logged call\" and \"total_usd = max(calls_sum_usd, key_delta_usd)\". If the T4 job has no settle lines, spend = {calls_sum: maybe >0, key_delta:0, total:max}. If calls_sum >0 but no settle lines, V5's \"Each spend-ledger settle line matches one logged call\" is vacuously true. But \"total_usd = max(calls_sum, key_delta)\" holds. OK.\n\nBut if calls_sum > 0 from logs but the spend line is absent, then xp.spend.source is null. V5 might require source. Not my part. I'll include source null.\n\n### Let me now also reconsider: does the emitter need `x-plumbline.spend` to use `calls_sum_usd` from LOGS, or from the spend ledger's `ledger_sum_usd`?\n\nINTERFACE: \"spend (calls_sum_usd, key_delta_usd, total_usd, tolerance_usd: 0.0001, source)\". V5: \"calls_sum_usd equals the sum of the per-call total_cost values in the harness logs within US$0.0001\". So calls_sum from logs. key_delta from spend line. total = max. Good, matches my plan.\n\n### Alright, let me also handle: `x-plumbline.inputs` must be deep-equal. If ci[\"x-plumbline\"] is absent, don't set inputs. X1 says \"when there is one\". Good.\n\n### Let me now write the full emit_custody.py.\n\nI'll go through the original and insert changes.\n\nLet me carefully reconstruct with additions. I'll copy the original and modify.\n\nChanges list:\n1. Docstring: mention hand times and --allow-hand-times and x-plumbline arrays. (Not strictly necessary but good.)\n2. Imports: none new needed (json round-trip for deepcopy).\n3. Add helper functions: `emitter_stamp`, `ledger_lines`, `writer_source`, `call_cost`, `call_hand`.\n4. `build()` signature: add `allow_hand: bool = False`.\n5. Inside build: `hand = {}` and `mark()`.\n6. Mark freeze, approvals, pins, disposition, startedAt/producedAt, passes, controls, call hands.\n7. After computing pred and xp, add schema/hand_times/allowances/arrays/spend/inputs.\n8. Add hand-time refusals if not allow.\n9. Remove `H.fill_logger_times` calls (or keep; remove).\n10. `main()`: add `--allow-hand-times`, pass to build.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nWait — where exactly to place the hand-time refusal and x-plumbline additions relative to `check_order` and `xp[\"gaps\"]`? The current order:\n\n```\n    xp[\"emitter\"] = {...}\n    xp[\"time_tolerance_s\"] = H.TOLERANCE_S\n    xp[\"times\"] = {...}\n    tsrc = time_sources(pred, xp)\n    xp[\"timeSources\"] = tsrc\n    xp[\"time_sources\"] = dict(tsrc)\n    check_order(pred, packet, ci, now, ref)\n    xp[\"emittedAt\"] = H.iso(now)\n    xp[\"gaps\"] = gaps.rows\n    pred[\"x-plumbline\"] = xp\n    return ...\n```\n\nI'll insert after `xp[\"time_sources\"]` and before `check_order`? Or after check_order. Let me insert the x-plumbline additions after `xp[\"emittedAt\"]` and before `xp[\"gaps\"]`:\n\n```\n    xp[\"emittedAt\"] = H.iso(now)\n    xp[\"schema\"] = \"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1\"\n    xp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]\n    if hand and allow_hand:\n        xp[\"allowances\"] = {\"hand_times\": {\"switch\": \"--allow-hand-times\",\n                                           \"writer\": emitter_stamp(), \"at\": H.iso(now)}}\n    if hand and not allow_hand:\n        for e in hand.values():\n            ref.add(\"hand-time\", f\"{e['field']} {e['file']}:{e['line']} ({e['source']})\")\n    # F1h arrays\n    xp[\"generations\"] = ledger_array(lines, \"generation\")\n    ...\n    xp[\"spend\"] = spend_block(lines, all_calls)\n    inp_x = ci.get(\"x-plumbline\")\n    if isinstance(inp_x, dict):\n        xp[\"inputs\"] = json.loads(json.dumps(inp_x))\n    xp[\"gaps\"] = gaps.rows\n    pred[\"x-plumbline\"] = xp\n```\n\nBut `lines` and `all_calls` must be in scope. Let me define them early in build.\n\nAlso, `ref.add(\"hand-time\", ...)` — the base main prints `REFUSED (history)`. Good.\n\nHmm, but there's a concern: if hand times exist and not allow, we add refs. But we ALSO may have added other refs (cross_check). Fine.\n\nBut T2 says \"writes nothing\" — main handles (doesn't write if ref).\n\nNow, one more: the `hand` dict order. `[hand[k] for k in hand]` preserves insertion order. P-T2d \"later line's value, line and source are the ones used and reported\" — since I overwrite `hand[field]`, the later entry replaces. But dict insertion order keeps the FIRST insertion position with the LAST value. That's fine for content.\n\nBut wait — for P-T2d, the field is the same (e.g., bar.approvals[0].at), so overwrite. Good.\n\n### Potential issue: `line_in` for custody.json values\n\n`line_in(inputs_path, value, key)` searches for the value string in the file. If the value appears elsewhere, wrong line. But acceptable.\n\nFor `bar.frozenAt`, I pass `b.get(\"frozenAt\")` and key \"frozenAt\". Good.\n\nFor approvals, `a.get(\"at\")` and key \"at\". Good.\n\nFor disposition, `dsp.get(\"at\")` and key \"at\". Hmm, but `at` may appear in multiple places. Acceptable.\n\n### Potential issue: duplicate field names across approvals\n\n`bar.approvals[i].at` uses index i. Good, unique.\n\n### Now `all_calls`\n\nIn build, after maker_history: `all_calls += mk.get(\"_calls\") or []`. But `maker_history` returns `out` without `_calls`. I need to add it. Let me modify maker_history to include `\"_calls\": calls` in the returned dict. But `out` is used elsewhere? `mk.get(\"endpoint\")`, `mk.get(\"startedAt\")`, etc. Adding `_calls` is harmless. But `maker[\"x-plumbline\"] = mk[\"x\"]` uses mk[\"x\"]. Fine.\n\nSimilarly evaluator_history returns `{\"calls\": named, ...}`. `named` is dict name->call. I'll use `list(named.values())`. No need to add `_calls`.\n\nBut `all_calls` should include only calls used? For spend, V5 wants sum of per-call total_cost in the harness logs. All calls in the logs. So include all. Good.\n\nLet me collect: after maker_history, `all_calls += list(mk.get(\"_calls\") or [])`. But maker_history filters calls by m.get(\"calls\"). So only used calls. Hmm, for spend we want ALL calls in the log. But the fixture's log has only used calls. Fine.\n\nFor evaluators, `all_calls += list((eh.get(\"calls\") or {}).values())`. `eh.get(\"calls\")` is `named` = {name: call} for all calls. Good.\n\n### `maker_history` modification\n\nAdd `\"_calls\": calls` to out. Let me look at the return:\n```\nout = {\"startedAt\": ..., \"producedAt\": ..., \"endpoint\": ..., \"x\": {...}}\nrx = routing_x(...)\nif rx: out[\"x\"][\"routing\"] = rx\n...\nreturn out\n```\nAdd `\"_calls\": calls`.\n\nBut if `not calls: return {}` — then no `_calls`. Fine.\n\n### `collect call hands` placement\n\nFor maker: after `mk = maker_history(...)`, do:\n```\ncalls = mk.get(\"_calls\") or []\nif calls:\n    try:\n        first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)\n        last = max(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n        first = last = None\n    for c, which in ((first, \"sent_at\"), (last, \"received_at\")):\n        if c is not None:\n            e = call_hand(c, which)\n            if e: hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n```\n\nHmm, `c.started` could be None for some calls; `min` with None keys fails in py3. Let me filter:\n```\nusable = [c for c in calls if c.started is not None]\nfirst = min(usable, key=lambda c: c.started) if usable else None\nusable2 = [c for c in calls if c.at is not None]\nlast = max(usable2, key=lambda c: c.at) if usable2 else None\n```\n\nActually mk computed `first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)` already, so calls have started. But to be safe, guard.\n\nFor evaluator calls (in evaluator loop), and for passes/controls, I use `call_hand(call, \"received_at\")`.\n\n### `hand_time_entries` field format\n\n`base = f\"{self.log}#{self.name or self.i}\"`. So field = `harness-maker-fixture.log#call-1.sent_at` etc. The INTERFACE says `<log file name>#<call or index>.sent_at`. Good.\n\nBut `self.log` is `path.name` (from read_log: `Call(path.name, i, it, lines)`). So just the file name, not relative to job. INTERFACE says `<log file name>`. Good.\n\n### Now, `call_hand`:\n\n```\ndef call_hand(c, which):\n    for e in c.hand_time_entries():\n        if str(e.get(\"field\",\"\")).endswith(\".\" + which):\n            return e\n    return None\n```\n\n### Now let me reconsider the case where a maker call has no sent_at but the fixture's startedAt comes from custody.json too.\n\nIf mk has startedAt (from log fallback), we don't mark startedAt. The call-level entry covers it. Good.\n\n### Edge: `H.cross_check(ref, \"startedAt\", ci.get(\"startedAt\"), started, ...)` — if ci.startedAt differs from log by >60s → ref time. In job-rejected, is there a ci.startedAt? If yes and it matches, fine. If not, skip. So no new ref.\n\nBut if job-rejected has ci.startedAt AND the log call lacks sent_at (server time), then `started` = created/date, and cross_check compares ci.startedAt to created. If within 60s, fine. If the fixture was built so they match, fine. Since base test expects exit 0, they must be within tolerance (or ci.startedAt absent). Good.\n\n### Let me now write everything.\n\nActually, I realize I should double check the `mark` for approvals uses the right `i`. The loop is `for i, a in enumerate(b.get(\"approvals\") or [])`. And `live = ev_log[\"approve\"].get(a.get(\"role\"))`. Good.\n\n### Let me also make sure I don't break the `x-plumbline.sources` / `time_sources`.\n\nUnchanged.\n\n### Now let me consider: should `xp[\"hand_times\"]` be present even when allow and empty? T4 says \"an empty hand_times\" for no-switch tool job. So yes, always present. I set `xp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]` → [] if empty. Good.\n\n### `xp[\"allowances\"]` only when hand and allow. T3: \"the record carries the allowance x-plumbline.allowances.hand_times (switch, writer, at)\". So present when allow and non-empty. If allow and empty, no allowances (nothing to allow). Good.\n\n### Now the emitter's writer stamp must equal the file's sha256 at run time (T1 is ledger; but T3 allowances.writer is the emitter's writer). Use `emitter_stamp()`.\n\n### Let me write the file.\n\nOne more consideration: the `main()` refusal print for hand-time. The base main:\n```\nif ref:\n    print(f\"REFUSED (history): {len(ref.rows)} fact(s) ...\")\n    for kind, msg in ref.rows:\n        print(f\"  [{kind}] {msg}\")\n    ...\n    return 3\n```\nGood.\n\nBut T2: \"exits 3 with refusal kind hand-time, names every hand-time field and its file:line\". So `[hand-time] bar.frozenAt inputs/custody.json:12 (inputs-no-ledger)`. Good.\n\n### Now, is `inputs/custody.json` the right file string? `rel_inputs` is `inputs_path.relative_to(job).as_posix()` = \"inputs/custody.json\". Good. For ledger, \"inputs/custody-events.jsonl\". Good.\n\n### Let me also double check the `line` for ledger lines: `fz.get(\"_line\")`. events_log sets `e[\"_line\"] = n`. Good.\n\n### Let me now write tests/validate_schemas.py.\n\nI need to be careful: the base test file is long. I'll reproduce it with modifications. Let me plan the modifications:\n\n1. `def emit(job, out, tree=None, extra=(), allow_hand=True):` and inside add `--allow-hand-times` if allow_hand.\n2. `refused(...)` calls emit with default allow_hand=True. Good.\n3. The `emit(FIX, rec_path)` call → allow_hand=True (default). Good.\n4. Add new cases at the end (before `shutil.rmtree`).\n\nWait, but there's a problem: `refused()` uses `emit(j, o, **kw)`. If a base refusal case's ONLY refusal were hand-time, allowing would make it pass. We established none are. But let me double check `fail-order` (overlay hand-times + order). The overlay has hand times that break order. With allow_hand, order still fires. Good.\n\nBut `failures/hand-times` overlay: \"log contradicts the hand time\". cross_check \"time\" fires. Good.\n\n5. Also, the base test `out_ = refused(\"hand times typed after the maker call also break event order\", job_copy(\"fail-order\", \"hand-times\"), \"order\")` — this variable `out_` unused. Fine.\n\n6. Add T2/T3/F1h/X1 cases.\n\nNow, an important detail: the base test's `emit()` is defined as:\n```\ndef emit(job, out, tree=None, extra=()):\n    return run_py([str((tree or SNAP) / \"kit\" / \"emit_custody.py\"), \"--job-dir\", str(job), \"--out\", str(out), *extra])\n```\nI'll change to:\n```\ndef emit(job, out, tree=None, extra=(), allow_hand=True):\n    args = [str((tree or SNAP) / \"kit\" / \"emit_custody.py\"), \"--job-dir\", str(job), \"--out\", str(out), *extra]\n    if allow_hand:\n        args.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n    return run_py(args)\n```\n\nBut wait: some calls pass `extra=(\"--expect-revision\", ...)`. Order of flags doesn't matter for argparse. Good.\n\n7. But there's a subtlety: `SNAP` is a clean checkout of the working tree created by `clean_checkout(TMP / \"tree-clean\")`. This copies tracked + untracked files. It includes my new emit_custody.py and new custody_history.py (if present in the working tree). Since the harness writes my files before running, SNAP will have them. Good.\n\nBut `clean_checkout` uses `git ls-files -co --exclude-standard`. If the working tree has uncommitted changes (my new files), `-o` includes untracked, and `-c` includes tracked (with working-tree content copied via shutil.copy2). Then it commits. So SNAP has my version. Good.\n\n8. Now, the new test cases need to run after `SNAP` is defined. I'll