d\": 0.0001,\n               \"source\": f\"{LEDGER_REL}:{sp['_line']}\" if sp else None}\n```\n\nWait, but if there's no spend line and no calls, total=0. Fine.\n\nHmm, but `sp[\"_line\"]` — I set `_line` in ledger_lines. Good.\n\n### F1h arrays\n\n```\ndef arr(event):\n    return [{**{k: v for k, v in e.items() if k != \"_line\"}, \"source\": f\"{LEDGER_REL}:{e['_line']}\"}\n            for _, e in lines if e.get(\"event\") == event]\nxp[\"generations\"] = arr(\"generation\")\nxp[\"evaluations\"] = arr(\"evaluation\")\nxp[\"relays\"] = arr(\"relay\")\nxp[\"amendments\"] = arr(\"amendment\")\n```\n\nBut careful: the `events_log` function sets `e[\"_line\"]` on the dicts it returns. If I use `ledger_lines` separately, the dicts are distinct copies (parsed twice). Fine.\n\nWait, but `events_log` also parses and sets `_line`. If both parse, no conflict.\n\nHmm, one issue: the generation line has `event: \"generation\"`, and events_log stores `out[e[\"event\"]] = e` for non-approve/pin events, so `out[\"generation\"]` = last generation line. That's existing behavior for... hmm, is `ev_log[\"generation\"]` used anywhere? Not in build. So fine.\n\n### x-plumbline.inputs\n\n```\ninp_x = ci.get(\"x-plumbline\")\nif isinstance(inp_x, dict):\n    xp[\"inputs\"] = copy.deepcopy(inp_x)\n```\nNeed `import copy` or use json round-trip. `import copy` at top. Actually I'll use `json.loads(json.dumps(inp_x))` to avoid new import. But deep copy via json is fine (JSON data). Let me add `import copy` — simpler and safe. Actually the file already imports argparse, json, os, re, subprocess, sys. I'll add `copy`? Or just use json round-trip. json round-trip is fine for JSON objects.\n\n`xp[\"inputs\"] = json.loads(json.dumps(inp_x))`.\n\nBut if inp_x is not a dict (e.g., a string), skip. X1 says \"An x-plumbline object in inputs/custody.json\". So only dict.\n\n### Shadow protection\n\nX1 held-out P-X1: \"input keys that try to set the emitter's own keys (verdict, gaps, times, hand_times