ol approve line with `at` matching custody.json's approval.at, then no cross_check ref. Then append a hand approve line with a DIFFERENT at → cross_check ref \"time\" (mismatch with custody.json). Hmm, that adds a \"time\" ref too. But we're testing hand-time refusal, and both fire. The output has [hand-time]. Fine. Actually the hand line's at differs from custody.json → ref time. But that's okay; we assert [hand-time] present.\n\nHmm, but for a clean P-T2d test, maybe I should make the hand line's at match custody.json so only hand-time fires. But the point of P-T2d is \"a tool line overridden by a later hand line\" → the field's source becomes hand. Let me just assert the output contains the field name with ledger-hand.\n\nLet me keep tests simple and robust.\n\n**T3 (allow):**\n```\no = TMP / \"t3-allow.json\"\nrc, out = emit(FIX, o, allow_hand=True)\nr = json.loads(o.read_text()) if o.is_file() else {}\nht = r.get(\"predicate\",{}).get(\"x-plumbline\",{}).get(\"hand_times\")\nres((\"custody · T3 with --allow-hand-times the record is written and hand_times lists the hand fields\",\n     rc == 0 and isinstance(ht, list) and len(ht) > 0 and all({\"field\",\"file\",\"line\",\"source\"} <= set(e) for e in ht)\n     and \"allowances\" in r[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"], ...))\n```\nAnd assert allowances.hand_times has switch/writer/at.\n\nAlso assert the hand_times set matches the fields we expect? Hard. Let me assert it includes \"bar.frozenAt\" and \"disposition.at\" at least (since job-rejected has no freeze/dispose lines). Actually, does job-rejected have a freeze line? Let me check the base tests: \"live-match\" appends a freeze line. \"bar-moved\" appends a freeze line. So default has no freeze → bar.frozenAt is inputs-no-ledger. Good. And no dispose line → disposition.at inputs-no-ledger. So hand_times should include both.\n\nBut wait — `routing.maker.declaredAt`: pin line exists. If it has no writer → ledger-no-writer → hand. If it has writer tool → not. Unknown. I won't assert on it.\n\nLet me assert `{\"bar.frozenAt\", \"disposition.at\"} <= {e[\"field\"] for e in ht}`.\n\n**F1h:**\nBuild a job with generation/evaluation/relay/amendment/spend lines via the ledger, emit with --allow-hand-times, assert x-plumbline arrays present with sources.\n\nHmm, this requires running the ledger commands. The base test already has `LEDGER = SNAP / \"kit\" / \"custody_ledger.py\"` and `run_py`. I can add a test that runs generation open/close etc. But that's a lot. Alternatively, append ledger lines directly (JSON) with writer stamps and assert arrays.\n\nLet me append lines directly to custody-events.jsonl:\n```\nappend_text(j/\"inputs\"/\"custody-events.jsonl\", \"\".join(json.dumps(e)+\"\\n\" for e in (\n  {\"event\":\"generation\",\"action\":\"open\",\"seat\":\"maker\",\"n\":1,\"prompt_file\":\"p.md\",\"prompt_sha256\":\"a\"*64,\"at\":\"...\",\"writer\":\"kit/custody_ledger.py@abc\"},\n  {\"event\":\"evaluation\",\"batch\":\"b1\",\"evaluator\":\"eval-a\",\"verdict_file\":\"v.json\",\"verdict_sha256\":\"b\"*64,\"at\":\"...\",\"writer\":\"...\"},\n  {\"event\":\"relay\",\"kind\":\"relay\",\"quote\":\"q\",\"from\":\"bot\",\"source_chat\":\"c\",\"source_at\":\"...\",\"at\":\"...\",\"writer\":\"...\"},\n  {\"event\":\"amendment\",\"file\":\"f.md\",\"file_sha256\":\"c\"*64,\"quote\":\"q\",\"channel\":\"c\",\"said_at\":\"...\",\"at\":\"...\",\"writer\":\"...\"},\n)))\n```\nThen emit and assert `xp[\"generations\"]`, `[\"evaluations\"]`, `[\"relays\"]`, `[\"amendments\"]` each non-empty and each element has \"source\" starting \"inputs/custody-events.jsonl:\".\n\nBut wait — the timestamps must not violate order/after-emit. The record's emittedAt is now (real clock). If I use a fixed past date like \"2026-10-20T...\", and the fixture's other times are around 2026-10-20, order checks might fire. Actually check_order checks events in the record; generation/evaluation lines aren't in check_order's `events` list (only frozenAt, approvals, startedAt, producedAt, checks, controls, disposition). So generation etc. times aren't order-checked. Good. But they're also not \"after-emit\" checked. Good.\n\nBut the ledger lines with `at` in the past — no issue.\n\nHmm, but adding a relay/evaluation line with a `verdict_file` that doesn't exist — the emitter doesn't read those files (only carries the line). Good.\n\nLet me use timestamps consistent with the fixture (2026-10-20). Actually doesn't matter.\n\n**X1:**\n- Add `x-plumbline` object to custody.json inputs, emit with --allow-hand-times, assert `predicate[\"x-plumbline\"][\"inputs\"] == inp` deep-equal.\n- Add `x-plumbline` with keys `verdict`, `gaps`, `times`, `hand_times` set to junk; emit; assert `xp[\"verdict\"]` etc. unchanged (not the junk) and `xp[\"inputs\"]` deep-equal.\n\nLet me write:\n```\nj = job_copy(\"x1\")\nc = json.loads((j/\"inputs\"/\"custody.json\").read_text())\ninp = {\"note\": \"from inputs\", \"verdict\": \"JUNK\", \"gaps\": [\"JUNK\"], \"times\": \"JUNK\", \"hand_times\": \"JUNK\"}\nc[\"x-plumbline\"] = inp\n(j/\"inputs\"/\"custody.json\").write_text(json.dumps(c, indent=2))\nrc, out = emit(j, TMP/\"x1.json\", allow_hand=True)\nrx = json.loads((TMP/\"x1.json\").read_text())[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"]\nres((\"custody · X1 inputs x-plumbline comes out deep-equal under .inputs and cannot shadow the emitter's keys\",\n     rc == 0 and rx.get(\"inputs\") == inp and rx.get(\"verdict\") != \"JUNK\"\n     and isinstance(rx.get(\"gaps\"), list) and isinstance(rx.get(\"hand_times\"), list)\n     and rx.get(\"times\") != \"JUNK\", f\"exit {rc}\"))\n```\n\nBut careful: `rx.get(\"verdict\")` for job-rejected is \"reject\" (from the §5 record). So `!= \"JUNK\"` holds. `rx[\"gaps\"]` is a list. `rx[\"hand_times\"]` is a list. `rx[\"times\"]` is a dict. Good.\n\nBut also — does adding `x-plumbline` to custody.json break anything else? The emitter reads `ci.get(\"x-plumbline\")` only for inputs. Other code ignores it. Good.\n\nHmm, but `c[\"x-plumbline\"]` with `hand_times: \"JUNK\"` — the emitter sets `xp[\"hand_times\"]` independently. Good.\n\nWait, but `c[\"x-plumbline\"]` keys `verdict` etc. — does the emitter's `xp` get `verdict` from `ci.get(\"verdict\")` or from the §5 record? It's from `rec.get(\"verdict\")`. So `xp[\"verdict\"]` = \"reject\". Not \"JUNK\". Good.\n\n**T4-like clean check (optional):** Building a fully tool-built job is heavy. Skip; the held-out T4 covers it. But I can add a test that a tool-written ledger line (with writer) is NOT a hand time. E.g., append a freeze line with a tool writer matching custody.json's frozenAt → no hand time for bar.frozenAt. Hmm, but other fields still hand. Hard to isolate.\n\nLet me instead test: take job-rejected, append a freeze line with writer=kit/custody_ledger.py@... and at matching custody.json bar.frozenAt. Then emit with --allow-hand-times and assert \"bar.frozenAt\" NOT in hand_times. That tests tool lines aren't flagged. But custody.json frozenAt must match. Let me read it.\n\nActually, simpler: assert that a tool-written line isn't flagged. But other fields... Let me do:\n```\nj = job_copy(\"t4-tool\")\nc = json.loads((j/\"inputs\"/\"custody.json\").read_text())\nfrozen = c[\"bar\"][\"frozenAt\"]\nappend_text(j/\"inputs\"/\"custody-events.jsonl\", json.dumps({\"event\":\"freeze\",\"at\":frozen,\"bar_sha256\":sha256(bar.md),\"writer\":\"kit/custody_ledger.py@abc\"})+\"\\n\")\nrc, out = emit(j, TMP/\"t4.json\", allow_hand=True)\nrx = json.loads(...)[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"]\nres((\"custody · a tool-written ledger line is not a hand time\", \"bar.frozenAt\" not in {e[\"field\"] for e in rx[\"hand_times\"]}, ...))\n```\n\nBut wait — `bar_sha256` must match the actual bar file hash, else \"bar changed after freeze\" ref (order). Let me compute it.\n\nAlso, the freeze line's `at` must match custody.json's frozenAt to avoid cross_check \"time\" ref. If they match, no ref. Good.\n\nBut is there a freeze line already? If the fixture has one, appending another (tool) → last wins. If the existing one is hand/no-writer, my appended tool line overrides → not hand. Good.\n\nHmm, but if the existing freeze line is already tool-written, then bar.frozenAt already not hand. Either way the test passes.\n\nLet me compute bar hash: `hashlib.sha256((FIX/\"custody\"/\"bar.md\").read_bytes()).hexdigest()`. But I'm editing a copy `j`, and `j/custody/bar.md` is the same. Use `(j/\"custody\"/\"bar.md\")`.\n\nOK. But this test requires the freeze `at` to equal custody.json's frozenAt. Let me get it.\n\nActually, there's a risk: if the fixture's frozenAt is not RFC or missing. Let me guard with a conditional.\n\nHmm, let me not over-engineer. Let me focus on robust core tests.\n\n### Now let me think about whether job-rejected actually has a pin line with a writer or not, because it affects the T3 hand_times assertion.\n\nI don't know the fixture contents. Let me be conservative in assertions: only assert `{\"bar.frozenAt\", \"disposition.at\"} <= fields`, since those definitely come from custody.json with no ledger line (based on base tests appending freeze/dispose lines to test them). Actually, is there a dispose line by default? The base test doesn't append dispose lines anywhere. And `disposition.at` comes from custody.json. So yes, inputs-no-ledger. And freeze: base tests append freeze lines, implying none by default. So bar.frozenAt inputs-no-ledger. Good.\n\nBut wait — what if the fixture DOES have freeze/approve/dispose lines (with no writer)? Then they'd be ledger-no-writer hand times, still in hand_times with field bar.frozenAt. So the assertion `{\"bar.frozenAt\"} <= fields` holds either way. Good.\n\nFor `disposition.at`: if there's a dispose line (no writer) → ledger-no-writer; if not → inputs-no-ledger. Either way field is disposition.at. Good.\n\nSo assert both fields present. \n\n### Let me now also double-check: will my hand-time detection flag `passes.pass-a.at` or controls? In job-rejected, passes link to harness calls with received_at. So no. But if a control's call is linked and has received_at, no hand. Good.\n\nBut what about `startedAt`/`producedAt`? Maker log calls have sent_at/received_at? The base fixture's maker log — the base test `same-family` modifies it. It likely has sent_at/received_at (since base emitter reads them for startedAt/producedAt and expects exit 0 with no gap). Actually base emitter uses `first.sent` and `last.received`; if they were None, it'd fall back to date/created and still work, but `xp[\"times\"]` would say... hmm. The base test \"fixture job emits with no gap\" — no gap means all required fields sourced. startedAt from log (even server time) is sourced. So maybe the log lacks sent_at/received_at and uses server headers.\n\nIf the maker log calls lack sent_at/received_at, then under my rule they'd be hand times (`<log>#call.sent_at` etc.). That's fine — with --allow-hand-times they're listed. But it adds to hand_times. My assertion only checks subset, so fine.\n\nBut T4 (held-out) requires a tool-built job to have EMPTY hand_times. The tool-built job uses or_call.py which writes sent_at/received_at. So empty. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: T4's job is built by tools including or_call.py \"with its mocked transport writing the harness logs\". or_call.py writes logger sent_at/received_at. So no hand times from logs. And ledger lines all have writer. And custody.json values all have ledger lines. So empty hand_times. \n\nBut there's a catch: does the tool-built job have `disposition.at` with a dispose line? T4 lists \"dispose\" as a command used. So yes, dispose line (tool) → disposition.at not hand. Good. And freeze line → bar.frozenAt not hand. And approve lines → approvals not hand. And pin line → routing not hand. And generation/evaluation lines → arrays (not hand fields). So empty. \n\nBut what about `startedAt`/`producedAt`? or_call.py logs with sent_at/received_at → not hand. Good.\n\nWhat about `passes.<id>.at` / `controls[<id>].at`? These come from eval calls (or_call.py) with received_at → not hand. Good.\n\nSo T4 passes.\n\n### Wait, one more: `routing.<seat>.declaredAt` in the tool-built job.\n\nThe pin command writes `declaredAt` to custody.json AND a pin line. pin_for uses live.at. So not hand if writer tool. Good.\n\n### Now, the `x-plumbline.spend` for T4: no spend command listed, so no spend line. calls_sum from or_call logs. But do the logs have total_cost? The mocked transport's chat.json might include usage/cost. Not sure. If not, calls_sum=0. total=max(0,0)=0. V5 is verifier's concern with its own fixtures. For T4, spend presence isn't checked (only hand_times empty). So fine.\n\nHmm, but V5 says \"Each spend-ledger settle line matches one logged call\" and \"total_usd = max(calls_sum_usd, key_delta_usd)\". If the T4 job has no settle lines, spend = {calls_sum: maybe >0, key_delta:0, total:max}. If calls_sum >0 but no settle lines, V5's \"Each spend-ledger settle line matches one logged call\" is vacuously true. But \"total_usd = max(calls_sum, key_delta)\" holds. OK.\n\nBut if calls_sum > 0 from logs but the spend line is absent, then xp.spend.source is null. V5 might require source. Not my part. I'll include source null.\n\n### Let me now also reconsider: does the emitter need `x-plumbline.spend` to use `calls_sum_usd` from LOGS, or from the spend ledger's `ledger_sum_usd`?\n\nINTERFACE: \"spend (calls_sum_usd, key_delta_usd, total_usd, tolerance_usd: 0.0001, source)\". V5: \"calls_sum_usd equals the sum of the per-call total_cost values in the harness logs within US$0.0001\". So calls_sum from logs. key_delta from spend line. total = max. Good, matches my plan.\n\n### Alright, let me also handle: `x-plumbline.inputs` must be deep-equal. If ci[\"x-plumbline\"] is absent, don't set inputs. X1 says \"when there is one\". Good.\n\n### Let me now write the full emit_custody.py.\n\nI'll go through the original and insert changes.\n\nLet me carefully reconstruct with additions. I'll copy the original and modify.\n\nChanges list:\n1. Docstring: mention hand times and --allow-hand-times and x-plumbline arrays. (Not strictly necessary but good.)\n2. Imports: none new needed (json round-trip for deepcopy).\n3. Add helper functions: `emitter_stamp`, `ledger_lines`, `writer_source`, `call_cost`, `call_hand`.\n4. `build()` signature: add `allow_hand: bool = False`.\n5. Inside build: `hand = {}` and `mark()`.\n6. Mark freeze, approvals, pins, disposition, startedAt/producedAt, passes, controls, call hands.\n7. After computing pred and xp, add schema/hand_times/allowances/arrays/spend/inputs.\n8. Add hand-time refusals if not allow.\n9. Remove `H.fill_logger_times` calls (or keep; remove).\n10. `main()`: add `--allow-hand-times`, pass to build.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nWait — where exactly to place the hand-time refusal and x-plumbline additions relative to `check_order` and `xp[\"gaps\"]`? The current order:\n\n```\n    xp[\"emitter\"] = {...}\n    xp[\"time_tolerance_s\"] = H.TOLERANCE_S\n    xp[\"times\"] = {...}\n    tsrc = time_sources(pred, xp)\n    xp[\"timeSources\"] = tsrc\n    xp[\"time_sources\"] = dict(tsrc)\n    check_order(pred, packet, ci, now, ref)\n    xp[\"emittedAt\"] = H.iso(now)\n    xp[\"gaps\"] = gaps.rows\n    pred[\"x-plumbline\"] = xp\n    return ...\n```\n\nI'll insert after `xp[\"time_sources\"]` and before `check_order`? Or after check_order. Let me insert the x-plumbline additions after `xp[\"emittedAt\"]` and before `xp[\"gaps\"]`:\n\n```\n    xp[\"emittedAt\"] = H.iso(now)\n    xp[\"schema\"] = \"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1\"\n    xp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]\n    if hand and allow_hand:\n        xp[\"allowances\"] = {\"hand_times\": {\"switch\": \"--allow-hand-times\",\n                                           \"writer\": emitter_stamp(), \"at\": H.iso(now)}}\n    if hand and not allow_hand:\n        for e in hand.values():\n            ref.add(\"hand-time\", f\"{e['field']} {e['file']}:{e['line']} ({e['source']})\")\n    # F1h arrays\n    xp[\"generations\"] = ledger_array(lines, \"generation\")\n    ...\n    xp[\"spend\"] = spend_block(lines, all_calls)\n    inp_x = ci.get(\"x-plumbline\")\n    if isinstance(inp_x, dict):\n        xp[\"inputs\"] = json.loads(json.dumps(inp_x))\n    xp[\"gaps\"] = gaps.rows\n    pred[\"x-plumbline\"] = xp\n```\n\nBut `lines` and `all_calls` must be in scope. Let me define them early in build.\n\nAlso, `ref.add(\"hand-time\", ...)` — the base main prints `REFUSED (history)`. Good.\n\nHmm, but there's a concern: if hand times exist and not allow, we add refs. But we ALSO may have added other refs (cross_check). Fine.\n\nBut T2 says \"writes nothing\" — main handles (doesn't write if ref).\n\nNow, one more: the `hand` dict order. `[hand[k] for k in hand]` preserves insertion order. P-T2d \"later line's value, line and source are the ones used and reported\" — since I overwrite `hand[field]`, the later entry replaces. But dict insertion order keeps the FIRST insertion position with the LAST value. That's fine for content.\n\nBut wait — for P-T2d, the field is the same (e.g., bar.approvals[0].at), so overwrite. Good.\n\n### Potential issue: `line_in` for custody.json values\n\n`line_in(inputs_path, value, key)` searches for the value string in the file. If the value appears elsewhere, wrong line. But acceptable.\n\nFor `bar.frozenAt`, I pass `b.get(\"frozenAt\")` and key \"frozenAt\". Good.\n\nFor approvals, `a.get(\"at\")` and key \"at\". Good.\n\nFor disposition, `dsp.get(\"at\")` and key \"at\". Hmm, but `at` may appear in multiple places. Acceptable.\n\n### Potential issue: duplicate field names across approvals\n\n`bar.approvals[i].at` uses index i. Good, unique.\n\n### Now `all_calls`\n\nIn build, after maker_history: `all_calls += mk.get(\"_calls\") or []`. But `maker_history` returns `out` without `_calls`. I need to add it. Let me modify maker_history to include `\"_calls\": calls` in the returned dict. But `out` is used elsewhere? `mk.get(\"endpoint\")`, `mk.get(\"startedAt\")`, etc. Adding `_calls` is harmless. But `maker[\"x-plumbline\"] = mk[\"x\"]` uses mk[\"x\"]. Fine.\n\nSimilarly evaluator_history returns `{\"calls\": named, ...}`. `named` is dict name->call. I'll use `list(named.values())`. No need to add `_calls`.\n\nBut `all_calls` should include only calls used? For spend, V5 wants sum of per-call total_cost in the harness logs. All calls in the logs. So include all. Good.\n\nLet me collect: after maker_history, `all_calls += list(mk.get(\"_calls\") or [])`. But maker_history filters calls by m.get(\"calls\"). So only used calls. Hmm, for spend we want ALL calls in the log. But the fixture's log has only used calls. Fine.\n\nFor evaluators, `all_calls += list((eh.get(\"calls\") or {}).values())`. `eh.get(\"calls\")` is `named` = {name: call} for all calls. Good.\n\n### `maker_history` modification\n\nAdd `\"_calls\": calls` to out. Let me look at the return:\n```\nout = {\"startedAt\": ..., \"producedAt\": ..., \"endpoint\": ..., \"x\": {...}}\nrx = routing_x(...)\nif rx: out[\"x\"][\"routing\"] = rx\n...\nreturn out\n```\nAdd `\"_calls\": calls`.\n\nBut if `not calls: return {}` — then no `_calls`. Fine.\n\n### `collect call hands` placement\n\nFor maker: after `mk = maker_history(...)`, do:\n```\ncalls = mk.get(\"_calls\") or []\nif calls:\n    try:\n        first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)\n        last = max(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n        first = last = None\n    for c, which in ((first, \"sent_at\"), (last, \"received_at\")):\n        if c is not None:\n            e = call_hand(c, which)\n            if e: hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n```\n\nHmm, `c.started` could be None for some calls; `min` with None keys fails in py3. Let me filter:\n```\nusable = [c for c in calls if c.started is not None]\nfirst = min(usable, key=lambda c: c.started) if usable else None\nusable2 = [c for c in calls if c.at is not None]\nlast = max(usable2, key=lambda c: c.at) if usable2 else None\n```\n\nActually mk computed `first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)` already, so calls have started. But to be safe, guard.\n\nFor evaluator calls (in evaluator loop), and for passes/controls, I use `call_hand(call, \"received_at\")`.\n\n### `hand_time_entries` field format\n\n`base = f\"{self.log}#{self.name or self.i}\"`. So field = `harness-maker-fixture.log#call-1.sent_at` etc. The INTERFACE says `<log file name>#<call or index>.sent_at`. Good.\n\nBut `self.log` is `path.name` (from read_log: `Call(path.name, i, it, lines)`). So just the file name, not relative to job. INTERFACE says `<log file name>`. Good.\n\n### Now, `call_hand`:\n\n```\ndef call_hand(c, which):\n    for e in c.hand_time_entries():\n        if str(e.get(\"field\",\"\")).endswith(\".\" + which):\n            return e\n    return None\n```\n\n### Now let me reconsider the case where a maker call has no sent_at but the fixture's startedAt comes from custody.json too.\n\nIf mk has startedAt (from log fallback), we don't mark startedAt. The call-level entry covers it. Good.\n\n### Edge: `H.cross_check(ref, \"startedAt\", ci.get(\"startedAt\"), started, ...)` — if ci.startedAt differs from log by >60s → ref time. In job-rejected, is there a ci.startedAt? If yes and it matches, fine. If not, skip. So no new ref.\n\nBut if job-rejected has ci.startedAt AND the log call lacks sent_at (server time), then `started` = created/date, and cross_check compares ci.startedAt to created. If within 60s, fine. If the fixture was built so they match, fine. Since base test expects exit 0, they must be within tolerance (or ci.startedAt absent). Good.\n\n### Let me now write everything.\n\nActually, I realize I should double check the `mark` for approvals uses the right `i`. The loop is `for i, a in enumerate(b.get(\"approvals\") or [])`. And `live = ev_log[\"approve\"].get(a.get(\"role\"))`. Good.\n\n### Let me also make sure I don't break the `x-plumbline.sources` / `time_sources`.\n\nUnchanged.\n\n### Now let me consider: should `xp[\"hand_times\"]` be present even when allow and empty? T4 says \"an empty hand_times\" for no-switch tool job. So yes, always present. I set `xp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]` → [] if empty. Good.\n\n### `xp[\"allowances\"]` only when hand and allow. T3: \"the record carries the allowance x-plumbline.allowances.hand_times (switch, writer, at)\". So present when allow and non-empty. If allow and empty, no allowances (nothing to allow). Good.\n\n### Now the emitter's writer stamp must equal the file's sha256 at run time (T1 is ledger; but T3 allowances.writer is the emitter's writer). Use `emitter_stamp()`.\n\n### Let me write the file.\n\nOne more consideration: the `main()` refusal print for hand-time. The base main:\n```\nif ref:\n    print(f\"REFUSED (history): {len(ref.rows)} fact(s) ...\")\n    for kind, msg in ref.rows:\n        print(f\"  [{kind}] {msg}\")\n    ...\n    return 3\n```\nGood.\n\nBut T2: \"exits 3 with refusal kind hand-time, names every hand-time field and its file:line\". So `[hand-time] bar.frozenAt inputs/custody.json:12 (inputs-no-ledger)`. Good.\n\n### Now, is `inputs/custody.json` the right file string? `rel_inputs` is `inputs_path.relative_to(job).as_posix()` = \"inputs/custody.json\". Good. For ledger, \"inputs/custody-events.jsonl\". Good.\n\n### Let me also double check the `line` for ledger lines: `fz.get(\"_line\")`. events_log sets `e[\"_line\"] = n`. Good.\n\n### Let me now write tests/validate_schemas.py.\n\nI need to be careful: the base test file is long. I'll reproduce it with modifications. Let me plan the modifications:\n\n1. `def emit(job, out, tree=None, extra=(), allow_hand=True):` and inside add `--allow-hand-times` if allow_hand.\n2. `refused(...)` calls emit with default allow_hand=True. Good.\n3. The `emit(FIX, rec_path)` call → allow_hand=True (default). Good.\n4. Add new cases at the end (before `shutil.rmtree`).\n\nWait, but there's a problem: `refused()` uses `emit(j, o, **kw)`. If a base refusal case's ONLY refusal were hand-time, allowing would make it pass. We established none are. But let me double check `fail-order` (overlay hand-times + order). The overlay has hand times that break order. With allow_hand, order still fires. Good.\n\nBut `failures/hand-times` overlay: \"log contradicts the hand time\". cross_check \"time\" fires. Good.\n\n5. Also, the base test `out_ = refused(\"hand times typed after the maker call also break event order\", job_copy(\"fail-order\", \"hand-times\"), \"order\")` — this variable `out_` unused. Fine.\n\n6. Add T2/T3/F1h/X1 cases.\n\nNow, an important detail: the base test's `emit()` is defined as:\n```\ndef emit(job, out, tree=None, extra=()):\n    return run_py([str((tree or SNAP) / \"kit\" / \"emit_custody.py\"), \"--job-dir\", str(job), \"--out\", str(out), *extra])\n```\nI'll change to:\n```\ndef emit(job, out, tree=None, extra=(), allow_hand=True):\n    args = [str((tree or SNAP) / \"kit\" / \"emit_custody.py\"), \"--job-dir\", str(job), \"--out\", str(out), *extra]\n    if allow_hand:\n        args.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n    return run_py(args)\n```\n\nBut wait: some calls pass `extra=(\"--expect-revision\", ...)`. Order of flags doesn't matter for argparse. Good.\n\n7. But there's a subtlety: `SNAP` is a clean checkout of the working tree created by `clean_checkout(TMP / \"tree-clean\")`. This copies tracked + untracked files. It includes my new emit_custody.py and new custody_history.py (if present in the working tree). Since the harness writes my files before running, SNAP will have them. Good.\n\nBut `clean_checkout` uses `git ls-files -co --exclude-standard`. If the working tree has uncommitted changes (my new files), `-o` includes untracked, and `-c` includes tracked (with working-tree content copied via shutil.copy2). Then it commits. So SNAP has my version. Good.\n\n8. Now, the new test cases need to run after `SNAP` is defined. I'll add them near the end, before `shutil.rmtree(TMP, ...)`.\n\nLet me now also think: the T3 test asserts `rc == 0` for job-rejected with allow_hand. But does job-rejected emit exit 0 at base? Yes (base test). With allow_hand, hand times silenced, other refs none → 0. Good.\n\nBut wait — I add `xp[\"schema\"]`, `xp[\"hand_times\"]`, etc. Does the custody schema validation still pass? The base test `check(S, \"emitted record is valid against the PINNED custody schema\", True, rec, V_CUST)` uses `rec` from the emit. If my additions break V_CUST, this base test fails. Risk. Let me think about whether the pinned custody schema restricts x-plumbline.\n\nThe base record's x-plumbline has: job_id, sources, events_log, terminal_state, outcome, verdict, iteration, s5_record, earlier_iterations, pass_rule, disposition_source, packet, emitter, time_tolerance_s, times, timeSources, time_sources, emittedAt, gaps, disposition_at_source. That's a lot of arbitrary keys. For V_CUST to accept, x-plumbline must be `additionalProperties: true` or have no constraints. So adding more keys is fine.\n\nBut `hand_times` is an array of objects. If x-plumbline has `additionalProperties: true` and no property schema, fine. If it has `properties` with specific types for known keys and `additionalProperties: true`, new keys are fine. So likely OK.\n\nHmm, but what if the pinned schema defines `x-plumbline` as `{\"type\": \"object\"}` with no additionalProperties → defaults to true. Fine.\n\nActually, custody SPEC section 4 says \"producer fields only under x-\", implying x- is open. So fine.\n\nBut `hand_times[].line` could be `null`. If the schema doesn't constrain, fine.\n\nOK. But there's another risk: the derived `custody-record.schema.json` (V_CREC). The base test `check(S, \"emitted record is valid against the derived Plumbline custody schema\", True, rec, V_CREC)`. If the derived schema has stricter x-plumbline... Let me check: the derived schema is built from the pinned schema plus Plumbline rules. The base rec passes. My additions should too if the derived schema doesn't forbid them. But the derived schema might ADD constraints on x-plumbline (e.g., requiring x-plumbline.verdict enum). The base test \"x-plumbline.verdict outside Plumbline's four verdicts is refused\" shows the derived schema constrains x-plumbline.verdict. So the derived schema HAS properties for x-plumbline. Does it allow additional keys? If `additionalProperties` is not false, yes.\n\nHmm, this is a real risk. If the derived schema sets `additionalProperties: false` on x-plumbline and lists specific keys, my new keys (schema, hand_times, allowances, generations, evaluations, relays, amendments, spend, inputs) would be rejected → base test fails.\n\nBut wait — the bar REQUIRES the emitter to write these keys into x-plumbline (INTERFACE section 2). And the schemas are frozen at base. So the derived schema must already allow them (the bar designers would have checked). Actually, the derived schema is generated by `tools/build_schemas.py --custody` from the pinned schema. If the pinned schema allows arbitrary x-plumbline, so does the derived. And the base test's \"x-plumbline.verdict outside four verdicts is refused\" — that means the derived schema DOES constrain verdict. How? Maybe build_schemas adds a Plumbline-specific schema layer.\n\nHmm. Let me think. `schemas/custody-record.schema.json` is derived and pinned at base. The base test validates the base record against it and expects True. The base record has `x-plumbline.verdict`. The derived schema must allow x-plumbline with at least these keys. If it uses `additionalProperties: false` with an explicit list, then the list includes all base keys but NOT my new keys → fail.\n\nThis is dangerous. Let me consider: maybe the derived schema is permissive on x-plumbline (only constrains verdict via `properties.verdict.enum` but additionalProperties true). Then fine.\n\nGiven the bar explicitly says the emitter writes `schema`, `hand_times`, `allowances`, `generations`, etc. into x-plumbline, and the schemas are frozen, the ONLY way this works is if the schema permits them. So I'll trust that. If the base test fails, the harness will tell me and I'll adjust (e.g., the schema might need... but I can't edit schemas). Hmm.\n\nWait, actually — maybe the derived schema at base already anticipates these keys? Unlikely, since they're new. But `additionalProperties: true` is the default in JSON Schema. So unless explicitly false, new keys are allowed. The base test constraining verdict suggests `properties.verdict.enum` but `additionalProperties` default true. So adding keys is fine. \n\nBut `hand_times` with wrong structure — if the schema has `properties.hand_times` with a strict type... it wouldn't at base (new key). So no constraint. Fine.\n\nI'll proceed.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider: maybe `x-plumbline` in the derived schema is `{\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"verdict\": {...}}, \"additionalProperties\": true}`. Then fine.\n\nOK.\n\n9. One more: the base test `bad = copy.deepcopy(rec); bad[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"][\"verdict\"] = \"pass\"; check(S, \"x-plumbline.verdict outside Plumbline's four verdicts is refused\", False, bad, V_CREC)`. This still works (verdict enum).\n\n10. The base test `bad = copy.deepcopy(rec); bad[\"predicate\"][\"violations\"][0][\"x-plumbline\"].pop(\"locator\", None)` — still works.\n\nOK.\n\n### Let me also reconsider T5(b): \"Every refusal case that exits 3 at base exits 3 on the subject, with a superset of base's refusal kinds, both without and with --allow-hand-times.\"\n\nMy test helper uses --allow-hand-times for all synthetic cases. So the \"with switch\" path is tested. The \"without switch\" path adds hand-time refs. The held-out evaluator tests both. My emitter: without switch, hand-time refs added (superset). With switch, same as base. Good.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle issue. Consider a base refusal case that exits 3 due to, say, \"endpoint\". WITHOUT the switch, my emitter ALSO adds hand-time refs. So it exits 3 with kinds {endpoint, hand-time} — superset. Good. WITH the switch, only {endpoint}. Same as base. Good.\n\nBut what about a base case that exits 0 (e.g., `agent-adopted`)? WITHOUT the switch, my emitter adds hand-time refs → exits 3! That would break \"every test that exists at base must still pass\" if the test runs without the switch. But my test helper uses the switch, so it exits 0. Good for my tests. But the held-out evaluator might run the base fixture `agent-adopted` scenario... no, held-out cases are T2-T7 fixtures.\n\nHmm, but T5(b) only talks about refusal cases (exit 3 at base). Clean cases (exit 0) aren't covered by T5(b). But G2 \"every test that exists at base must still pass\". My tests pass because I use the switch. Good.\n\nBut wait — the bar's T4 says the tool-built job emits with NO switch. That's a different job (held-out). Fine.\n\n### OK, let me now also think about whether the evaluator's T2 held-out fixtures would be refused correctly.\n\nP-T2a: \"ledger lines with no writer\". So they take a clean tool-built job (or fixture) and strip `writer` from a ledger line. My `writer_source` returns \"ledger-no-writer\" → mark hand → refuse. Good. But which field? The line's event type. If it's a freeze line → bar.frozenAt. If approve → approvals. If pin → routing. If dispose → disposition. If generation/evaluation/etc. → no field marked (not in the hand-time field list). Hmm. If P-T2a strips writer from a `generation` line, my code wouldn't flag it. But T2 says \"names every hand-time field\" — generation times aren't in the field list, so maybe P-T2a uses a freeze/approve/pin/dispose line. I'll assume so.\n\nActually, to be safe, maybe I should also consider generation/evaluation/relay/amendment/spend lines' `at` as hand times? But the field list doesn't include them. The bar's field list is explicit. So no.\n\nHmm, but \"ledger lines with no writer\" is broad. Let me re-read P-T2a: \"ledger lines with no writer\". If the fixture has a freeze line without writer, my code flags bar.frozenAt. Good.\n\nBut what if the fixture has ONLY generation lines without writer and no freeze/approve/etc.? Then my code wouldn't flag. But the record would still have typed times in custody.json (frozenAt etc.) → flagged as inputs-no-ledger. So it'd still refuse. Good — as long as the fixture has hand-typed custody.json values. Since P-T2a is a \"planted fixture\", it likely has both.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: flag ANY ledger line with no writer/hand that carries a time used by the record. The used times are the ones I enumerate. Generation etc. times aren't used in the record's time fields. So OK.\n\n### P-T2c: \"a time in custody.json with no ledger line\". E.g., frozenAt typed, no freeze line → inputs-no-ledger. My code flags. Good.\n\n### P-T2d: \"a tool line overridden by a later hand line\". E.g., a tool approve line then a hand approve line. My `events_log` keeps the LAST approve per role → the hand line → `live` is hand → mark ledger-hand. Good. And `writer_source(live)` = ledger-hand. \n\nBut careful: `events_log` does `out[\"approve\"][e.get(\"role\")] = e` — last wins. And sets `_line`. Good. So the later hand line is `live`. Mark hand. \n\n### P-T2b: `writer: \"hand\"` → ledger-hand. Good.\n\n### Now, what about the `at` value for the field in the refusal? T2 wants \"names every hand-time field and its file:line\". My message includes field, file:line, source. Good.\n\n### Let me also make sure the refusal happens BEFORE writing. main checks `if ref:` before writing. Good.\n\n### One more: T3 \"x-plumbline.hand_times[] lists exactly the hand-time fields, no more and no fewer, each with file:line, and the record carries the allowance\".\n\nMy hand_times = all marked fields. If I over-mark (e.g., call-level entries for unused calls), it's \"more\". Let me be conservative: only mark call-level entries for calls whose times are actually used (first maker call sent_at, last maker call received_at, linked pass/control calls received_at). I've designed that. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: for a pass linked to a call, I mark `.received_at` if missing. But the pass's `at` = call.received. If call.received exists, no mark. Good. But what if the call has received_at but the pass ALSO has a typed `at` that differs? cross_check fires \"time\". Not hand. Good.\n\n### Now, what about `controls[<id>].at` when a call is linked but the control ALSO has a typed `at`? The code uses `call.at` (log) and cross_checks the typed. So log wins. No hand unless call missing received.\n\n### OK.\n\n### Let me now write the code. I'll be meticulous.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one thing: the `hand` marking for approvals. In the record, `approvals.append({... \"at\": ts(a.get(\"at\"), ...)})`. So the record's approval.at is from custody.json. If `live` exists and is tool, custody.json's `a.at` was written by the tool (approve command writes appr with at=t). So not hand. Good. If `live` is hand, mark. If no live, mark inputs-no-ledger.\n\nBut what if `live` exists (tool) but custody.json's a.at was hand-edited to a different value? Then cross_check ref \"time\" (mismatch). Record refused. So no hand needed. Good.\n\n### Let me write.\n\nI'll now produce the full emit_custody.py. Let me carefully transcribe the original and insert.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the docstring update. The task says whole file. I'll update the docstring minimally to mention the new behaviour (helps J1/J2/G3 but those are other parts). I'll add a paragraph.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"...\"\"\"\n```\nI'll keep the original docstring and append a paragraph about hand times.\n\nLet me write the helpers after `line_in` or near the top.\n\nNow, careful about `call_cost`: `c.raw` — `Call` has `.raw`. Good.\n\nLet me write `ledger_array`:\n```\ndef ledger_array(lines, event):\n    return [{**{k: v