n- In terminal block, call emitter with allow flag.\n- After emitter success, run verifier. If verifier fails, log close_refused and return code. Do not log closed.\n- Move `log_row(job,\"closed\",...)` to after custody/verify success.\n- If no terminal state, no custody/verify; log closed and return code.\n- If code !=0, return code (no closed row? Base did not log closed if code !=0). Good.\n- Need `run_verifier(job, a)` helper:\n```\ndef run_verifier(job, a) -> tuple[int,str]:\n    custody = a.custody or os.environ.get(\"PLUMBLINE_CUSTODY\")\n    if not custody:\n        print(\"NOT CLOSED: Plumbline verify cannot run (no --custody and no $PLUMBLINE_CUSTODY).\")\n        return 2, \"cannot-run\"\n    record = job/\"custody\"/\"record.custody.json\"\n    files = job/\"custody\"/\"files\"\n    evidence = job/\"custody\"/\"evidence\"\n    ledger = job/\"inputs\"/\"custody-events.jsonl\"\n    args = [\"--record\",str(record),\"--files\",str(files),\"--evidence\",str(evidence),\"--ledger\",str(ledger),\"--custody\",str(custody)]\n    rv = subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(HERE/\"plumbline_verify.py\"), *args], capture_output=True, text=True)\n    sys.stdout.write(rv.stdout); sys.stderr.write(rv.stderr)\n    kind = \"verify\"\n    for line in rv.stdout.splitlines():\n        if line.startswith(\"FAIL \"):\n            parts = line.split()\n            if len(parts) >= 2: kind = parts[1]\n            break\n    return rv.returncode, kind\n```\nBut `--files` may not exist. The verifier should handle missing dirs. For close, job may not have custody/files? The emitter's subject files under `custody/files`. It may. If missing, verifier still can run? It might need files for subject? Our verifier doesn't use --files much except job root fallback. We can pass.\n\n- In do_close terminal block:\n```\nif doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS:\n    print(\"\\ncustody:\")\n    cc = call(\"emit_custody.py\", [\"--job-dir\", str(job)] + ([\"--allow-hand-times\"] if a.allow_han