ject.\n- `terminal_state` string/null.\n- `outcome` string/null.\n- `verdict` string/null.\n- `iteration` integer/null.\n- `disposition_source` string.\n- `disposition_at_source` string.\n\nWhat about `x-plumbline` nested on `bar.approvals[]`? We'll define `xNested` as object additionalProperties true. In predicate schema, we can define `bar` etc? We don't need validate predicate deeply; predicate additionalProperties true. But \"covers nested x-plumbline objects\" means schema should have definitions. We can add `$defs/xNested`. The verifier walks and validates nested against xNested. The schema file itself can include `$defs/xNested`. Good.\n\nNeed schema file valid JSON. We'll write.\n\n### Test file content\n\nWe'll write `tests/test_plumbline_verify.py` with unittest or pytest? Base tests likely pytest. We'll use unittest (pytest discovers). Need no external deps.\n\nTest helper `run_verifier(record, custody, ...)` uses subprocess or calls V.main? Better subprocess to test CLI. But we need create temp dirs. Use `subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(VERIFIER), ...])`.\n\nFor unit functions, import V.\n\nLet's write tests:\n\n```\nimport json, os, subprocess, sys, tempfile, unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nsys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT/\"kit\"))\nimport plumbline_verify as V\n```\n\nHelper `write_job(tmp)`:\n- job = tmp/\"job\"; inputs; custody; evidence; files.\n- packet.json minimal.\n- custody.json `{}`.\n- ledger empty.\n- fake custody = tmp/\"custody\"; checker/check.py with `if __name__...` that always exits 0. But our verifier runs it with args; any args okay. Script:\n```\nimport sys\nsys.exit(0)\n```\n- pin = sha256(check.py).hexdigest()\n- tools/pin.json `{\"checker/check.py\": pin}`.\n- record path job/custody/record.custody.json.\nReturn paths.\n\nTest `test_schema_missing_block`:\n- record minimal with predicate {}.\n- run verifier. Expect return 4, stdout contains \"FAIL schema\".\nBut standard check: fake