te_job(tmp)`:\n```\njob=tmp/\"job\"; (job/\"inputs\").mkdir(parents=True); (job/\"custody\"/\"evidence\").mkdir(parents=True); (job/\"custody\"/\"files\").mkdir(parents=True)\n(job/\"packet.json\").write_text(\"{}\")\n(job/\"inputs\"/\"custody.json\").write_text(\"{}\\n\")\n(job/\"inputs\"/\"custody-events.jsonl\").write_text(\"\")\nrecord=job/\"custody\"/\"record.custody.json\"\nreturn job, record\n```\nFor schema test record:\n```\ndef minimal_record(xp):\n    return {\"_type\":..., \"subject\":[], \"predicateType\":..., \"predicate\":{\"x-plumbline\":xp}}\n```\nsubject empty allowed.\n\nTest CLI:\n```\ndef run_v(record, job, custody):\n    return subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(VERIFIER), \"--record\", str(record), \"--files\", str(job/\"custody/files\"), \"--evidence\", str(job/\"custody/evidence\"), \"--ledger\", str(job/\"inputs/custody-events.jsonl\"), \"--custody\", str(custody)], capture_output=True, text=True)\n```\nExpect returncode.\n\nFor schema missing block, record `{\"predicate\":{}}`. But root schema requires predicate.x-plumbline -> fail. Good.\n\nFor hand_times string, record with xp `{\"schema\":SCHEMA_VERSION,\"hand_times\":\"bad\"}`. Root passes, xPlumbline fails type. Good.\n\nFor unknown key, xp `{\"schema\":..., \"hand_times\":[], \"extra\":1}`. Fail.\n\nFor wrong checker, record can be anything; standard check runs first, pin mismatch -> exit 2. Need record file exists. Good.\n\nUnit tests:\n- `test_hand_time_set_reads_hand_ledger`: create job with ledger line writer hand. custody.json with bar.frozenAt. record empty. Call `V.hand_time_set(job, ledger, custody, record, evidence)`. Expect any e field bar.frozenAt source ledger-hand.\n- `test_events_missing_generation`: ledger with generation open+close. record xp generations empty. Call `V.check_events(job, ledger, record, {})`. Expect kind events. But check_events also tries generation calls with empty -> events. Good.\n- `test_spend_mismatch`: create ledger spend line, spend ledger file, key reading file. record xp spend calls_sum 1.0 while no calls. Call `V.check_spend(job, ledger, record, [])`. Expect spend.\n- `test_shadow`: record xp inputs verdict accept, xp.verdict accept. Call `V.check_shadow(job, ledger, record, [], [], {})`? Need signature. Let's design `check_shadow(job, ledger_path, record, all_calls, hand, calls_by_seat)`. Use `V.check_shadow(job, ledger, record, [], [], {})`. But derived verdict from s5_record absent -> None, so shadow not detected for verdict. We can create s5 record file and set xp.s5_record. Write `iteration-1-eval-record.json` with verdict \"reject\". Then inputs.verdict \"accept\", xp.verdict \"accept\". Derived \"reject\" -> shadow. Good.\n- `test_no_emit_import`: read source.\n\nNeed ensure unit functions are exposed.\n\n### Potential base tests\n\nModifying plumbline_job.py could break base tests if they call close without --custody. To mitigate, maybe in do_close if no custody path, skip verifier? But V8 says close runs verifier. The held-out will provide. Base tests likely don't call close. If they do, they may not set env. Let's inspect likely `tests/test_custody_ledger.py`: It might test ledger only. `validate_schemas.py` runs emitter on fixtures, not close. So okay.\n\nBut `validate_schemas.py` at base runs emitter on `job-rejected` and dirties logs. Our part doesn't fix emitter. T7 is other part. We don't touch. Base tests must pass; they may already pass despite dirty logs. Fine.\n\n### Check plumbline_job close integration with base behavior\n\nBase `do_close` writes packet facts before custody. We'll preserve. We'll move log closed. Need ensure `doc` variable used after. Let's rewrite do_close fully.\n\nPseudo:\n\n```\ndef do_close(a) -> int:\n    job = ...\n    validations...\n    ...\n    print(\"\\nclose:\")\n    code = call(\"plumbline.py\", ...)\n    packet_path = ...\n    before = ...\n    doc0 = ...\n    ... write packet facts ...\n    if code == 0:\n        doc = json.loads(...)\n        disp = doc.get(\"disposition\")\n        ...\n        if doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS:\n            print(\"\\ncustody:\")\n            cargs = [\"--job-dir\", str(job)]\n            if a.allow_hand_times: cargs.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n            cc = call(\"emit_custody.py\", cargs)\n            if cc != 0:\n                ... return cc\n            vc, kind = run_verifier(job, a)\n            if vc != 0:\n                print(f\"NOT CLOSED: Plumbline verify failed ({kind})\")\n                log_row(job, \"close_refused\", {\"kind\": kind, \"exit\": vc}, a.run_log)\n                return vc\n        log_row(job, \"closed\", {...}, a.run_log)\n        print(f\"\\njob closed: {job}\")\n    return code\n```\nBut base prints `job closed` before custody. We print after. Fine.\nIf `code != 0`, no log closed. Base also no log closed. Good.\nIf terminal state and custody success, log closed. Good.\n\nNeed `run_verifier` defined before do_close. It needs `a.custody` and `a.allow_hand_times`? Verifier reads allowance from record, no switch. So no.\n\n`run_verifier`:\n```\ndef run_verifier(job: Path, a) -> tuple[int, str]:\n    custody = (getattr(a, \"custody\", None) or os.environ.get(\"PLUMBLINE_CUSTODY\") or \"\").strip()\n    if not custody:\n        print(\"NOT CLOSED: Plumbline verify cannot run: no --custody and no $PLUMBLINE_CUSTODY.\")\n        return 2, \"cannot-run\"\n    record = job/\"custody\"/\"record.custody.json\"\n    files = job/\"custody\"/\"files\"\n    evidence = job/\"custody\"/\"evidence\"\n    ledger = job/\"inputs\"/\"custody-events.jsonl\"\n    args = [\"--record\", str(record), \"--files\", str(files), \"--evidence\", str(evidence),\n            \"--ledger\", str(ledger), \"--custody\", custody]\n    rv = subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(HERE/\"plumbline_verify.py\"), *args], capture_output=True, text=True)\n    sys.stdout.write(rv.stdout); sys.stderr.write(rv.stderr)\n    kind = \"verify\"\n    for line in rv.stdout.splitlines():\n        if line.startswith(\"FAIL \"):\n            parts = line.split()\n            if len(parts) >= 2: kind = parts[1]\n            break\n    return rv.returncode, kind\n```\nIf record missing, verifier exit 2. Good.\n\n`do_custody`:\n```\ndef do_custody(a):\n    args = [...]\n    if a.allow_hand_times: args.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n    cc = call(\"emit_custody.py\", args)\n    if cc != 0: return cc\n    vc, kind = run_verifier(Path(a.job_dir).expanduser(), a)\n    if vc != 0:\n        print(f\"NOT CLOSED: Plumbline verify failed ({kind})\")\n    return vc\n```\nBut `run_verifier` needs a.custody. `do_custody` parser add.\n\nNeed `--custody` in close and custody. `--allow-hand-times` in close and custody.\n\nPotential issue: `do_custody` may be called with `--out-dir`, so record is not at default path. The verifier expects `job/custody/record.custody.json`. If `--out-dir` given, record elsewhere. V8 says `custody` re-emit also runs verifier. We need pass record path. `run_verifier` currently fixed. We can add optional `record_path` param. In `do_custody`, if `a.out_dir`, record = Path(a.out_dir)/f\"{job.name}.custody.json\"? The emitter writes `DIR/<job_id>.custody.json`. Job id may differ from job.name. Hard to know. We can read job_id from packet. Simpler: if out_dir, don't run verifier? But V8 says custody also runs verifier. We can compute: load packet.json for job_id or job.name. Use `job_id = packet.get(\"job_id\") or job.name`. Then record = out_dir/f\"{job_id}.custody.json\". If `a.inputs` etc. Good.\nModify `run_verifier(job, a, record=None)`.\n\nIn do_close, record default.\n\n### More verifier details: Standard check pin\n\nIf `--custody` points to the subject checkout (Plumbline repo) not external, then `checker/check.py` may not exist. But V1 says checker's `checker/check.py`. The external custody repo has it. We'll use `--custody` as given. If not found, maybe look under `REPO/checker/check.py`? The subject repo might have `checker/check.py`? Not listed. We can try both `Path(custody)/checker/check.py` and `REPO/checker/check.py`. But pin should match. We'll implement candidates.\n\n### Write final files\n\nNeed be careful with output markers and no extra prose. We'll output schema, verifier, plumbline_job, test. Maybe no fixtures. Number files 4.\n\nLet's write code fully.\n\n#### Verifier code details\n\nLet's write `check_schema` with custom validator.\n\n```\ndef _type_ok(v, t):\n    if t == \"object\": return isinstance(v, dict)\n    if t == \"array\": return isinstance(v, list)\n    if t == \"string\": return isinstance(v, str)\n    if t == \"integer\": return isinstance(v, int) and not isinstance(v, bool)\n    if t == \"number\": return isinstance(v, (int,float)) and not isinstance(v,bool)\n    if t == \"boolean\": return isinstance(v, bool)\n    if t == \"null\": return v is None\n    return True\n```\n\n`_validate`:\n```\ndef _validate(inst, sch, root, path):\n    errs=[]\n    if sch is True or sch == {}: return errs\n    if sch is False: return [(path,\"false schema\")]\n    if \"$ref\" in sch:\n        ref=sch[\"$ref\"]\n        if ref.startswith(\"#/$defs/\"):\n            name=ref.split(\"/\")[-1]\n            return _validate(inst, root[\"$defs\"][name], root, path)\n        return errs\n    if \"const\" in sch and inst != sch[\"const\"]: errs.append((path,\"const\"))\n    if \"enum\" in sch and inst not in sch[\"enum\"]: errs.append((path,\"enum\"))\n    if \"type\" in sch:\n        ts = sch[\"type\"] if isinstance(sch[\"type\"], list) else [sch[\"type\"]]\n        if not any(_type_ok(inst,t) for t in ts): errs.append((path,\"type\"))\n    if \"allOf\" in sch:\n        for s in sch[\"allOf\"]: errs += _validate(inst,s,root,path)\n    if \"anyOf\" in sch:\n        if not any(not _validate(inst,s,root,path) for s in sch[\"anyOf\"]): errs.append((path,\"anyOf\"))\n    if \"oneOf\" in sch:\n        ok = sum(1 for s in sch[\"oneOf\"] if not _validate(inst,s,root,path))\n        if ok != 1: errs.append((path,\"oneOf\"))\n    if isinstance(inst, dict):\n        for r in sch.get(\"required\",[]):\n            if r not in inst: errs.append((path+\".\"+r,\"required\"))\n        props = sch.get(\"properties\") or {}\n        for k,v in inst.items():\n            if k in props:\n                errs += _validate(v, props[k], root, path+\".\"+k)\n            else:\n                ap = sch.get(\"additionalProperties\", True)\n                if ap is False:\n                    errs.append((path+\".\"+k,\"additionalProperties\"))\n                elif isinstance(ap, dict):\n                    errs += _validate(v, ap, root, path+\".\"+k)\n    if isinstance(inst, list) and \"items\" in sch:\n        for i,v in enumerate(inst):\n            errs += _validate(v, sch[\"items\"], root, f\"{path}[{i}]\")\n    if isinstance(inst, str):\n        if \"minLength\" in sch and len(inst) < sch[\"minLength\"]: errs.append((path,\"minLength\"))\n        if \"pattern\" in sch and not re.search(sch[\"pattern\"], inst): errs.append((path,\"pattern\"))\n    if isinstance(inst, (int,float)) and not isinstance(inst,bool):\n        if \"minimum\" in sch and inst < sch[\"minimum\"]: errs.append((path,\"minimum\"))\n        if \"maximum\" in sch and inst > sch[\"maximum\"]: errs.append((path,\"maximum\"))\n    return errs\n```\n\n`check_schema`:\n```\ndef check_schema(record, schema_path):\n    fails=[]\n    if not schema_path.is_file(): return [fail(\"schema\",\"schema file\",str(schema_path),0)]\n    try: schema=load(schema_path)\n    except Exception: return ...\n    for path,msg in _validate(record,schema,schema,\"$\"):\n        fails.append({\"kind\":\"schema\",\"field\":path,\"file\":str(schema_path),\"line\":0})\n    xp = ((record.get(\"predicate\") or {}).get(\"x-plumbline\"))\n    if not isinstance(xp, dict):\n        return fails + [fail(\"schema\",\"predicate.x-plumbline\",str(record),0)]\n    if xp.get(\"schema\") != SCHEMA_VERSION:\n        fails.append(fail(\"schema\",\"x-plumbline.schema\",str(record),0))\n    # validate xp again? root already did. But if root schema failed due other, still.\n    if not fails:\n        for path, val in iter_xplumbline(record):\n            if path == \"predicate.x-plumbline\": continue\n            for p,m in _validate(val, schema[\"$defs\"][\"xNested\"], schema, path):\n                fails.append(...)\n    return fails\n```\nBut if root schema fails due missing x-plumbline, we still try xp None and add. Fine.\n\n`iter_xplumbline`:\n```\ndef iter_xplumbline(obj, path=\"\"):\n    if isinstance(obj, dict):\n        for k,v in obj.items():\n            p = f\"{path}.{k}\" if path else k\n            if k == \"x-plumbline\":\n                yield p, v\n            else:\n                yield from iter_xplumbline(v,p)\n    elif isinstance(obj, list):\n        for i,v in enumerate(obj):\n            yield from iter_xplumbline(v,f\"{path}[{i}]\")\n```\nBut this will yield predicate x-plumbline and nested. We skip predicate.\n\nNeed validate xp against xPlumbline even if root schema validation already did. The root schema predicate.x-plumbline ref xPlumbline, so yes. But if root fails due other, still. We'll explicitly validate xp to catch P-V2b etc even if root fails? Root validation will catch. But if root fails due missing predicate? It may not validate xp. We can validate xp if dict.\n\n`check_schema` should return only schema failures, not other. If xp missing, return schema. If xp schema wrong, add.\n\n#### Hand time set code details\n\nNeed `line_of`:\n```\ndef line_of(path, value, key=None):\n    if value in (None,\"\") or not path.is_file(): return None\n    try: lines=path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines()\n    except OSError: return None\n    vs=str(value)\n    hits=[n for n,ln in enumerate(lines,1) if vs in ln and (key is None or f'\"{key}\"' in ln)]\n    if not hits: hits=[n for n,ln in enumerate(lines,1) if vs in ln]\n    return hits[0] if hits else None\n```\n\n`mark` closure.\n\nNeed `calls_by_seat` in hand_time_set. Use `read_calls`.\n\n`read_calls` with `ci` may be None. Good.\n\n`find_log`:\n```\ndef find_log(job, ev_dir, rel, pattern):\n    if rel:\n        for base in (ev_dir, job):\n            p=base/rel\n            if p.is_file(): return p\n        return None\n    hits=sorted(ev_dir.glob(pattern)) if pattern and ev_dir.is_dir() else []\n    return hits[0] if len(hits)==1 else None\n```\nIf pattern None, returns None.\n\n`read_calls`:\n```\ndef read_calls(job, ev_dir, ci):\n    by={}; allc=[]\n    def add(seat, log):\n        if not log: return\n        try: calls=H.read_log(log, H.Refusals(), str(seat))\n        except Exception: return\n        d=by.setdefault(str(seat),{})\n        for c in calls:\n            name=getattr(c,\"name\",None) or str(getattr(c,\"i\",\"\"))\n            d[name]=c; allc.append(c)\n    if isinstance(ci, dict):\n        m=ci.get(\"maker\") or {}\n        if m.get(\"kind\")==\"model\":\n            add(\"maker\", find_log(job, ev_dir, m.get(\"harness_log\"), \"harness-maker*.log\"))\n        for e in ci.get(\"evaluators\") or []:\n            if e.get(\"kind\")==\"model\":\n                rel=e.get(\"harness_log\") or next((x.get(\"evidence_file\") for x in ci.get(\"external\") or [] if x.get(\"subject\")==e.get(\"id\")), None)\n                add(str(e.get(\"id\")), find_log(job, ev_dir, rel, None))\n    if not allc and ev_dir.is_dir():\n        for log in sorted(ev_dir.glob(\"*.log\")):\n            add(log.stem, log)\n    return by, allc\n```\nIf ci has maker kind model but no log, allc empty, then scan all logs. Good.\n\n`call_time(c)`:\n```\nreturn getattr(c,\"started\",None) or getattr(c,\"at\",None)\n```\n\n#### Events check details\n\nNeed `ledger_array` with source. Use `ledger_rel`.\n\n`check_events` returns list of dict. Use `add`.\n\nFor generation calls, if `calls_by_seat` empty, but maybe no generation lines. Fine.\n\nEvaluation sha: `e.get(\"verdict_sha256\")` may be None. If