    src=td/\"src\"; (src/\"sub\").mkdir(parents=True)\n            (src/\"a.txt\").write_text(\"alpha\\n\")\n            (src/\"sub\"/\"b.bin\").write_bytes(b\"\\x00\\x01\")\n            os.chmod(src/\"a.txt\", 0o444)\n            os.chmod(src/\"sub\"/\"b.bin\", 0o400)\n            os.chmod(src/\"sub\", 0o555)\n            os.chmod(src, 0o555)\n            work1=td/\"work1\"; sum1=td/\"sum1.json\"\n            r=subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(FIXTURE_RUN), \"--src\", str(src), \"--work\", str(work1), \"--summary\", str(sum1)], capture_output=True, text=True)\n            self.assertEqual(r.returncode, 0, r.stderr)\n            for p in work1.rglob(\"*\"):\n                if p.is_file():\n                    self.assertTrue(os.access(p, os.W_OK), p)\n            s1=json.loads(sum1.read_text())\n            self.assertIn(\"started_at\", s1); ...\n            self.assertTrue(s1[\"writer\"].startswith(\"tools/edi/fixture_run.py@\"))\n            self.assertEqual({f[\"path\"] for f in s1[\"files\"]}, {\"a.txt\",\"sub/b.bin\"})\n            # run again\n            work2=td/\"work2\"; sum2=td/\"sum2.json\"\n            ...\n            s2=...\n            self.assertEqual(s1[\"files\"], s2[\"files\"])\n            # contents\n            self.assertEqual((work1/\"a.txt\").read_bytes(), b\"alpha\\n\")\n```\nNeed chmod source back before TemporaryDirectory cleanup? Read-only dirs can cause cleanup failure. Our test should restore modes in finally. Use `os.chmod` recursively. Or use `tempfile.TemporaryDirectory` cleanup may fail on read-only. We can use `ignore_cleanup_errors=True` (Python 3.10+). Or restore. We'll restore in finally:\n```\nfor root, dirs, files in os.walk(src):\n    for d in dirs: os.chmod(Path(root)/d, 0o755)\n    for f in files: os.chmod(Path(root)/f, 0o644)\nos.chmod(src, 0o755)\n```\nDo before leaving.\n\nAlso test mode format? We can assert `isinstance(f[\"mode\"], str)` and `int(f[\"mode\"], 8) & 0o200`. If mode \"0o644\", int(...,8) works? `int(\"0o644\", 8)` works? Yes, Python int with base 8 accepts \"0o644\"? Let's check: int(\"0o644\", 8) -> 420? Actually int(\"0o644\", 8) accepts prefix? I think int(\"0o644\", 8) returns 420. Yes. If \"644\" also. Good. We'll not assert exact.\n\n### tests/test_edi_or_call.py\n\nNeed helper to create job spend, mock, key, messages.\n\nUse tempfile. Run subprocess. Need ensure key file mode 600. For mock, can omit key file. Our or_call allows. But test P1 no key file to verify. We'll use no key file for mock.\n\nJob spend:\n```\ndef make_job(td, bound=1.0, reserve=0.0):\n    spend = td/\"spend\"/\"ledger.jsonl\"\n    job = {\"job_id\":\"test-job\",\"bound_usd\":bound,\"reserve_usd\":reserve,\"spend_ledger\":str(spend),\"prices\":[{\"model\":\"test/model\",\"tag\":\"test/fp8\",\"prompt_usd_per_token\":0.001,\"completion_usd_per_token\":0.002,\"max_completion_tokens\":100}]}\n    (td/\"job-spend.json\").write_text(json.dumps(job))\n    return job, td/\"job-spend.json\"\n```\nMessages file `[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"hi\"}]`. Worst = len(json bytes) * 0.001 + 100*0.002. len ~ 40 -> 0.04 + 0.2 = 0.24. Bound 1.0.\n\nMock:\n```\ndef write_mock(mock, usage=0.0, total_cost=0.01, provider=\"test\", status=200):\n    mock.mkdir()\n    (mock/\"key.json\").write_text(json.dumps({\"status\":200,\"headers\":{},\"body\":{\"data\":{\"usage\":usage}}}))\n    (mock/\"chat.json\").write_text(json.dumps({\"status\":200,\"headers\":{\"Date\":\"...\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"gen-1\",\"provider\":provider,\"choices\":[{\"message\":{\"content\":\"reply\"}}],\"usage\":{\"cost\":0.01}}}))\n    (mock/\"generation.json\").write_text(json.dumps({\"status\":200,\"headers\":{},\"body\":{\"data\":{\"total_cost\":total_cost,\"provider_name\":provider}}}))\n```\nNeed `key.json` body usage. Good.\n\nTest P1 and F8:\n```\ndef run_or_call(td, mock, *extra):\n    cmd = [sys.executable, str(OR_CALL), \"--seat\",\"maker\",\"--log\",str(td/\"harness-maker.log\"), \"--model\",\"test/model\",\"--tag\",\"test/fp8\",\"--pin-provider\",\"test\",\"--messages\",str(td/\"messages.json\"),\"--out\",str(td/\"reply.txt\"),\"--job-spend\",str(td/\"job-spend.json\"),\"--mock-transport\",str(mock)] + list(extra)\n    return subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)\n```\nTest:\n1. `test_mock_call_writes_job1_log_entry`:\n- make job/messages/mock usage 0 total 0.01.\n- run no init (auto-zero baseline).\n- expect return 0.\n- reply text \"reply\".\n- log = json.loads; entry keys include all base keys. Check.\n- requests: at least one POST chat and GET key and GET generation.\n- Check no Authorization in requests text.\n- Check spend ledger has reserve and settle.\n2. `test_key_at_limit_refused_before_chat`:\n- init baseline with usage 0: run `--init-spend` first. Need mock usage 0. Then rewrite mock key usage 5.0. Run call. Expect return 6. Check log not exists. Check requests.jsonl has no POST (only GET key). But init spend also recorded GET key in same requests file. We can clear requests file after init or use separate mock for call? If use same mock, requests has init GET. We can read lines and check no `POST`. Good. Also no chat POST. Check `not any(r[\"method\"]==\"POST\" for r in requests)`. Good.\n3. `test_max_cost_pre_send_refused`:\n- fresh job/mock usage 0. Run with `--max-cost 0.0001` -> return 8. No log. requests file may not exist because max-cost check before guard. Good. Check no requests file or no POST. \n4. `test_max_cost_after_settle_over_cap`:\n- job bound 1.0, mock usage 0, total_cost 0.9. Need worst ~0.24 < max_cost 0.5. total_cost 0.9 > 0.5. Run with `--max-cost 0.5`. Expect return 9. No reply file. Log exists. Spend ledger settle has over_cap true. Check.\n5. `test_missing_job_spend_exit_2`:\n- run with nonexistent job spend -> return 2.\n6. `test_spend_status_stamps_writer`:\n- init? run `--spend-status --out status.json --job-spend ... --mock-transport ...`. Need key file? mock allows. Check status has read_at, writer starts, job_id, key_usage_us