and `--verdict` + `--summary` working exactly as now.\"\n\nNow, are there other required args that need REFUSED coverage? All argparse errors are covered by the parser override.\n\nWait — also `sub.add_parser(n)` calls: I should double check that our custom class's `error` is used for subparser errors like missing `--seat`. Yes.\n\nBut hold on: `argparse.ArgumentParser.error` in the top-level: `ap.parse_args` for unknown command → error → REFUSED. Good.\n\nNow let me also double-check the L1(a) flow regarding the summary-only path and the summary stamp refusal. The bar F1d: \"With --summary, it takes started_at/finished_at and writer from the instrument's own stamp, and refuses a summary with no stamp.\" With verdict absent, summary file is the verdict file, and also must have the stamp. In my code, `p` = summary path; then in `if a.summary:` block we read it and check stamps → refuse if missing. Good.\n\nEdge: summary file is not valid JSON → refuse \"summary ... is not JSON\". But note order: p read for sha first (fine), then summary block reads JSON. If it's invalid JSON we refuse — good, and nothing appended.\n\nNow think about whether `verdict_file` should be relative path of the summary when verdict absent: yes, relpath(job, p) where p = summary file. Good.\n\nNow L2: read_log signature change.\n\nAlso, are there existing callers that pass positionally? In custody_ledger: `H.read_log(log, ref, seat)` — fine. In emit_custody presumably `H.read_log(p, ref, who)`. Fine.\n\nNow tests: add tests to tests/test_custody_ledger.py (allowed path: new tests/test_custody_ledger*.py; the existing file tests/test_custody_ledger.py is one we created in iteration 1 — it's a new file relative to base, so we can rewrite it).\n\nAdd:\n- test L1a: evaluation with only --summary (stamped) appends a line with verdict_file = summary path, verdict_sha256, summary fields; evaluation with only --summary unstamped → non-zero, REFUSED on stderr, no change.\n- test L1b: argparse error (e.g., missing --job-dir, or unknown command, or `--n` non-int) exits 2 and prints REFUSED: on stderr, ledger & custody.json byte-identical.\n- test L2: `H.read_log(log_path)` works with only the path.\n\nNeed to capture stderr: use `contextlib.redirect_stderr(io.StringIO())`. But note `refuse` uses print to sys.stderr — the module references `sys.stderr` at call time, so redirect works.\n\nFor argparse error, `print_usage(sys.stderr)` uses the module-level sys.stderr lookup at call time? `ArgumentParser.print_usage(file=None)` → `self._print_message(self.format_usage(), file)`; `_print_message(message, file=None)` → `if file is None: file = _sys.stderr` — argparse imports sys as _sys and references `_sys.stderr` at call time. And `redirect_stderr` replaces `sys.stderr` in the sys module — yes, `_sys.stderr` is the same attribute, so redirect works.\n\nAlso our `print(f\"REFUSED: {message}\", file=sys.stderr)` works.\n\nLet me write the test for argparse errors:\n\n```python\ndef test_usage_errors_print_refused_and_exit_2(self):\n    before_ev = self.events.read_bytes()\n    before_ci = (self.job / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\").read_bytes()\n    cases = [\n        [\"generation\", \"open\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job), \"--seat\", \"maker\"],  # missing --prompt -> our refuse(2)\n        [\"generation\", \"close\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job), \"--seat\", \"maker\", \"--n\", \"x\"],  # argparse\n        [\"evaluation\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job), \"--batch\", \"B\"],  # missing evaluator, no verdict/summary\n        [\"nonsense\"],\n    ]\n```\n\nHmm wait: `generation open --seat maker` without --prompt: our code returns refuse(..., 2) — already had that. But argparse: `--n` for close is type=int; `--n x` → argparse error → REFUSED. Good.\n\n`[\"nonsense\"]` → invalid choice → argparse error.\n\nFor each case, capture stderr, assert SystemExit code 2 or return value 2. Careful: argparse errors raise SystemExit; our refuse returns 2. So:\n\n```python\ndef _run_expect(self, args):\n    err = io.StringIO()\n    code = None\n    with contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):\n        try:\n            code = L.main(args)\n        except SystemExit as exc:\n            code = exc.code\n    return code, err.getvalue()\n```\n\nThen assert code == 2 and any line startswith \"REFUSED:\".\n\nAlso check that ledger and custody.json bytes unchanged after all cases.\n\nAlso for evaluation with no --verdict and no --summary: our new check returns refuse(..., 2) with REFUSED. Good — but careful: argparse previously required --verdict; now not required. So that path reaches our refusal.\n\nWait — is `--evaluator` required? yes. In case [\"evaluation\", \"--job-dir\", job, \"--batch\", \"B\"] → missing --evaluator → argparse error → REFUSED via parser. Good.\n\nNow test L1a:\n\n```python\ndef test_evaluation_summary_without_verdict(self):\n    summary = self.job / \"summary.json\"\n    summary.write_text(json.dumps({\n        \"started_at\": \"...\", \"finished_at\": \"...\", \"writer\": \"tools/edi/x@abc\"}), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    before = self.events.read_bytes()\n    self.assertEqual(self.run_cmd(\"evaluation\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job), \"--batch\", \"B1\",\n                                  \"--evaluator\", \"e1\", \"--summary\", str(summary)), 0)\n    ev = self.read_events()[-1]\n    self.assertEqual(ev[\"summary_file\"], \"summary.json\")\n    self.assertEqual(ev[\"verdict_file\"], \"summary.json\")\n    self.assertEqual(ev[\"verdict_sha256\"], sha256(summary.read_bytes()).hexdigest())\n    self.assertEqual(ev[\"summary_sha256\"], sha256(summary.read_bytes()).hexdigest())\n    self.assertEqual(ev[\"summary_writer\"], \"tools/edi/x@abc\")\n    self.assertEqual(ev[\"started_at\"], ...)\n    self.assertEqual(ev[\"finished_at\"], ...)\n    # unstamped summary refused\n    bad = self.job / \"bad.json\"\n    bad.write_text(json.dumps({\"started_at\": \"x\"}), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    before = self.events.read_bytes()\n    code, err = ...\n    assert code == 3, \"REFUSED:\" in err\n    self.assertEqual(self.events.read_bytes(), before)\n```\n\nNote summary path relative: since summary is inside the job, relpath returns \"summary.json\". Good.\n\nWait: `verdict_file` when verdict absent — p = job/summary; relpath(job, p) = \"summary.json\". Yes.\n\nAlso test that `--verdict` alone still works (already in existing test) and `--verdict`+`--summary` (existing test).\n\nTest L2:\n\n```python\ndef test_read_log_path_only(self):\n    log = self.job / \"harness.log\"\n    log.write_text(json.dumps({\n        \"call\": \"c1\",\n        \"request\": {\"url\": \"https://api.deepinfra.com/v1/chat/completions\"},\n        \"response\": {\"created\": 1767225600},\n        \"sent_at\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\n        \"received_at\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:05Z\",\n    }) + \"\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    calls = H.read_log(log)\n    self.assertEqual(len(calls), 1)\n    self.assertEqual(calls[0].name, \"c1\")\n```\n\nAlso maybe test that read_log(path) doesn't raise on an unreadable/missing log? Not necessary.\n\nNow, also need to be careful: existing test file uses `[PHONE]` placeholder?? Look at the provided file:\n\n```python\n            \"response\": {\"created\": [PHONE]},\n```\n\nThat's odd — looks like a redaction artifact from the prompt display, not real code. It appears twice in the given \"as written\" file. Hmm. That would be a syntax error... The prompt shows the file as written by an earlier part or attempt. `[PHONE]` is likely redaction of a phone-number-like timestamp? Actually it's probably redaction of a numeric epoch, e.g. 1767225600, by the harness's anonymizer? Weird. Anyway I'm returning the whole file, so I'll replace with a real number: 1767225600 (2026-01-01T00:00:00Z).\n\nWait, but if that file was committed with `[PHONE]` it'd be a syntax error and tests would fail. The iteration-1 feedback said most of this part passed, including T1, F1b, F1c, F1e, F1g. So the tests presumably ran fine, meaning the real file had a number and the prompt display redacted it. I'll use a proper epoch.\n\nAlso check: in test_hand_time_entries, `read_log` adds refusals when call has no... it has created and Date, fine.\n\nLet me now also double-check `main()` for the case where `a.cmd == \"show\"`: no change.\n\nOne more thing: the argparse override must not break `--help` (exit 0). `error()` not called. Fine.\n\nAlso: `parse_args` with `argv=None` uses sys.argv — fine.\n\nAnother possible usage error: `Path(a.job_dir).expanduser().resolve()` — no error. `json.loads` of custody.json invalid → ValueError traceback (not REFUSED). Hmm, \"every usage error of every command (argparse errors included) must still exit 2 AND print REFUSED\". Invalid custody.json isn't exactly a usage error, but to be safe we could wrap the ledger read in a try/except and refuse with code 2. Let me add that:\n\n```python\n    try:\n        ci = json.loads(ledger_p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")) if ledger_p.is_file() else {\"custody_inputs\": \"plumbline/custody-inputs/v1\"}\n    except (ValueError, OSError) as exc:\n        return refuse(f\"cannot read {ledger_p} ({exc})\", 2)\n```\n\nHmm, careful: `a.cmd == \"show\"` doesn't need ci but reading happens before show. If custody.json is invalid and show is run... base behavior? In iteration-1, show would crash with traceback too. Adding refuse is fine and safer.\n\nActually wait: reading custody.json happens for show too; if it's corrupt, show would refuse — that's acceptable.\n\nAlso `--job-dir` pointing to nonexistent dir: for e.g. freeze, `(job/rel).is_file()` false → refuse(2). Fine.\n\nNow also, one more subtlety in L1(b): \"changes neither the ledger nor custody.json (byte-identical)\". With argparse errors nothing is read/written. With our `refuse()` paths, writes happen only at the end (`write_custody` + `append_event`) after all checks. Good.\n\nBut careful: `generation` branch — no writes. `pin` — `ci.setdefault(\"routing\", {})[a.seat] = pin` happens before append; but pin refusals occur before that assignment. Check: pin refusals (host invalid, via invalid, log has calls) all before `ci.setdefault`. Good. `approve`: refusals before mutation. `dispose`: mutations after checks. `freeze`: checks before mutation. Good.\n\nNow write the full files.\n\ncustody_history.py: only change read_log signature and add `if ref is None: ref = Refusals()`.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```python\ndef read_log(path: Path, ref: Refusals | None = None, who: str = \"log\") -> list[Call]:\n    \"\"\"A harness log: one JSON object per call, a JSON list of them, or JSON Lines.\n\n    `ref` and `who` are optional so a reader that only wants the calls can call `read_log(path)` (P1):\n    refusals then go to a throwaway Refusals().\"\"\"\n    if ref is None:\n        ref = Refusals()\n```\n\nRest unchanged.\n\nNow custody_ledger.py full file with changes:\n\n1. Parser subclass.\n2. evaluation --verdict optional + fallback.\n3. ledger read try/except.\n\nLet me write the parser class:\n\n```python\nclass RefusingParser(argparse.ArgumentParser):\n    \"\"\"argparse's usage errors exit 2 with a `REFUSED:` line, as every other refusal does (F1a, INTERFACE 1).\"\"\"\n\n    def error(self, message):\n        self.print_usage(sys.stderr)\n        print(f\"REFUSED: {message}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        raise SystemExit(2)\n```\n\nAnd use `ap = RefusingParser(...)`. Subparsers inherit via parser_class default = type(self) → RefusingParser. Let me double check CPython: In argparse.py, `ArgumentParser.add_subparsers`:\n\n```python\n    def add_subparsers(self, **kwargs):\n        ...\n        # create a new action\n        if kwargs.get('parser_class') is None:\n            kwargs['parser_class'] = type(self)\n```\n\nHmm, actually I recall:\n\n```python\n        # create a new action and parse the arguments\n        action = parsers_class(**kwargs)\n```\n\nwhere earlier:\n\n```python\n        parsers_class = self._pop_action_class(kwargs, 'parsers')\n```\n\nHmm. Let me check actual code (Python 3.11):\n\n```python\n    def add_subparsers(self, **kwargs):\n        if self._subparsers is not None:\n            self.error(_('cannot have multiple subparser arguments'))\n\n        # add the parser class to the arguments if it's not present\n        kwargs.setdefault('parser_class', type(self))\n        ...\n```\n\nYes! `kwargs.setdefault('parser_class', type(self))`. So subparsers use RefusingParser. \n\nAlso `print_usage(sys.stderr)` — fine. Could use `self.print_usage(file=sys.stderr)`; positional works.\n\nAlso add `exit`? No, default exit raises SystemExit(status). We raise SystemExit(2) directly.\n\nNow the evaluation arg:\n\n```python\n    ev.add_argument(\"--verdict\", help=\"the verdict file; may be omitted when --summary is given (the summary is then also the verdict)\")\n```\n\nAnd branch code:\n\n```python\n    elif a.cmd == \"evaluation\":\n        target = a.verdict or a.summary\n        if not target:\n            return refuse(\"evaluation requires --verdict FILE (or --summary FILE to serve as the verdict)\", 2)\n        p = Path(target)\n        if not p.is_absolute():\n            p = job / p\n        if not p.is_file():\n            return refuse(f\"no {'verdict' if a.verdict else 'summary'} file at {p}\", 2)\n        vsha = sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n        ev = {\"event\": \"evaluation\", \"batch\": a.batch, \"evaluator\": a.evaluator,\n              \"verdict_file\": relpath(job, p), \"verdict_sha256\": vsha, \"at\": t}\n        if a.iteration is not None:\n            ev[\"iteration\"] = a.iteration\n        if a.summary:\n            sp = Path(a.summary)\n            ...\n```\n\nWait, if a.verdict given and missing, message \"no verdict file at\". If only summary given and missing → \"no summary file at\". If both given and verdict missing → \"no verdict file at\" (verdict takes precedence). Fine.\n\nAlso `refuse(..., 2)` for a missing file — base used 2, keep.\n\nNow the ledger read:\n\n```python\n    ledger_p, events_p = job / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\", job / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\"\n    try:\n        ci = (json.loads(ledger_p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")) if ledger_p.is_file()\n              else {\"custody_inputs\": \"plumbline/custody-inputs/v1\"})\n    except (ValueError, OSError) as exc:\n        return refuse(f\"cannot read {ledger_p} ({exc})\", 2)\n    if a.cmd == \"show\":\n```\n\nHmm, but for `show` with corrupt custody.json, refusing is new behavior; acceptable.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety: `show` shouldn't require valid custody.json maybe. Base (iteration-1) code crashed on corrupt json too (json.loads raises). So refuse is strictly better.\n\nAlso, should we validate `ci` is a dict? If it's a list, `ci.setdefault` fails with AttributeError → traceback. Add:\n\n```python\n    if not isinstance(ci, dict):\n        return refuse(f\"{ledger_p} does not hold a JSON object\", 2)\n```\n\nGood.\n\nAlso `events_p` unused variable... it's used in show branch. Yes.\n\nNow, another thought about L1(b): \"every usage error of every command (argparse errors included) must still exit 2\". Our refuse() default code is 3 for refusals; usage errors pass 2. Existing usage errors: check all `refuse(...)` calls with 2:\n- freeze: no bar file (2), already frozen (2)\n- approve: not frozen (2)\n- pin: bad host (2), bad via (2)\n- dispose: bad decided-at (2), missing decided-source (2)\n- generation open: missing --prompt (2), missing prompt file (2)\n- generation close: missing --n (2)\n- evaluation: missing verdict (2), missing file (2), bad JSON (2), missing read_at stamp? no that's 3.\n- amendment: missing file (2), bad said-at (2)\n- relay: bad source-at (2)\n- spend: missing ledger (2), missing reading (2), bad JSON (2), bad read_at format (2)\n- unknown command (2)\n\nAll good.\n\nNow tests file. Rewrite tests/test_custody_ledger.py with additions. Keep existing tests. Fix `[PHONE]`.\n\nLet me use `created: 1767225600` (2026-01-01T00:00:00Z epoch). 1767225600 = 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z? Let's check: 2025-01-01T00:00:00Z = 1735689600. Plus 365 days = 31536000 → 1767225600 = 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z. Yes (2025 not a leap year).\n\nNow, also add a test that evaluation with --summary only and no stamp is refused with REFUSED on stderr and exit 3. And byte-identical events.\n\nHelper for stderr capture:\n\n```python\nimport contextlib, io\n\ndef run_capture(self, *args):\n    err = io.StringIO()\n    try:\n        with contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):\n            code = L.main(list(args))\n    except SystemExit as exc:\n        code = exc.code if isinstance(exc.code, int) else 2\n    return code, err.getvalue()\n```\n\nCareful: `contextlib.redirect_stderr` must encompass the SystemExit raising. SystemExit propagates through the with-block, fine (context restored).\n\nAlso stdout: refuse prints nothing to stdout. argparse error prints usage to stderr. Fine.\n\nTest cases for usage errors:\n\n```python\n    def test_usage_errors_refuse_with_refused_line(self):\n        before_ev = self.events.read_bytes()\n        before_ci = (self.job / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\").read_bytes()\n        cases = [\n            [\"nonsense\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job)],\n            [\"generation\", \"close\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job), \"--seat\", \"maker\", \"--n\", \"x\"],\n            [\"evaluation\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job), \"--batch\", \"B1\"],\n            [\"evaluation\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job), \"--batch\", \"B1\", \"--evaluator\", \"e1\"],\n            [\"freeze\"],\n        ]\n        for args in cases:\n            code, err = self.run_capture(*args)\n            self.assertEqual(code, 2, (args, err))\n            self.assertTrue(any(ln.startswith(\"REFUSED:\") for ln in err.splitlines()), (args, err))\n            self.assertEqual(self.events.read_bytes(), before_ev)\n            self.assertEqual((self.job / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\").read_bytes(), before_ci)\n```\n\nCheck `[\"nonsense\", \"--job-dir\", ...]` — argparse invalid choice error → SystemExit(2). `[\"freeze\"]` missing --job-dir → error. `[\"evaluation\", ... \"--batch\", \"B1\"]` missing --evaluator → argparse error. `[\"evaluation\" ... --batch B1 --evaluator e1]` no verdict/summary → our refuse(2) with REFUSED.\n\nHmm — one more: `--job-dir` missing for `nonsense`: argparse reports invalid choice before missing job-dir? It collects all errors? argparse errors on the first problem encountered; either way exit 2 with REFUSED.\n\nNow L1a test:\n\n```python\n    def test_evaluation_summary_without_verdict(self):\n        summary = self.job / \"summary.json\"\n        summary.write_text(json.dumps({\n            \"started_at\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\n            \"finished_at\": \"2026-01-01T00:01:00Z\",\n            \"writer\": \"tools/edi/x@abc\",\n        }), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        before = self.events.read_bytes()\n        self.assertEqual(self.run_cmd(\"evaluation\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job), \"--batch\", \"B1\",\n                                      \"--evaluator\", \"e1\", \"--summary\", str(summary)), 0)\n        after = self.events.read_bytes()\n        self.assertTrue(after.startswith(before))\n        ev = self.read_events()[-1]\n        sha = sha256(summary.read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n        self.assertEqual(ev[\"verdict_file\"], \"summary.json\")\n        self.assertEqual(ev[\"verdict_sha256\"], sha)\n        self.assertEqual(ev[\"summary_file\"], \"summary.json\")\n        self.assertEqual(ev[\"summary_sha256\"], sha)\n        self.assertEqual(ev[\"summary_writer\"], \"tools/edi/x@abc\")\n        self.assertEqual(ev[\"started_at\"], \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\")\n        self.assertEqual(ev[\"finished_at\"], \"2026-01-01T00:01:00Z\")\n        # a summary with no stamp is refused with a REFUSED: line, nothing appended\n        bad = self.job / \"bad.json\"\n        bad.write_text(json.dumps({\"started_at\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\"}), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        before = self.events.read_bytes()\n        code, err = self.run_capture(\"evaluation\", \"--job-dir\", str(self.job), \"--batch\", \"B2\",\n                                     \"--evaluator\", \"e1\", \"--summary\", str(bad))\n        self.assertEqual(code, 3, err)\n        self.assertTrue(any(ln.startswith(\"REFUSED:\") for ln in err.splitlines()), err)\n        self.assertEqual(self.events.read_bytes(), before)\n```\n\nAnd L2 test:\n\n```python\n    def test_read_log_accepts_path_alone(self):\n        log = self.job / \"harness.log\"\n        log.write_text(json.dumps({\n            \"call\": \"c1\",\n            \"request\": {\"url\": \"https://api.deepinfra.com/v1/openai/chat/completions\"},\n            \"sent_at\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\n            \"received_at\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:05Z\",\n            \"response\": {\"created\": 1767225600},\n        }) + \"\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        calls = H.read_log(log)\n        self.assertEqual(len(calls), 1)\n        self.assertEqual(calls[0].name, \"c1\")\n        self.assertEqual(H.iso(calls[0].received), \"2026-01-01T00:00:05Z\")\n```\n\nNote read_log with no ref: internal refusals dropped. If the call lacks created/date it would add refusal to throwaway ref — fine.\n\nAlso test that read_log(path) with an unreadable file doesn't raise: \n\n```python\n        self.assertEqual(H.read_log(self.job / \"missing.log\"), [])\n```\n\nHmm: path.read_text raises OSError → ref.add → return []. With ref None → fresh. Returns []. Good.\n\nNow, also keep `test_writer_stamp_on_every_line` etc.\n\nOne more consideration: `run_capture` catches SystemExit; but for successful runs code is 0.\n\nNow, check the existing tests still pass with modifications:\n\n- test_generation_open_close: unaffected.\n- test_evaluation_sha_and_summary: verdict-only path unchanged; verdict+summary unchanged; summary-without-stamp with verdict given → still refused (the summary block checks). Yes: a.verdict given, p = verdic