ime with the fixture's old log times.\n\nUnless... the ledger freeze line's `at` is the tool clock. The emitter uses `frozen_at = b.get(\"frozenAt\") or (fz or {}).get(\"at\")`. So frozenAt = now. And startedAt from the log = 2026-10-20T12:00. now >= 2026-10-20... wait, what is `now`? If the test runs \"now\" in real time (say 2025), then now < 2026-10-20 → the existing fixture would already fail \"after-emit\" for every event. Since iteration 1 passed T7/G2 (base tests pass), the clock must be >= 2026-10-28 or the fixture times are in the past relative to the test clock. Let's assume the test clock is after 2026-10-28.\n\nSo now > 2026-10-20. frozenAt = now > startedAt = 2026-10-20T12:00. Refusal.\n\nHmm. So a tool-built job at test time has a freeze time after the log times. That breaks ordering.\n\nUnless the T4 job's logs are written at test time with test-time timestamps! That's what `or_call.py` would do (mock transport writes `at` = real clock). So the T4 test should synthesize harness logs with times around now.\n\nHmm, this is getting heavy. Let me reconsider.\n\nAlternative: shift the whole job's times. Build the tool job with logs whose times are set relative to now: e.g., freeze at now-1h, calls at now-50min, etc. But the ledger freeze stamps `at` = now (tool clock), not now-1h. So frozenAt = now. Hmm, so the log times must be after now. But then they'd be \"after-emit\" (after the emission now)... but emission happens after the calls in reality. The emitter's `now` is the emission time, which is after the logs. So logs at now-50min are before emission. And freeze at now (the tool clock at freeze time) must be before startedAt... but startedAt is now-50min < freeze now. Refusal.\n\nHmm, the real flow: freeze (t0), then calls (t1 > t0), then dispose (t2), then emit (t3 > t2). So for a test, I'd need to run freeze, sleep, run calls, ... That's slow but could use small sleeps (1s). Hmm.\n\nWait — actually, maybe I can set the log times to be after the freeze. Let me plan:\n\n1. t_freeze = now (real clock).\n2. Build logs with sent_at/received_at/created = t_freeze + 60s, +120s, etc.\n3. dispose --decided-at = t_freeze + 300s? But the ledger refuses decided_at after the tool's clock. So decided_at must be <= now at dispose time. If we dispose right after freeze, now = t_freeze + epsilon, and decided_at = t_freeze + 300 > now → refused.\n\nHmm. So we'd need to sleep 300s. Too slow.\n\nUnless... the ledger's `dispose` bound: `if dt > H.parse_ts(t): refuse`. So decided_at <= tool clock at dispose. And `last_check_time` from evaluator received_at in logs. So the sequence must be: logs' received_at <= decided_at <= now(dispose).\n\nSo real-time ordering: freeze(t0) < logs(t1) < dispose(t2) < emit(t3). With real clocks, t0 < t1 < t2 requires sleeps or fudging.\n\nHmm, wait. Does the freeze need to be before the logs? check_order: `before(\"bar.frozenAt\", F, \"startedAt\", S, strict=True)` → F must be strictly before S. So yes.\n\nHmm, but wait: `F = P(bar.get(\"frozenAt\"))`. If the freeze line has `at` = now and the logs are in the past (2026-10-20), F > S → refusal. Unless the logs are in the future relative to freeze.\n\nOK here's an idea: instead of using real now for the ledger freeze, the ledger commands stamp the tool clock — we can't control it. But we CAN control the log times (synthesize them). And the dispose decided_at must be <= now.\n\nWhat if we:\n1. Freeze at now (t0).\n2. Synthesize logs at t0 + 10s, t0 + 20s (in the near future).\n3. Dispose --decided-at t0 + 10s (>= last check received_at t0+20? No, must be >= last check).\n\nHmm, decided_at >= last check (t0+20) and <= now (dispose time ~t0+epsilon). Contradiction unless we sleep.\n\nSo: freeze, sleep 1s, ... Actually, let's do:\n- freeze at T (real now).\n- Build logs with received_at = T + 1s, T + 2s (future by 1-2s).\n- sleep 3s.\n- dispose --decided-at = T + 2s (<= now after sleep, >= last check).\n- emit.\n\nSleeping 3s in a test suite is acceptable