oducedAt = maker received_at. Evaluator checks' times = evaluator received_at. So the maker log times must be before the evaluator log times. If I stamp maker log at base, and evaluator log at base + 10s, good.\n\nAlso `before(\"startedAt\", S, \"producedAt\", Pd)` — S < Pd. Maker: first sent < last received. Good.\n\nAnd `before(\"the last check\", last, \"disposition.at\", D)` — evaluator received_at <= decided_at.\n\nLet me plan times (relative to freeze_at = F):\n- maker log calls: sent_at = F+1, received_at = F+2 (single call). If multiple calls, step.\n- evaluator log calls: sent_at = F+3, received_at = F+4 for call 1; F+5/F+6 for call 2; F+7/F+8 for call 3. Hmm, but checks use received_at.\n\nHmm, wait: but the fixture's `passes` may reference specific calls. The check `at` = call.at = received_at. Multiple checks may share a call. Fine.\n\n- decided_at = F+10. Sleep until F+10.5? That's 10s. Hmm, too long.\n\nLet me compress: maker sent=F+1, received=F+2; eval call 1 sent=F+2, received=F+2? Hmm, step must be >0 for ordering within a log? Not necessarily. Let me just use sub-second? No, RFC allows fractional but the ledger uses whole seconds. Hmm, actually the emitter parses RFC with optional fractional. But `H.iso` truncates to seconds.\n\nSimplest: \n- maker: sent_at = F+1, received_at = F+1 (same second). startedAt=F+1, producedAt=F+1. Order: startedAt <= producedAt ok.\n- eval calls: sent_at = F+2, received_at = F+2.\n- decided_at = F+3. Sleep until F+4 (3-4s).\n- freeze at F.\n\nHmm, wait: `before(\"bar.frozenAt\", F, \"startedAt\", S, strict=True)` — F < S → F < F+1 ok.\n\nSleep 4s. Acceptable? Meh. Let me think if I can avoid the sleep.\n\nThe dispose bound: decided_at >= last check (evaluator received_at = F+2) and <= tool clock at dispose. If dispose runs at ~F (real), then tool clock ~F, and decided_at=F+3 > F → refused.\n\nWhat if I don't use the real ledger `dispose` but write the dispose line directly? The test's goal is the emitter, and E1 is ab