ond), decided_at = E+1. Hmm, if real = E+1.5, the tool clock string = E+1 (truncated). Then at == decided_at. Ugh, truncation.\n\nSleep until real >= E+2.5, dispose with decided_at = E+1. Then line at = E+2, decided_at = E+1. Difference! E1 exercised. And decided_at (E+1) >= last check (evaluation at = E, evaluator received_at = F+2 <= E). Good. And <= tool clock (E+2). Good.\n\nSo sleep ~2.5s after evaluation. OK, 2.5s. Acceptable.\n\nHmm, wait: actually the evaluation line's `at` = E. And decided_at = E+1 >= E. Good.\n\nBut also, the evaluator received_at must be <= decided_at. If I stamp the eval log with base = F (freeze time), received_at = F+3 (3 calls, step 2: i=0 → F+0/F+1, i=1 → F+2/F+3, i=2 → F+4/F+5). Hmm, F+5 could be > decided_at = E+1 if E ≈ F (evaluation runs right after freeze). E is the real time when `evaluation` runs, ~F + 1-2s (after several subprocess calls). And the logs are stamped F+0..F+5. So last check could be F+5 > E+1 → dispose refused!\n\nUgh. Let me stamp the logs relative to F but with small steps, or relative to a time before E.\n\nHmm, this timing dance is fragile. Let me simplify: stamp the logs with base = F (freeze time) and step=1:\n- maker log (1 call): sent F+0, received F+1.\n- eval log (3 calls): sent F+1, received F+1? Hmm, step=1: i=0 → F+0/F+1; i=1 → F+1/F+2; i=2 → F+2/F+3.\n\nSo max received_at = F+3. E (evaluation run) is real time ≈ F + 0.5-1.5s. Hmm, F is truncated to the second. The commands freeze→approve×2→pin→generation×2→evaluation take maybe 1.5s. So E ≈ F + 2. Then decided_at must be >= max(E=F+2, F+3) = F+3. And tool clock at dispose (after sleep) must be >= F+3. Sleep until real >= F+4, dispose decided_at = F+3. OK.\n\nHmm, but F+3 vs real: real F is the truncated second, actual freeze happened at F+0.x. Commands take ~1.5s → E ≈ F+1.5-2.5. Let me not rely on tight timing. Let me instead:\n\n1. Run all the ledger commands EXCEPT dispose.\n2. Read the last event's `at` (the evaluation line's at) = E_line.\n3. Read the max received_at from the stamped logs (I control it: max_recv).\n4. last_check = max(E_line, max_recv, ...).\n5. Wait until real >= last_check + 2.5s.\n6. dispose --decided-at = last_check + 1s.\n\nThat's robust. The sleep could be up to a few seconds. Let me compute: if I stamp the logs with base = F and max_recv = F+3, and E_line ≈ F+2, last_check = F+3, wait until F+5.5. So ~4-5s of sleeping. Hmm.\n\nLet me reduce: stamp the logs with base = F and step=1 but only 3 calls: max_recv = F+2 (i=0: F+0/F+1, i=1: F+1/F+2, i=2: F+2/F+3). Hmm 3 calls → i=2 → F+2/F+3. So max_recv = F+3.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, stamp the logs in the PAST relative to F: base = F - 100s. Then max_recv = F-97 < E_line. But then check_order: `before(\"bar.frozenAt\", F, \"startedAt\", S, strict=True)` — F > S → refusal \"order\"! Damn.\n\nHmm. So logs must be after the freeze.\n\nWhat if I stamp the logs at F+1 (single step 0, all calls same second F+1)? Then max_recv = F+1. E_line ≈ F+2 (evaluation at). last_check = max(F+2, F+1) = F+2. Wait until F+4.5, dispose decided_at = F+3. Sleep ≈ 2s.\n\nBut: all eval calls at F+1/F+1 → all checks at F+1. Ordering: producedAt (maker received F+1) <= checks (F+1)? `before(\"producedAt\", Pd, n, t)`: `a > b` → F+1 > F+1 false → ok (non-strict). Good. And startedAt (maker sent F+1) < producedAt (F+1)? `before` non-strict: `S > Pd`? no. OK.\n\nHmm, but `check_order` also has `before(\"startedAt\", S, \"producedAt\", Pd)` non-strict. Fine.\n\nSo: stamp maker log: sent_at = received_at = F+1. Stamp eval log: all calls sent_at = received_at = F+1.\n\nHmm, but `read_log` checks `c.created and c.date` consistency — unchanged, fine.\n\nBut wait: the emitter's `startedAt` = first maker call sent_at = F+1, `producedAt` = last maker received_at = F+1. checks at F+1. Good.\n\nAnd `disposition.at` = decided_at = F+3 (or whatever). `before(\"the last check\", last=F+1, \"disposition.at\", D=F+3)` ok.\n\nAnd `after-emit`: all t <= now + tol. now = emission time ≈ F+4. F+3 < F+4. ok.\n\nNow, timing: after the ledger commands (through evaluation), real time ≈ F+1.5-2.5. Wait until real >= F+3.5 (so the tool clock at dispose is F+3 or F+4). dispose with decided_at = F+2? Hmm, must be >= last_check = max(E_line, F+1). E_line = evaluation line at = real time at evaluation ≈ F+1 or F+2. So decided_at must be >= E_line. Let me just compute decided_at from the actual event log after all commands:\n\n```python\n    evs = [json.loads(x) for x in (j / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\").read_text().splitlines()]\n    last = max(_parse(e[\"at\"]) for e in evs if e.get(\"at\"))\n    last = max(last, fz + timedelta(seconds=2))  # the stamped logs' received_at\n    decided = last + timedelta(seconds=1)\n    while time.time() < decided.timestamp() + 0.8:\n        time.sleep(0.1)\n    run(\"dispose\", \"--by\", disp_by, \"--state\", disp_state, \"--decided-at\", _iso(decided), \"--decided-source\", \"chat\")\n```\n\nHmm, but the ledger's last_check also includes the evaluator received_at from the logs (F+1). And the evaluation line at. So `last` computed from events + fz+2 covers it. Actually, `fz + 2` where fz is the freeze at; the logs are stamped fz+1. So last >= fz+2 > fz+1. Good.\n\nWait, but the tool clock at dispose must be >= decided. The loop waits until real >= decided + 0.8. Then the tool clock (truncated second) >= decided. Good.\n\nHmm, `_iso(decided)` truncates to seconds. And the ledger compares `dt > H.parse_ts(t)` where t = tool clock (truncated). If decided = last+1 and real >= decided+0.8, then tool clock second >= decided. Good.\n\nSleep duration: decided ≈ max(evaluation at ≈ F+1.5, fz+2 = F+2) + 1 = F+3. Real after commands ≈ F+1.5. Wait until F+3.8 → ~2.3s sleep. OK.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: `last = max(_parse(e[\"at\"]) for e in evs if e.get(\"at\"))` — evs includes generation lines at F+1, evaluation at F+1.5 (truncated to F+1 or F+2). Let me just compute. Fine.\n\nAlso `disp_by` and `disp_state`: read from the fixture's custody.json before popping disposition:\n\n```python\n    disp = cj.get(\"disposition\") or {}\n    disp_by, disp_state = disp.get(\"by\"), disp.get(\"state\")\n```\n\n`--state given|declined`. If disp_state is None, omit. If it's something else... The fixture emits with disposition.state from the packet, not custody.json. Hmm, the `dispose` command sets `disp[\"state\"] = a.state` if given. The emitter's disposition.state = packet's disposition_state (not custody.json). So state doesn't matter for the emitter. But to be safe, pass `--state` only if it's in (\"given\",\"declined\").\n\nHmm, `dispose --by` is required. `disp_by` must exist (else the fixture emits with a gap). If None, use a placeholder? Let me use `disp.get(\"by\") or \"Fixture Owner\"`.\n\nHmm, wait: `dispose` writes `disp = {\"by\": ..., \"at\": decided_at, ...}`. It does NOT check `by` against anything. So any name works. But the emitter compares `live.get(\"by\") != dsp.get(\"by\")` — both from the same source, so consistent.\n\nOK.\n\nNow, `run(\"evaluation\", ...)` — the verdict file. I'll use the bar file \"custody/bar.md\". The ledger computes its sha. Fine.\n\nAlso `generation open --prompt custody/bar.md`. Fine.\n\nHmm, `pin` requires the maker log to be absent. I delete it before pin and restore after. But `seat_logs` also checks `ci[\"maker\"][\"harness_log\"]`. If the fixture's maker.harness_log is set (e.g. \"harness-maker-fixture.log\"), deleting the file makes `(ev/named).is_file()` false. Good.\n\nHmm, wait: is the maker log path exactly `custody/evidence/harness-maker-fixture.log`? From the test, yes.\n\nBut there might be additional harness logs (e.g. `harness-eval-a.log`). `seat_logs` for seat \"maker\" globs `harness-maker*.log`. OK.\n\nNow — after all this, run the emitter with no switch:\n\n```python\no = TMP / \"tool-built.json\"\nrc, out = emit(j, o, allow_hand=False)\nrt = json.loads(o.read_text())[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"] if o.is_file() else {}\nres