ition.at = 12:30 and the line decided_at = 12:31 → mismatch → refusal. But also the record's disposition.at would be... never written. Fine.\n\nAlso, `by` must match to isolate the time mismatch. I set the line's by = cj's by. Good.\n\nHmm, but there's another subtlety: the line has no `writer` → writer_source = \"ledger-no-writer\" → `mark(\"disposition.at\", ...)`. That's a hand-time mark too! With allow_hand=True, it's listed. But the refusal for the mismatch still fires (ref.add(\"time\")). So rc=3. Good. Actually, hmm — with a no-writer line, the disposition.at is also a hand time, but with allow_hand, no hand-time refusal. And the time mismatch refusal fires. Good.\n\nActually, let me add `\"writer\": \"kit/custody_ledger.py@abc123def456\"` to avoid the no-writer hand mark. I did. Good.\n\nNow let me also think: does adding these tests risk breaking on the actual fixture contents? The risk is in `tool_built_job` (heavy). Let me make it defensive with try/except and report failure (not crash).\n\nActually, if `tool_built_job` raises, the whole suite crashes (exception propagates). I wrapped it in try/except and `res(...)` with False. But `res` takes (name, ok, why). Let me make sure the except is structured.\n\nHmm, but if it fails, the test FAILS — which is correct behavior for a broken fix, but if my assumptions about the fixture are wrong, the whole part fails. Risky but necessary.\n\nLet me reduce risk: instead of requiring the exact maker log name, glob for `harness-maker*.log`. And for roles, handle empty.\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe I don't need to delete the maker log for pin if there are no calls in it... but there is a call. OK.\n\nHmm, one more risk: `run(\"approve\", ...)` — the fixture's approvals might include `kind: \"agent\"` or `adopted`. If a fixture approval is an agent with adopted, then `approve --by X` (default person) would be refused by `check_approval` (looks_agent or seat mismatch). Hmm. The default fixture (job-rejected) should be clean persons. The \"agent-adopted\" test is a copy with modifications. So the base fixture has persons. OK.\n\nBut to be safe, pass `--kind` if the fixture has it:\n\n```python\n    roles = [(a.get(\"role\"), a.get(\"by\"), a.get(\"kind\")) for a in ...]\n    ...\n    for role, by, kind in roles:\n        extra = [\"--kind\", kind] if kind in (\"person\", \"agent\") else []\n        run(\"approve\", \"--role\", role, \"--by\", by, *extra)\n```\n\nHmm, but an agent approval also needs `--adopted-document/--adopted-version`. Let me include if present. Getting complex. Let me just assume persons and pass `--kind person` explicitly? If the fixture's approval kind is \"person\", same. Let me pass nothing (default person). Fine.\n\nHmm, wait: there's a subtle issue with `approve`: it checks `H.check_approval(appr, 0, ci, packet, ref)` where `ci` is the current custody.json. At that point, `bar.approvals` has been popped, and `ci[\"orchestrator\"]` is present. Good.\n\nNow, `freeze` requires `bar.file` in ci. The fixture's bar.file = \"custody/bar.md\". Good.\n\nLet me now also handle the `evaluation` command's `--evaluator`: read from `cj[\"evaluators\"][0][\"id\"]`. But wait — `evaluator_history` is called for model evaluators and needs the harness log. The eval log is `harness-eval-a.log`. The evaluator id \"eval-a\" maps to that log via `evaluators[].harness_log` or external. Fine.\n\nOK. Now let me write everything out.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider one thing about `tool_built_job`: after popping `disposition`, `routing`, `startedAt`, `producedAt`, and `bar.frozenAt`/`approvals`, the `freeze` command writes `ci[\"bar\"][\"frozenAt\"]` and `ci[\"orchestrator\"]`? No, orchestrator is only set if `--orchestrator` passed. The fixture's `orchestrator` stays (I don't pop it). Good, approve needs it.\n\nWait, does `freeze` preserve `orchestrator`? Yes, it reads ci, modifies bar, writes back.\n\nHmm, but I popped `bar.frozenAt` and `bar.approvals` from the in-mem