```\n\nWait, but careful: if `call is not None` and `f.get(\"at\")` is typed, we mark it as hand time. But then later code sets `at_v, xc` from the call. Good.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety about which line the `at` is on. `line_in(inputs_path, f.get(\"at\"), \"at\")` finds the line with the value and `\"at\"` key. Fine.\n\nActually, wait. There's a deeper subtlety: E2 says \"a pass or control that DOES carry a typed `at` is a hand time even when a harness call is linked to it.\" So we mark it. But what about the case where `f.get(\"at\")` matches the call's time exactly? Still a hand time — it's typed. OK.\n\nHmm, but that would make the fixture `job-rejected` have hand times for passes. Let me check: the test says:\n\n```python\nrc, out = emit(FIX, rec_path)   # allow_hand=True\nres((f\"{S} · fixture job emits with no gap (exit 0)\", rc == 0, ...))\n```\n\nSo fixture emits exit 0 with allow_hand. But does it have gaps? \"no gap\" — rc == 0 means no hard gaps. Hmm, but wait: with E2, does marking a hand time create a gap? No, hand times are refusals (exit 3) without the switch, and with the switch they're listed. No gaps. Good.\n\nBut hold on — the T3 test:\n\n```python\no = TMP / \"t3-allow.json\"\nrc, out = emit(FIX, o, allow_hand=True)\nrx = json.loads(o.read_text())[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"] if o.is_file() else {}\nht = rx.get(\"hand_times\") or []\nby_field = {e.get(\"field\"): e for e in ht}\nres((f\"{S} · T3 with --allow-hand-times the record is written and hand_times lists the hand fields with file:line\",\n     rc == 0 and \"disposition.at\" in by_field and by_field[\"disposition.at\"].get(\"line\") is not None\n     and len(ht) >= 1 and all(e.get(\"file\") and e.get(\"source\") for e in ht), ...))\n```\n\nOK.\n\nNow E3: every call of every harness log read (Maker and each evaluator) must be checked for both `.sent_at` and `.received_at`. Currently:\n\nIn `maker_history`, the emitter gets `_calls` and in `build`:\n```python\n    if mcalls:\n        us = [c for c in mcalls if c.started is not None]\n        ua = [c for c in mcalls if c.at is not None]\n        if us:\n            e = call_hand(min(us, key=lambda c: c.started), \"sent_at\")\n            if e:\n                hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n        if ua:\n            e = call_hand(max(ua, key=lambda c: c.at), \"received_at\")\n            if e:\n                hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n```\n\nChange: for every call in mcalls, add all `hand_time_entries()`. Similarly for evaluator calls.\n\nLet me write a helper:\n\n```python\ndef flag_call_hands(calls, hand: dict) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Every call of a harness log: each .sent_at / .received_at with no logger value is a hand time (T7b).\"\"\"\n    for c in calls:\n        for e in c.hand_time_entries():\n            hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n```\n\nAnd in build, replace the maker block:\n\n```python\n    if mcalls:\n        flag_call_hands(mcalls, hand)\n```\n\nAnd for evaluators, in the checks/controls loop, the linked call's hand entries are added. But E3 says every call in the log counts, not just linked ones. Hmm. Let's re-read:\n\n\"Change: for every call of every harness log read (Maker and each evaluator), flag each of `.sent_at` and `.received_at` that has no logger value, with the source and file:line of the server time used\"\n\nSo for each evaluator's log, every call must be flagged. In `evaluator_history`, the calls are returned as `{\"calls\": named, ...}` where `named` maps name → Call. Some calls may not be linked to any check/control. We need to flag all of them.\n\nLet me restructure: `evaluator_history` returns `eh` with `calls` dict. In build, we have `ev_calls[e.get(\"id\")], ev_ep[e.get(\"id\")] = eh.get(\"calls\", {}), eh.get(\"endpoint\")`. So we could flag all calls there:\n\n```python\n            ev_calls[e.get(\"id\")], ev_ep[e.get(\"id\")] = eh.get(\"calls\", {}), eh.get(\"endpoint\")\n            flag_call_hands(eh.get(\"calls\", {}).values(), hand)\n```\n\nBut careful: `named` deduplicates by name (`named.setdefault(c.name or str(c.i), c)`). If two calls have the same name, only the first is kept. Hmm. That could miss some calls. Better: have `evaluator_history` also return the raw list, or flag inside `evaluator_history`.\n\nActually simpler: flag inside `evaluator_history` and `maker_history`? But `hand` dict is in build. We could pass a callback or return the entries. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: return `_calls` list from evaluator_history too. Let me add `\"_calls\": calls` to the returned dict, and then in build:\n\n```python\n            flag_call_hands(eh.get(\"_calls\") or [], hand)\n```\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety: the calls list in `evaluator_history` is `[c for c in H.read_log(log, ref, who) if c.at]` — filters out calls with no `at`. A call with no `at` would have both sent_at and received_at missing... Actually if `c.at` is None, read_log adds a refusal (\"carries neither a response Date header nor a `created` time\"), so the whole emit refuses anyway. But for completeness, flagging calls with no `at` doesn't hurt. Hmm, but the field naming uses `self.name or self.i`. Fine.\n\nActually, let me be careful: in `maker_history`, `calls = [c for c in H.read_log(log, ref, \"maker\") if c.at]` and then filtered by `m.get(\"calls\")`. So `mcalls` only includes calls with `at`. A call with no `at` is refused by read_log. OK.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually there's a subtlety with `if m.get(\"calls\"): calls = [c for c in calls if c.name in m[\"calls\"]]`. So the maker calls list may be filtered to specific calls. But E3 says every call in every harness log read counts. If the log has calls not in `m[\"calls\"]`, should they count? Hmm. I'd say yes — \"every call in every harness log the emitter reads\". The log is read; all its calls count. But the filter is for selecting which calls define startedAt/producedAt. Let me flag all calls read from the log, before filtering. Hmm, but the filtered ones aren't \"read\" for times... Let me think about what's safest.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: if we flag every call in the log including ones not selected, then a log with extra calls lacking logger times would refuse. That seems consistent with T7b: \"a call that has no logger sent_at/received_at is either refused as hand-time or...\". The bar says \"a call\". I think flagging all calls in the log is right.\n\nBut hmm, there's a risk: what if a harness log contains a call with no sent_at/received_at that is intentionally not part of the job (e.g., a warm-up call)? Then the emitter would refuse. The bar says a call with no logger times is a hand time. So refusing is correct behavior. OK.\n\nLet me now design the implementation:\n\nIn `maker_history`, return `\"_calls\": calls` (already does, but calls is filtered). Hmm. Let me add `\"_all_calls\": all_calls` where all_calls is the unfiltered list. Actually, simpler: flag inside build using the returned calls. But which list?\n\nLet me look at how `mcalls` is used:\n\n```python\n    mk = maker_history(...) if m.get(\"kind\") == \"model\" else {}\n    mcalls = mk.get(\"_calls\") or []\n    if mcalls:\n        us = [c for c in mcalls if c.started is not None]\n        ua = [c for c in mcalls if c.at is not None]\n        ...\n```\n\nI'll change `maker_history` to also return the full list, or just flag within `maker_history`. Hmm, passing `hand` into maker_history would be a signature change. Let me just return `\"_calls\"` as the full list and handle the filtering for startedAt/producedAt separately. Wait, the filter `if m.get(\"calls\")` affects which calls define startedAt/producedAt. Let me keep that.\n\nActually simplest: in maker_history, after reading calls and filtering, add:\n\n```python\n    out[\"_calls\"] = calls\n    out[\"_all_calls\"] = all_read_calls\n```\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me restructure minimally:\n\n```python\ndef maker_history(job, ev_dir, ci, m, ref, pin=None, gaps=None) -> dict:\n    ...\n    all_calls = H.read_log(log, ref, \"maker\")\n    calls = all_calls\n    if m.get(\"calls\"):\n        calls = [c for c in calls if c.name in m[\"calls\"]]\n    calls = [c for c in calls if c.at]\n    if not calls:\n        return {}\n    ...\n    out = {..., \"_calls\": calls, \"_log_calls\": all_calls, ...}\n```\n\nThen in build:\n\n```python\n    if mcalls:\n        flag_call_hands(mk.get(\"_log_calls\") or mcalls, hand)\n```\n\nHmm wait, but if `calls` is empty (no call has `at`), maker_history returns `{}` before we can flag. In that case, read_log has already refused (no at). So it doesn't matter.\n\nHmm, actually read_log only refuses if `c.at is None` for a call — \"carries neither a response Date header nor a `created` time\". So a call with a Date header has `at` from the Date header. OK.\n\nLet me simplify: I'll flag inside `maker_history` and `evaluator_history` by returning the hand entries. Hmm, that changes more.\n\nAlternative approach: in build, after getting `mk`, flag all calls from `mk.get(\"_calls\")`. Since `_calls` is the filtered list (by m[\"calls\"] and by c.at), and typically m[\"calls\"] is not set, `_calls` is all calls with `at`. Calls without `at` are refused anyway. So flagging `_calls` covers everything that matters.\n\nWait, is that right? A call without `at` but with sent_at? `at = received or date or created`. If received exists, at exists. If no received, date or created → at. So `at is None` means no received, no date, no created → both sent_at and received_at missing → read_log refuses. So a call without `at` is always a refusal. Good.\n\nSo flagging `_calls` (calls with at) is sufficient.\n\nSimilarly for evaluator_history: `calls = [c for c in H.read_log(...) if c.at]`. `named` dedupes. Let me add `\"_calls\": calls` to the return dict and flag those.\n\nHmm wait, but `named` uses `c.name or str(c.i)`. If the log has calls without names, `str(c.i)` is the index. `hand_time_entries` uses `f\"{self.log}#{self.name or self.i}\"`. So field names use the index. Good.\n\nBut if two calls share a name, `named` keeps only one, but `_calls` has both. Flagging `_calls` covers both. But then the field names would collide (same name), and `hand[e[\"field\"]] = e` would overwrite. That's an edge case; fine.\n\nOK. Now let me also think about E1's effect on the `disposition_at_source` and sources.\n\nCurrent:\n```python\n    if not at:\n        at = H.iso(now)\n        ...\n    elif live:\n        xp[\"disposition_at_source\"] = \"logged live (inputs/custody-events.jsonl)\"\n        xp[\"sources\"][\"disposition.at\"] = f\"{LEDGER_REL}:{live['_line']}\"\n```\n\nWith the fix, `at` = decided_at when live has it. The source is the ledger line. That's right: the ledger line carries the decided_at value. Good.\n\nNow let me think about `check_order`. The disposition time D = decided_at. The order check: `before(\"the last check\", last, \"disposition.at\", D)` and `if n != \"disposition.at\" and close and t > close + ...`. For disposition.at, it skips the close check (since disposition is often after close?). Actually the base code skips close check for disposition.at. And `if t > now + ...` — decided_at <= tool clock <= now. Fine.\n\nHmm wait, there's one more: the ledger `dispose` line's `at` (tool clock) is not checked against `now`. That's fine.\n\nNow let me also consider: does the T4 job's dispose line have `decided_at`? Yes: \"dispose --decided-at\". And decided_at >= last logged check. Good.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety: the ledger's `last_check_time` for the bound uses evaluator `received_at` in harness logs. If the harness log has no `received_at` (server time), then `last_check_time` doesn't include it. Hmm, but that's a ledger concern, not the emitter. Not in this part.\n\nOK now let's think about the tests. The feedback says:\n\n\"Tests: extend tests/validate_schemas.py (or new fixture folders under tests/fixtures/custody/) so a job built only with kit/custody_ledger.py (freeze/approve/pin/generation/evaluation/dispose --decided-at) emits exit 0 with no switch and an empty hand_times, and so E2 and E3 are covered. Never rewrite a file that exists under tests/fixtures/ at base.\"\n\nSo I need to add tests to validate_schemas.py:\n1. A tool-built job: build from scratch (or a copy of a fixture) using only ledger commands, emit with no switch → exit 0, empty hand_times.\n2. E2: typed pass `at` with a linked call is a hand time (refused without switch).\n3. E3: evaluator calls with no logger sent_at are flagged.\n\nBuilding a full job from scratch with only ledger tools is complex: we need a job dir with packet.json, inputs, custody/bar.md, evidence files, harness logs, passes files, etc. The ledger commands only write custody.json and the event log.\n\nHmm. Let me think: can I build the T4 job by copying the fixture `job-rejected` and then re-typing its custody.json via ledger commands? The point of T4 is \"a job built at batch time only by the subject's own tools\". For a test, we can take a copy of the fixture job, delete inputs/custody.json and inputs/custody-events.jsonl, and rebuild custody.json via ledger commands: freeze, approve (buyer, specialist), pin (maker), generation open/close, evaluation, dispose --decided-at.\n\nBut custody.json also needs: bar.dimensions, maker, evaluators, passes, controls, external, retention, disposition (set by dispose), etc. The ledger commands only set bar.file/frozenAt/approvals, orchestrator, routing, disposition.\n\nHmm. So a \"job built only with ledger tools\" for custody.json would lack dimensions, maker, evaluators, passes, controls... unless the Orchestrator writes those parts by hand? The bar says: \"A job built at batch time only by the subject's own tools emits with no switch, exit 0, check.py OK at CL1, and an empty hand_times. The tools used: custody_ledger.py freeze/approve/pin/generation/evaluation/dispose, plus tools/edi/or_call.py with its mocked transport writing the harness logs.\"\n\nHmm, but the custody.json's non-time parts (dim