ixture, remove the time fields from custody.json, and rebuild via ledger commands. But the ledger commands `freeze` refuses if `bar.frozenAt` is already set (\"the bar was already frozen at ...; a bar is frozen once\"). So I need to pop `bar.frozenAt` first. Similarly approve appends/replaces approvals. dispose sets disposition.\n\nHmm, but the ledger `approve` computes `barDigest` from the bar file and checks approval validity (orchestrator, seat match). The fixture's approvals: buyer \"Fixture Buyer\", specialist \"Fixture Specialist\"; packet names buyer \"Owner\"? Hmm. Let me look at the test:\n\n```python\nj = job_copy(\"seat-mismatch\")\nledger_edit(j, lambda c: c[\"bar\"][\"approvals\"][1].update(by=\"Someone Else\"))\nrefused(\"an approver who is not the packet's named seat holder\", j, \"approval\")\n```\n\nSo the packet names the specialist as \"Fixture Specialist\". OK, so approve --role specialist --by \"Fixture Specialist\" would pass.\n\nAnd orchestrator: the fixture's custody.json has `orchestrator`. If I keep it, approve works.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. And I don't know the exact fixture contents (packet, custody.json). Let me think about a safer approach.\n\nAlternative: create a NEW fixture folder under tests/fixtures/custody/ that is a minimal tool-built job. The allowed paths include \"new folders under tests/fixtures/custody/\". But \"A file that exists under tests/fixtures/ at the base is never rewritten (new fixture folders only).\" So I can add e.g. `tests/fixtures/custody/job-tool-built/`.\n\nBut building a whole new fixture requires knowing the custody schema requirements and check.py behavior. The record must be \"check.py OK at CL1\". Hmm, check.py is in the custody repo, not available. The existing fixture job-rejected presumably produces a record that passes check.py at CL1 (the test only validates against the schema, not check.py). Actually the test says \"fixture job emits with no gap (exit 0)\" — not check.py. Hmm.\n\nThe 