me with the fixture's old log times.\n\nUnless... the ledger freeze line's `at` is the tool clock. The emitter uses `frozen_at = b.get(\"frozenAt\") or (fz or {}).get(\"at\")`. So frozenAt = now. And startedAt from the log = 2026-10-20T12:00. now >= 2026-10-20... wait, what is `now`? If the test runs \"now\" in real time (say 2025), then now < 2026-10-20 → the existing fixture would already fail \"after-emit\" for every event. Since iteration 1 passed T7/G2 (base tests pass), the clock must be >= 2026-10-28 or the fixture times are in the past relative to the test clock. Let's assume the test clock is after 2026-10-28.\n\nSo now > 2026-10-20. frozenAt = now > startedAt = 2026-10-20T12:00. Refusal.\n\nHmm. So a tool-built job at test time has a freeze time after the log times. That breaks ordering.\n\nUnless the T4 job's logs are written at test time with test-time timestamps! That's what `or_call.py` would do (mock transport writes `at` = real clock). So the T4 test should synthesize harness logs with times around now.\n\nHmm, this is getting heavy. Let me reconsider.\n\nAlternative: shift the whole job's times. Build the tool job with logs whose times are set relative to now: e.g., freeze at now-1h, calls at now-50min, etc. But the ledger freeze stamps `at` = now (tool clock), not now-1h. So frozenAt = now. Hmm, so the log times must be after now. But then they'd be \"after-emit\" (after the emission now)... but emission happens after the calls in reality. The emitter's `now` is the emission time, which is after the logs. So logs at now-50min are before emission. And freeze at now (the tool clock at freeze time) must be before startedAt... but startedAt is now-50min < freeze now. Refusal.\n\nHmm, the real flow: freeze (t0), then calls (t1 > t0), then dispose (t2), then emit (t3 > t2). So for a test, I'd need to run freeze, sleep, run calls, ... That's slow but could use small sleeps (1s). Hmm.\n\nWait — actually, maybe I can set the log times to be after the freeze