nd dsp.get(\"by\") and live.get(\"by\") != dsp.get(\"by\"))):\n        ref.add(\"time\", f\"disposition: the ledger says {dsp.get('by')!r} at {at!r} but {LEDGER_REL} \"\n                        f\"logged {live.get('by')!r} at {live_at!r}\")\n    if live and not at and live_at:\n        at = live_at\n```\n\nGood.\n\n#### E2\n\nCurrent checks loop:\n```python\n        call = link_call(ev_calls, f.get(\"evaluator\"), f.get(\"call\"),\n                         \"eval_artifact\" if n_passes == 1 else None, f\"passes.{pid}\", ref)\n        if call is not None:\n            e = call_hand(call, \"received_at\")\n            if e:\n                hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n        elif f.get(\"at\"):\n            mark(f\"passes.{pid}.at\", rel_inputs, line_in(inputs_path, f.get(\"at\"), \"at\"), \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n```\n\nNew:\n```python\n        call = link_call(ev_calls, f.get(\"evaluator\"), f.get(\"call\"),\n                         \"eval_artifact\" if n_passes == 1 else None, f\"passes.{pid}\", ref)\n        # A typed `at` is a hand time whether or not a call is linked (T2c): a time only a tool gives is one\n        # the tools wrote, and a time typed into custody.json has no tool behind it.\n        if f.get(\"at\"):\n            mark(f\"passes.{pid}.at\", rel_inputs, line_in(inputs_path, f.get(\"at\"), \"at\"), \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n        if call is not None:\n            e = call_hand(call, \"received_at\")\n            if e:\n                hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n```\n\nHmm, but note: `call_hand(call, \"received_at\")` is now redundant if we flag all calls (E3). Actually with E3, every call's received_at is flagged in `hand`. So the per-link `call_hand` is redundant. But harmless. Actually, wait: if I flag all calls with field `<log>#<name>.received_at`, and the linked call's field is the same, then `hand[e[\"field\"]]` from E3 already covers it. So I can drop the per-link calls. But keeping them doesn't hurt (same dict entries). Hmm, but it's dead code. Let me remove them for clarity? Risk: if E3's flagging misses something... Let me keep them (belt and braces). Actually, let me remove them and rely on E3 to avoid confusion. Hmm, no — let me keep `call_hand` calls; they're harmless and ensure the linked call is flagged even if E3 changes. Actually, I'll remove to keep the code clean, since E3 flags every call including linked ones.\n\nHmm, wait: `flag_call_hands` is called with `eh.get(\"_calls\")` in build. Let me make sure the linked calls are in `_calls`. Yes.\n\nLet me just remove the per-link `call_hand` blocks and add the global flagging. Actually... let me be careful. The evaluator_history returns `calls` as a dict `named` for linking, and I'll add `_calls` list. In build, after `ev_calls[...] = eh.get(\"calls\", {})`, add `flag_call_hands(eh.get(\"_calls\") or [], hand)`.\n\nHmm, but if `eh` is `{}` (log missing), no calls. Fine.\n\nLet me now also handle: the `controls` loop:\n```python\n        call = link_call(ev_calls, k.get(\"evaluator\"), k.get(\"call\"), f\"eval_{str(k.get('id') or '').lower()}\",\n                         f\"controls[{k.get('id')}]\", ref)\n        if call is not None:\n            e = call_hand(call, \"received_at\")\n            if e:\n                hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n            H.cross_check(...)\n            ...\n        elif k.get(\"at\"):\n            mark(f\"controls[{k.get('id')}].at\", rel_inputs, line_in(inputs_path, k.get(\"at\"), \"at\"), \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n            xk[\"at\"] = k[\"at\"]\n            ...\n```\n\nNew: move the mark out:\n```python\n        if k.get(\"at\"):\n            mark(f\"controls[{k.get('id')}].at\", rel_inputs, line_in(inputs_path, k.get(\"at\"), \"at\"), \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n        call = link_call(...)\n        if call is not None:\n            H.cross_check(...)\n            ...\n        elif k.get(\"at\"):\n            xk[\"at\"] = k[\"at\"]\n            ...\n```\n\nWait, the `elif` branch also sets `xk[\"at\"]` and `xk[\"at_source\"]` and `xp[\"sources\"]`. If a call is linked, the record's control time is from the call, not the typed value. The typed value is a hand time (flagged) but the record uses the call's time. That's consistent with E2's instruction: \"keep the cross-check against the call when one is linked.\"\n\nHmm, but should the record's control `at` be the typed value or the call's? The bar says a control with a typed at is a hand time. The record's at — INTERFACE says \"A pass or control with no typed at gets its time from a tool\". So with a typed at, does the record use the typed value? Hmm. Looking at the current code: with a linked call, `xk[\"at\"] = H.iso(call.at)` (the call's time), and `H.cross_check` compares the typed value. So the record uses the tool time, and the typed value is only a cross-check. That's the design. With E2, the typed value is also flagged as a hand time. Fine.\n\nNow, the `mark` uses `line_in(inputs_path, k.get(\"at\"), \"at\")` — the line of the typed value in custody.json. Good.\n\n#### E3\n\nAdd helper:\n\n```python\ndef flag_call_hands(calls, hand: dict) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Every call of a harness log read: a `.sent_at`/`.received_at` the logger did not stamp is a hand time\n    (T7b), named with the server time's source and the file:line that carries it. The log is never rewritten.\"\"\"\n    for c in calls:\n        for e in c.hand_time_entries():\n            hand[e[\"field\"]] = e\n```\n\nIn build, maker:\n```python\n    mcalls = mk.get(\"_calls\") or []\n    flag_call_hands(mcalls, hand)\n```\n(replaces the us/ua block)\n\nWait, the us/ua block flags only first sent_at and last received_at. E3 says flag every call. So replace with flag_call_hands(mcalls, hand).\n\nHmm, but `mcalls` is filtered by `m.get(\"calls\")` and `c.at`. As discussed, calls with no `at` are refused by read_log. And filtered-out calls (not in m[\"calls\"])... E3 says \"every call of every harness log read\". Hmm. Let me include all read calls. Let me modify maker