ead the max received_at from the stamped logs (I control it: max_recv).\n4. last_check = max(E_line, max_recv, ...).\n5. Wait until real >= last_check + 2.5s.\n6. dispose --decided-at = last_check + 1s.\n\nThat's robust. The sleep could be up to a few seconds. Let me compute: if I stamp the logs with base = F and max_recv = F+3, and E_line ≈ F+2, last_check = F+3, wait until F+5.5. So ~4-5s of sleeping. Hmm.\n\nLet me reduce: stamp the logs with base = F and step=1 but only 3 calls: max_recv = F+2 (i=0: F+0/F+1, i=1: F+1/F+2, i=2: F+2/F+3). Hmm 3 calls → i=2 → F+2/F+3. So max_recv = F+3.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, stamp the logs in the PAST relative to F: base = F - 100s. Then max_recv = F-97 < E_line. But then check_order: `before(\"bar.frozenAt\", F, \"startedAt\", S, strict=True)` — F > S → refusal \"order\"! Damn.\n\nHmm. So logs must be after the freeze.\n\nWhat if I stamp the logs at F+1 (single step 0, all calls same second F+1)? Then max_recv = F+1. E_line ≈ F+2 (evaluation at). last_check = max(F+2, F+1) = F+2. Wait until F+4.5, dispose decided_at = F+3. Sleep ≈ 2s.\n\nBut: all eval calls at F+1/F+1 → all checks at F+1. Ordering: producedAt (maker received F+1) <= checks (F+1)? `before(\"producedAt\", Pd, n, t)`: `a > b` → F+1 > F+1 false → ok (non-strict). Good. And startedAt (maker sent F+1) < producedAt (F+1)? `before` non-strict: `S > Pd`? no. OK.\n\nHmm, but `check_order` also has `before(\"startedAt\", S, \"producedAt\", Pd)` non-strict. Fine.\n\nSo: stamp maker log: sent_at = received_at = F+1. Stamp eval log: all calls sent_at = received_at = F+1.\n\nHmm, but `read_log` checks `c.created and c.date` consistency — unchanged, fine.\n\nBut wait: the emitter's `startedAt` = first maker call sent_at = F+1, `producedAt` = last maker received_at = F+1. checks at F+1. Good.\n\nAnd `disposition.at` = decided_at = F+3 (or whatever). `before(\"the last check\", last=F+1, \"disposition.at\", D=F+3)` ok.\n\nAnd `after-emit`: all t <= now + tol. now = emission time ≈