e\"] = {\"note\": \"from the ledger's inputs\", \"verdict\": \"JUNK\", \"hand_times\": [\"JUNK\"],\n                         \"nested\": {\"k\": [1, 2, 3]}}\n    (j / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\").write_text(json.dumps(cj, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n```\n\nThen emit and assert `rt[\"inputs\"] == cj[\"x-plumbline\"]` and `rt[\"verdict\"] != \"JUNK\"` and `rt[\"hand_times\"] == []`.\n\nWait — the X1 fixture \"has input keys that try to set the emitter's own keys (verdict, gaps, times, hand_times): the emitter refuses it, or keeps those keys only under .inputs, and its own values are unchanged.\" Our emitter keeps them under .inputs. Good.\n\nBut careful: `rt[\"hand_times\"]` must be [] (empty), and inputs has `hand_times: [\"JUNK\"]`. The emitter's `xp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]` overwrites, so the emitter's own hand_times is []. Good.\n\nNow the `stamp_log` helper. It must handle both a JSON object (maker log) and a JSON array (eval log). Let me write it to read JSON, handle both, write back with indent=2 (which changes the file, but that's a test copy).\n\nWait — but the fixture's `harness-eval-a.log` is read by `read_log`, which does `json.loads(text)`. If it's a JSON array, fine. If it's JSON Lines, `json.loads` fails and per-line parsing works. My helper needs to handle both. Let me write:\n\n```python\ndef stamp_log(path, at):\n    \"\"\"Give every call the logger's own sent_at/received_at (T7b: without them every call is a hand time).\"\"\"\n    text = path.read_text()\n    try:\n        doc = json.loads(text)\n        items = doc if isinstance(doc, list) else [doc]\n        array = isinstance(doc, list)\n    except ValueError:\n        items, array = [], False\n        for ln in text.splitlines():\n            if ln.strip():\n                items.append(json.loads(ln))\n    for c in items:\n        if isinstance(c, dict):\n            c[\"sent_at\"] = c[\"received_at\"] = at\n    if array:\n        path.write_text(json.dumps(items, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n    else:\n        path.write_text(\"\".join(json.dumps(c) + \"\\n\" for c in items))\n```\n\nHmm, careful: if the maker log is a single object, `array=False`, and I write JSON Lines (one object per line). `read_log` handles that (`json.loads(text)` of a single-line object works too). Fine.\n\nActually, for the single object case, `json.dumps(c) + \"\\n\"` gives a one-line JSON object. `read_log` does `json.loads(text)` → succeeds (single object). Good.\n\nFor the array case, writing with indent=2 produces a JSON array. `read_log` → list. Good.\n\nHmm, but if the eval log is JSON Lines (3 objects on 3 lines), then `json.loads(text)` fails, per-line works. `array=False` → I write 3 lines. Good.\n\nNow, where do I call stamp_log? In tool_built_job, after restoring the maker log and before... hmm, the emitter reads them at emit time. Let me stamp both logs right before the emit, using `at = fz + 1s`.\n\nWait, but the maker log's `startedAt` = sent_at = fz+1, `producedAt` = received_at = fz+1. And the eval checks at fz+1. Then `before(\"producedAt\", Pd=fz+1, n, t=fz+1)` ok.\n\nBut: `check_order`'s `from_log` — `xp[\"times\"][\"startedAt\"] == \"harness log\"` if `mk.get(\"startedAt\")`. Yes. So the tolerance applies for close check. And close is popped, so no close check. Good.\n\nHmm, one more: the fixture's `passes` may have `at` typed — I pop it. And `controls` — pop. Then the times come from calls.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety: `f.get(\"at\")` is None after popping → no mark → no hand time. And `call` is linked → at from call. Good.\n\nBut what if a pass has no linked call (e.g., a script pass)? Then `f.get(\"at\")` is None and no call → `at_v = None` → `ts(None, ...)` → hard gap → exit 1! Hmm. The fixture has one model evaluator, so passes are model passes. Probably fine. But if there's a script pass... Let me check: `evaluators` has one model evaluator (eval-a). The fixture's passes are for eval-a. OK.\n\nHmm, but to be safe, maybe don't pop `at` if there's no call... too complex. Let me assume the fixture is coherent (it emits exit 0 with typed times; without typed times, the calls provide them).\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, there's a risk: if the fixture's passes DON'T have `at` (times from calls), then popping is a no-op and the fixture already emits with calls' times. But then E2's claim \"a typed passes.at is cross-checked but never flagged\" wouldn't apply to the fixture... but the feedback says it did. Ugh, uncertainty.\n\nLet me look at the `tol-59` test again:\n\n```python\nj = job_copy(\"tol-59\")\nledger_edit(j, lambda c: c[\"passes\"][\"pass-a\"].update(at=\"2026-10-20T12:01:02Z\"))\nrc, out = emit(j, TMP / \"tol59.json\")\nrt = json.loads((TMP / \"tol59.json\").read_text())[\"predicate\"] if rc == 0 else {}\nres((f\"{S} · a hand time 59 s off is accepted, and the record carries the LOG's time\", rc == 0 and\n     rt[\"checks\"][0][\"at\"] == \"2026-10-20T12:00:03Z\", ...))\n```\n\nIf pass-a had no `at` by default, `update(at=...)` adds it. If it had one, update changes it. Either way. The assertion `rt[\"checks\"][0][\"at\"] == \"2026-10-20T12:00:03Z\"` shows the check's at is the call's received_at (12:00:03). And the typed value 12:01:02 is within 59s of 12:00:03 → accepted. So the fixture's call received_at is 12:00:03.\n\nAnd `tol-61` sets 12:01:04 (61s off) → refused.\n\nHmm OK. Now, whether the fixture's pass-a has a default `at` — the `tol-59` test would work either way. But E2's feedback: \"with a linked call a typed `passes.<id>.at` is cross-checked but never flagged\". This is a general statement about the code path. It doesn't prove the fixture has one.\n\nBut T2 requires naming EVERY hand-time field. If the fixture had a typed passes.at, T2 would have failed (the field wasn't named). T2 isn't in the failed list. So the fixture does NOT have a typed passes.at. OK, so popping is a no-op for passes. Good. But E2 is still needed for held-out fixtures.\n\nHmm, then what about controls? Same — probably no typed at.\n\nOK so the T4 rebuild: passes/controls have no `at`; times from calls. Good.\n\nWait, but then the fixture's checks' times come from calls (received_at 12:00:03) and there's no hand time for them. And E3 adds hand times for the missing sent_at of those calls. Right.\n\nSo in the T4 rebuild, I stamp the logs → no hand times from calls. And the freeze/approve/pin/dispose lines → no hand times. Result: empty hand_times. \n\nBut hold on: does the fixture have a `startedAt`/`producedAt` typed in custody.json? `if not mk.get(\"startedAt\") and ci.get(\"startedAt\")`. The maker_history provides startedAt from the log, so ci's is only a cross-check (`H.cross_check(ref, \"startedAt\", ci.get(\"startedAt\"), started, ...)`). If the fixture has ci.startedAt = 2026-10-20T12:00:00 and I stamp the log at fz+1 (a different date), the cross-check would refuse \"time\"! So I must pop startedAt/producedAt from custody.json. I do that. Good.\n\nOK, now let me also double check the fixture's maker log's `response.created`/`Date` vs. my stamped sent_at/received_at. `read_log` checks `c.created and c.date` consistency — unchanged. It doesn't compare sent_at with created. So no issue.\n\nBut `check_order` uses `call.at` = received_at (stamped) for checks. And the maker's producedAt = stamped received_at. Fine.\n\nHmm, wait: `check_order` for the disposition: `D = P(disposition.at)`. decided = last+1. And `last` includes fz+2. So D >= fz+3. And `now` (emit) > D. Fine.\n\nHmm, also: `xp[\"timeSources\"]` etc. Fine.\n\nNow — `xp[\"sources\"][\"startedAt\"]` = `first.source_started()` = the line of sent_at. Fine.\n\nOK. Let me also consider: `dispose` sets `ci[\"disposition\"][\"at\"] = decided_at`. Then the emitter compares `live_at = decided_at` with `at = dsp.at = decided_at` → equal. Good. And `live.by == dsp.by`. Good.\n\nAnd `disposition_at_source` = \"logged live...\". Good.\n\nNow, the record's `disposition.at` = decided_at. \n\nLet me now also make sure the `evaluation` ledger command's `--evaluator` matches the fixture's evaluator id. Read `cj[\"evaluators\"][0][\"id\"]`.\n\nHmm, and `--batch`: the pass's batch. Let me read `cj[\"passes\"][\"pass-a\"][\"batch\"]` if present, else \"b1\". Actually the batch in the evaluation line isn't used by the emitter for checks (the checks use `f.get(\"batch\")` from passes). So any batch works. But let me use a real one for coherence.\n\nHmm, actually, wait: `evaluation` line's `at` is used by `last_check_time` in the ledger. Fine.\n\nNow let me write the whole test block. Also need `import time`.\n\nLet me also double-check the `emit` helper's default `allow_hand=True`; for the T4 test I pass `allow_hand=False`.\n\nNow, let me think about whether the tool-built job will have hard gaps. The fixture emits exit 0 (no hard gaps). Removing `at` from passes/controls and providing times from calls shouldn't create gaps. Removing `bar.frozenAt`/approvals and rebuilding them via ledger: the approve command writes the same roles/names/digests. The freeze writes the same bar file. So the record should be equivalent. Routing: pin with the same values. disposition: same by, different at (decided_at) — fine.\n\nOne concern: the `freeze` command's `bar_sha256` = sha of the bar file at freeze time. The emitter checks `fz[\"bar_sha256\"] != bar_digest[\"sha256\"]` → equal (same file). Good.\n\nAnother: `approve` writes `barDigest` = sha of the bar file. The emitter's `hexdigest(a.get(\"barDigest\"), ...)` copies it. Fine.\n\nHmm, `approve` calls `H.check_approval(appr, 0, ci, packet, ref)` — this checks the packet's seat names. The fixture's packet names \"Fixture Buyer\"/\"Fixture Specialist\" (since the fixture's approvals are by them and pass). Good.\n\nBut: `check_approval` also checks `looks_agent(by)`. \"Fixture Buyer\" — no agent words. Good.\n\nOK. Now, one more risk: the `roles` I read from custody.json might include more than buyer/specialist? The `approve` command requires `--role` in (\"buyer\",\"specialist\"). If the fixture has other roles... unlikely.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\nActually, wait. I realize there might be an issue with `run_py` and the ledger: `run_py([str(LEDGER), \"freeze\", \"--job-dir\", str(j)])`. The `LEDGER` = `SNAP / \"kit\" / \"custody_ledger.py\"`. And it imports custody_history from SNAP/kit. Good.\n\nBut the ledger's `writer_stamp` = sha12 of SNAP/kit/custody_ledger.py. And the emitter reads `writer` != \"hand\" → tool. Good.\n\nHmm, `writer_source`: `w = e.get(\"writer\")`; if `w == \"hand\"` → hand; if `w in (None, \"\")` → no-writer; else None (tool). So `\"kit/custody_ledger.py@abc\"` → None. Good.\n\nNow, let me write the final test block and code.\n\nLet me also double check the `refused` helper is unaffected.\n\nOne more thing: the feedback says \"Keep the hand-time marking at lines 793-798 as it is.\" — that's the disposition marking block. I keep it.\n\nLet me now write the full emit_custody.py with changes.\n\nLet me carefully produce the file. I'll take the given file and apply:\n\n1. Remove `call_hand` function (or keep). Let me keep it? If I remove usages, an unused function remains. Let me remove it to be clean. Actually, hmm — maybe keep it as it might be referenced elsewhere? No, it's module-level, only used in build. I'll remove it and add `flag_calls`.\n\nWait, actually — let me reconsider: is `call_hand` used anywhere else? Let me search the file text... \"call_hand(\" appears at:\n- def call_hand\n- in build: `e = call_hand(min(us...`, `e = call_hand(max(ua...`\n- in checks: `e = call_hand(call, \"received_at\")`\n- in controls: `e = call_hand(call, \"received_at\")`\n\nI'll remove all.\n\n2. `maker_history` signature + flag.\n3. `evaluator_history` signature + flag.\n4. build: pass `hand` to both; remove us/ua block; remove per-link call_hand.\n5. E1 disposition.\n6. E2 marks.\n\nLet me write it out.\n\nAlso, careful about the `if mcalls:` block removal. The current block:\n\n```python\n    mcalls =