raint is the evaluation line's `at` (tool clock at evaluation) and the evaluator received_at (fz+1). So last_check = max(evaluation at, fz+1). If evaluation runs at fz+1.x (truncated fz+1), then last = fz+1, decided = fz+2, wait until fz+3.9. Sleep ≈ 2.4s.\n\nBut if the evaluation runs at fz+2.x, last = fz+2. So my `fz+2` floor is a safe upper bound. Let me keep `fz + timedelta(seconds=2)`.\n\nHmm, actually, is `fz + 2` really an upper bound? The evaluation command's `at` could be fz+3 if the commands are slow. Then last = fz+3, decided = fz+4. The loop waits accordingly. So it's adaptive. Good.\n\nOK, ~3s sleep. Fine.\n\nNow, there's a risk: the ledger's `dispose` bound also includes `last_check_time`, which reads the evaluator logs' received_at (fz+1) and the evaluation line's at. My `last` uses the same events plus fz+2. So decided >= last_check. Good. Actually, `last_check_time` also scans `evdir.rglob(\"*.json\")` for instrument summaries (writer tools/edi/). The fixture's evidence might have such? Probably not. If it does, its finished_at could be later. Hmm. To be safe, let me catch the dispose refusal and retry with a later decided_at:\n\n```python\n    decided = last + timedelta(seconds=1)\n    while True:\n        while time.time() < decided.timestamp() + 1.9:\n            time.sleep(0.05)\n        rc, out = run_py([str(LEDGER), \"dispose\", \"--job-dir\", str(j), *args, \"--decided-at\", _iso(decided),\n                          \"--decided-source\", \"chat\"])\n        if rc == 0:\n            break\n        if \"is before the last logged check\" in out:\n            decided += timedelta(seconds=1)\n            continue\n        raise RuntimeError(f\"ledger dispose refused: {out.strip()[-200:]}\")\n```\n\nHmm, that's more robust. But if it loops, it sleeps again. Fine.\n\nHmm, but `run` raises on failure. Let me restructure to use run_py directly for dispose.\n\nOK. Let me simplify: compute `last` generously as `max(events, fz+2)`, and also add a fixed margin. If dispose refuses for being too early, bump and retry. Let me implement the retry.\n\nNow, E2 test:\n\n```python\nj = job_copy(\"t2-pass-at\")\ncj = json.loads((j / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\").read_text())\npass_at = \"2026-10-20T12:00:03Z\"\ncj[\"passes\"][\"pass-a\"][\"at\"] = pass_at\n(j / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\").write_text(json.dumps(cj, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\nrc, out = emit(j, TMP / \"t2pass.json\", allow_hand=False)\nres((f\"{S} · T2c/E2 a typed passes.<id>.at is a hand time even when a call is linked (inputs-no-ledger)\",\n     rc == 3 and \"passes.pass-a.at\" in out and \"inputs-no-ledger\" in out, f\"exit {rc}: {out.strip()[-140:]}\"))\n```\n\nWait, but `cj[\"passes\"][\"pass-a\"]` — key name \"pass-a\"? From the test `c[\"passes\"][\"pass-a\"]`. Yes.\n\nHmm, but is the evaluator linked? If the fixture's pass-a has no call and n_passes==1, the default \"eval_artifact\" is used → linked. So the call IS linked. Good, that's exactly E2's case.\n\nAnd the check `\"passes.pass-a.at\" in out` — the refusal line is `  [hand-time] passes.pass-a.at inputs/custody.json:NN (inputs-no-ledger)`. Good.\n\nAlso test the allow path: with allow_hand=True, the field appears in hand_times.\n\n```python\no = TMP / \"t2pass-allow.json\"\nrc, out = emit(j, o, allow_hand=True)\nht = {e.get(\"field\"): e for e in (json.loads(o.read_text())[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"][\"hand_times\"] if o.is_file() else [])}\nres((f\"{S} · T2c/E2 the typed pass time is listed under --allow-hand-times (and the record carries the call's time)\",\n     rc == 0 and \"passes.pass-a.at\" in ht and ht[\"passes.pass-a.at\"].get(\"source\") == \"inputs-no-ledger\"\n     and json.loads(o.read_text())[\"predicate\"][\"checks\"][0][\"at\"] == \"2026-10-20T12:00:03Z\",\n     f\"exit {rc}: {out.strip()[-140:]}\"))\n```\n\nHmm, the check's at == 12:00:03 (the call's received_at) and the typed value is also 12:00:03. That's confusing — both same. Let me use a different typed value within toler