tion.at = 12:30 and the line decided_at = 12:31 → mismatch → refusal. But also the record's disposition.at would be... never written. Fine.\n\nAlso, `by` must match to isolate the time mismatch. I set the line's by = cj's by. Good.\n\nHmm, but there's another subtlety: the line has no `writer` → writer_source = \"ledger-no-writer\" → `mark(\"disposition.at\", ...)`. That's a hand-time mark too! With allow_hand=True, it's listed. But the refusal for the mismatch still fires (ref.add(\"time\")). So rc=3. Good. Actually, hmm — with a no-writer line, the disposition.at is also a hand time, but with allow_hand, no hand-time refusal. And the time mismatch refusal fires. Good.\n\nActually, let me add `\"writer\": \"kit/custody_ledger.py@abc123def456\"` to avoid the no-writer hand mark. I did. Good.\n\nNow let me also think: does adding these tests risk breaking on the actual fixture contents? The risk is in `tool_built_job` (heavy). Let me make it defensive with try/except and report failure (not crash).\n\nActually, if `tool_built_job` raises, the whole suite crashes (exception propagates). I wrapped it in try/except and `res(...)` with False. But `res` takes (name, ok, why). Let me make sure the except is structured.\n\nHmm, but if it fails, the test FAILS — which is correct behavior for a broken fix, but if my assumptions about the fixture are wrong, the whole part fails. Risky but necessary.\n\nLet me reduce risk: instead of requiring the exact maker log name, glob for `harness-maker*.log`. And for roles, handle empty.\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe I don't need to delete the maker log for pin if there are no calls in it... but there is a call. OK.\n\nHmm, one more risk: `run(\"approve\", ...)` — the fixture's approvals might include `kind: \"agent\"` or `adopted`. If a fixture approval is an agent with adopted, then `approve --by X` (default person) would be refused by `check_approval` (looks_agent or seat mismatch). Hmm. The default fixture (job-rejected) should be