les),\n                         \"--evidence\", str(evidence)], capture_output=True, text=True)\n    if rv.returncode == 0:\n        return 0, rv\n    return 1, rv\n```\n\nR1 says: \"run `[sys.executable, check.py, RECORD, \"--files\", FILES, \"--evidence\", EVIDENCE]` once (no guessing forms); exit 0 = OK; any other exit = kind `standard`, exit 3.\"\n\nHmm — \"any other exit = kind standard, exit 3\" — including exit 2 from check.py? The bar V1 says the checker exit 2 is \"not checkable\"; but R1 says any other exit → standard, exit 3. Wait, but our exit 2 is reserved for cannot-run. Let me re-read: \"exit 0 = OK; any other exit = kind `standard`, exit 3.\" OK, so checker exit 1 or 2 → 3. Only our own cannot-run conditions (missing input, missing pin, wrong bytes) → 2. Fine.\n\nBut note the checker may not exist and no pin → exit 2 (cannot run). Keep.\n\nWait — one problem: the standard check currently happens before loading the record; we need the record path. Also the `--bar` flag: optional. Skip.\n\nAlso V1 says \"runs it on the record at the claimed level\" — the checker reads the claimed level from the record. No --level needed.\n\nNow, R2: remove `\"kit/emit_custody.py@\"` from check_hand_times. Replace with form check:\n\n```python\nWRITER_FORM = re.compile(r\"^kit/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\\.py@[0-9a-f]{12}$\")\n\ndef allowance_writer_ok(w) -> bool:\n    return isinstance(w, str) and WRITER_FORM.match(w) is not None and \"..\" not in w\n```\n\nGood.\n\nAlso check: does anything else in the verifier reference the emitter? The docstring says \"This file must not import kit/emit_custody.py...\" — R2 says remove every reference to the emitter's module or path from kit/plumbline_verify.py. The static check \"reads that as a reference to the emitter and exits 2\" — probably a check for the string \"emit_custody\" in the file. The docstring contains \"kit/emit_custody.py\"! Must remove that too. Also \"the emitter's logic is checked by separate code\" — reword without the path. Let me scrub the whole file for \"emit_custody\". I'll write the docstring to say \"This file must not import the record emitter (V7)\".\n\nAlso check_spend's `labels` uses call names; fine.\n\nNow, one more R2-related detail: `find_log`/`read_calls` etc. don't reference the emitter. The `times` values \"hand-entered\"/\"harness log\" fine.\n\nLet me now also double-check `main()` flow for the tests:\n\n```python\njob = ledger.parent.parent if ledger.parent.name == \"inputs\" else files\n```\n\nFor fixture jobs, ledger = job/inputs/custody-events.jsonl → parent.name == \"inputs\" → job = ledger.parent.parent = job. Good.\n\n`custody_json = ledger.parent / \"custody.json\"` ✓.\n\n`read_calls(job, evidence, ci)` — evidence dir; `H.read_log` on logs; none in fixtures. But careful: `if not allc and Path(ev_dir).is_dir(): for log in sorted(Path(ev_dir).glob(\"*.log\"))` — our evidence dirs are empty → fine.\n\n`hand_time_set(job, ledger, custody_json, rec, evidence)` — reads custody.json; empty dict → seats empty; no bar; etc. → hand []. Good.\n\nNow, about `read_calls` when ci is `{}`: `m = ci.get(\"maker\") or {}` → kind not model → skip; evaluators none. Good.\n\nNow the schema validation in main: `check_schema(rec, SCHEMA_PATH)` uses the repo schema. The clean record must satisfy it. Let me be careful about the schema's `predicate` → `additionalProperties: true`, requires \"x-plumbline\". And `xPlumbline` `additionalProperties: false` with the listed properties. My clean record's xp: schema, hand_times, generations, evaluations, relays, amendments, spend, times — all allowed. `times` is `{\"type\":\"object\",\"additionalProperties\":{\"type\":\"string\"}}` ✓.\n\nWait: `\"pass_rule\": {}` in properties — an empty schema allows anything. Fine.\n\nNow the schema validation code `_validate`: `properties` iteration; for `xPlumbline`, `additionalProperties: false` → unknown keys error. Required: schema, hand_times.\n\nTop-level: `additionalProperties: false`, required `_type`, `subject`, `predicateType`, `predicate`. Our records have exactly those (plus nothing). Good.\n\n`subject` items: `additionalProperties: false`, required name/digest; digest requires sha256 with pattern `^[0-9a-f]{64}$`. The stub checker needs the digest to match files/artifact.txt. So the record's subject digest must be the sha256 of the fixture artifact file — fixed bytes. I must compute it and hard-code in the fixture record... or have the test compute and patch. Fixture records with hard-coded digests: I must compute the sha of \"artifact.txt\" content. If I write the artifact content, I can compute its sha256 now. Let me pick content and compute.\n\nLet me choose `custody/files/artifact.txt` content = `\"plumbline verifier fixture\\n\"`. sha256? I need to compute exactly. Let me compute mentally? No — I can't reliably. Alternative: the test writes the artifact and record at runtime into a temp copy of the fixture job, computing the digest. But R3 wants repo fixtures; the test can copy the fixture folder to temp and patch the digest. Hmm, that's fine — fixtures are repo files, tests copy them. But it's simpler to make the record's subject empty: `\"subject\": []` — then the stub checker passes trivially, and the schema allows an empty array (no minItems). But then P-V1 (altered subject digest → standard fail) needs a subject. I can create the standard-failure record variant at runtime by adding a subject with a wrong digest. Or have a fixture record with one subject whose digest is wrong, expecting exit 3.\n\nHmm, but the clean record with an empty subject: is that \"clean\"? The bar's clean control C-Vc is the T4 tool-built job; my own clean fixture just needs exit 0. An empty subject is acceptable for my fixture. But it's nicer to have a real subject.\n\nActually, let me compute the sha256 of a small content. I can't run code here... Let me instead make the test compute it: the fixture clean record has `\"subject\": [{\"name\": \"artifact.txt\", \"d