me field twice). Harmless. But for the unknown-key test, the first `_validate(record, ...)` already catches it at `$.predicate.x-plumbline.unknown_key`. Fine.\n\nHmm, wait: `_validate` on the record descends into `predicate` → `x-plumbline` → `$defs/xPlumbline` via `$ref` → checks additionalProperties ✓.\n\nNow `iter_xplumbline(record)` yields `(\"predicate.x-plumbline\", xp)` and nested ones. For `predicate.x-plumbline` we skip. Nested x-plumbline objects: in our fixtures there are none (the emitter writes `maker.x-plumbline`, `checks[].x-plumbline`, etc., which are objects). `xNested` requires object → passes for dicts. If a nested value is a string (emitter bug), fail. OK.\n\nNow let me write everything out.\n\nAlso, I should double check `main`'s handling when `--custody` points to a dir with no checker and no pin: exit 2. In the close-wiring test we monkeypatch run_verifier, so no issue.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\nFinal check on `check_shadow`'s use of `calls_by_seat` for `times`: with the T4 job (maker model + log), `mcalls` non-empty → \"harness log\" ✓.\n\nBut hmm — what if the maker is a model but `m.get(\"calls\")` filters to a subset; still non-empty ✓.\n\nWhat if the maker is a person (no log)? The emitter's `mk = {}` → \"hand-entered\" ✓.\n\nOK.\n\nLet me also make `read_calls` robust: `ci` may be `{}` → `evidence_dirs` handles.\n\nWriting now.\n\nFor `find_log` with a list of dirs and a pattern:\n\n```python\ndef find_log(job: Path, dirs: list[Path], rel: str | None, pattern: str | None):\n    if rel:\n        for base in dirs:\n            p = Path(base) / rel\n            if p.is_file():\n                return p\n        return None\n    hits: list[Path] = []\n    seen: set[str] = set()\n    for base in dirs:\n        if not Path(base).is_dir():\n            continue\n        for p in sorted(Path(base).glob(pattern)) if pattern else []:\n            k = str(p.resolve())\n            if k not in seen:\n                seen.add(k)\n                hits.append(p)\n    return hits[0] if len(hits) == 1 else None\n```\n\nHmm — `sorted(Path(base).glob(pattern)) if pattern else []` — precedence: `for p in (sorted(...) if pattern else [])`. Write it explicitly.\n\nNow `read_calls`:\n\n```python\ndef read_calls(job: Path, ev_dir: Path, ci: dict):\n    by: dict[str, dict] = {}\n    allc: list = []\n    dirs = evidence_dirs(job, ev_dir, ci)\n\n    def add(seat: str, log):\n        if not log:\n            return\n        try:\n            calls = H.read_log(Path(log), H.Refusals(), str(seat))\n        except Exception:\n            return\n        d = by.setdefault(str(seat), {})\n        for c in calls:\n            name = getattr(c, \"name\", None) or str(getattr(c, \"i\", \"\"))\n            d[name] = c\n            allc.append(c)\n\n    if isinstance(ci, dict):\n        m = ci.get(\"maker\") or {}\n        if m.get(\"kind\") == \"model\":\n            add(\"maker\", find_log(job, dirs, m.get(\"harness_log\"), \"harness-maker*.log\"))\n        for e in ci.get(\"evaluators\") or []:\n            if e.get(\"kind\") == \"model\":\n                rel = e.get(\"harness_log\") or next(\n                    (x.get(\"evidence_file\") for x in ci.get(\"external\") or []\n                     if x.get(\"subject\") == e.get(\"id\")), None)\n                add(str(e.get(\"id\")), find_log(job, dirs, rel, None))\n    if not allc:\n        for base in dirs:\n            if Path(base).is_dir():\n                for log in sorted(Path(base).glob(\"*.log\")):\n                    add(log.stem, log)\n    return by, allc\n```\n\nHmm — the fallback globbing every *.log when no calls found could pick up unrelated logs. But it's a fallback; keep (iteration 1 had it).\n\nCareful: the fallback loops over dirs including the job dir; a log at job root would be added. Fine.\n\nNow `hand_time_set`'s log scan: use `evidence_dirs`.\n\nNow, `main`: `calls_by_seat, all_calls = read_calls(job, evidence, ci)`.\n\nLet me write the final verifier