ts[1]\nVERIFIER = ROOT / \"kit\" / \"plumbline_verify.py\"\nFIX = ROOT / \"tests\" / \"fixtures\" / \"custody\" / \"verify\"\nsys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT / \"kit\"))\nimport plumbline_job as J  # noqa: E402\nimport plumbline_verify as V  # noqa: E402\n```\n\nHmm — importing `plumbline_verify` at module level: it imports `custody_history`. OK.\n\nHelper functions as above.\n\nThen:\n\n```python\nclass KindTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp(self):\n        self._tmp = tempfile.TemporaryDirectory()\n        self.tmp = Path(self._tmp.name)\n\n    def tearDown(self):\n        self._tmp.cleanup()\n\n    def job(self, ledger=None, extra=None):\n        return make_job(self.tmp, ledger, extra)\n\n    def custody(self, wrong_pin=False):\n        return checkout(self.tmp, wrong_pin)\n```\n\nCareful: `checkout(self.tmp)` called twice creates the same dir; fine (rewrites). For the wrong pin, use a distinct name. Let me give `checkout(tmp, name)`.\n\n```python\ndef checkout(tmp: Path, name: str = \"checkout\", wrong_pin: bool = False) -> Path:\n    c = tmp / name\n    ...\n```\n\nThen `self.custody()` → tmp/checkout; `self.custody(\"bad\", True)` → tmp/bad.\n\nTests:\n\n```python\n    def test_clean_record_passes_both_layers(self):\n        job = self.job()\n        rv = run_verify(record(\"clean\", \"record.custody.json\"), job, self.custody())\n        self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 0, rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n        self.assertEqual(kinds(rv), [])\n\n    def test_standard_failure_exits_3(self):\n        job = self.job()\n        rv = run_verify(record(\"clean\", \"record-bad-subject.json\"), job, self.custody())\n        self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 3, rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n        self.assertEqual(kinds(rv), [\"standard\"])\n\n    def test_wrong_checker_bytes_exit_2(self):\n        job = self.job()\n        rv = run_verify(record(\"clean\", \"record.custody.json\"), job, self.custody(\"bad-checkout\", True))\n        self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 2, rv.stdout +