simple calls_sum-off case; kind `spend` is first. The bar says P-V5a → `spend`. Good enough.\n\n**shadow** (P-V6): a record where `inputs.verdict`/`inputs.hand_times` values were copied over the owned keys. check_shadow: the second loop fires when `xp[key] == inputs[key] and xp[key] != derived[key]`. Need `verdict` in derived: derived[\"verdict\"] only when s5_record names a file with \"verdict\". Hmm. Alternatively use `hand_times`: inputs.hand_times = [] and xp.hand_times = []? Then xp == inputs and xp != derived? derived hand_times = recomputed hand. If the job has a hand freeze, derived hand_times = [entry], and xp.hand_times = [] (copied from inputs) → hand_times check in check_hand_times fires hand-time-unflagged first. Hmm.\n\nBetter: use `verdict`. Add an s5 record file in the job with `{\"verdict\": \"reject\"}`. Record: xp.s5_record = {\"name\": \"iteration-1-eval-record.json\"}, xp.verdict = \"accept\", xp.inputs = {\"verdict\": \"accept\"}. Then check_shadow: derived[\"verdict\"] = \"reject\" (from the s5 file). First loop: xp.verdict \"accept\" != \"reject\" → shadow. Good, only shadow (no other check touches verdict).\n\nWait, the first loop already fires shadow for verdict. That's P-V6-ish (\"its own values are unchanged\" — the emitter keeps inputs under .inputs and its own values elsewhere). For P-V6 the planted record copies inputs.verdict over the owned verdict → shadow. ✓. Let me also include inputs.hand_times copied... not needed.\n\nActually, careful with `s5_record` path: `s5.get(\"name\")` relative to job → job / \"iteration-1-eval-record.json\". Create that file with `{\"verdict\": \"reject\"}`.\n\nAlso schema: xp.s5_record is `{\"type\":\"object\",\"additionalProperties\":true}` ✓.\n\n**allowance** (P-V3a): described above.\n\nAlso need: exit 2 for wrong-bytes checker. Existing test does that with a temp checkout. But R3 wants fixtures in new folders. I can add a fixture \"bad-checker\" checkout: `tests/fixtures/custody/verify/bad-custody/checker/check.py` + `tools/pin.json` with a wrong pin (all zeros). Then the verifier exits 2. That's a repo fixture. \n\nLet me now design the fixture layout:\n\n```\ntests/fixtures/custody/verify/\n  README.md?                       (optional; maybe not needed)\n  custody-checkout/\n    checker/check.py               (stub standard checker, verifies subject digests)\n    tools/pin.json                 ({\"checker/check.py\": \"<sha256 of the stub>\"})\n  bad-checkout/\n    checker/check.py               (same stub)\n    tools/pin.json                 ({\"checker/check.py\": \"<wrong sha>\"})\n  clean/\n    job/\n      packet.json\n      inputs/custody.json\n      inputs/custody-events.jsonl\n      custody/files/artifact.txt\n      custody/evidence/.keep?      (dirs must exist; git needs a file — use a README or a log)\n    record.custody.json\n  hand/                            (a job with a hand freeze line + its record variants)\n    job/...\n    record-no-allowance.json\n    record-unflagged.json\n    record-flag-without-source.json  (could reuse clean job + a flagged record)\n  ...\n```\n\nHmm, the pin for the stub checker must be the sha256 of the exact bytes of `checker/check.py` as committed. I can compute it in the test dynamically instead: the test writes the pin at runtime into a temp copy of the checkout. That avoids pinning bytes in the fixture (fragile when I write the file). But R3 wants repo fixtures. I can have the fixture checkout with `tools/pin.json` containing a placeholder, and the test rewrites `tools/pin.json` in a temp copy with the actual sha. That's clean and robust.\n\nActually simpler: the test copies the fixture checkout to a temp dir and writes `tools/pin.json` with the computed sha (or a wrong sha for the exit-2 case). Let's do that. Then the fixture `tools/pin.json` can contain `{}` (or be absent). But the bar's V2/V1 test wants a \"wrong-bytes checker copy → exit 2\". The test can copy the checkout, mutate check.py (append a comment)