": \"verify-clean\",\n  \"mode\": \"readings\",\n  \"terminal_state\": \"accepted\"\n}\n```\n\n`inputs/custody.json`:\n```json\n{\n  \"custody_inputs\": \"plumbline/custody-inputs/v1\",\n  \"bar\": {\n    \"file\": \"custody/bar.md\",\n    \"frozenAt\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNow — hmm, `check_shadow` doesn't read custody.json. `hand_time_set` does. Fine.\n\nNow let me write the test file's `_job` helper to also write nothing else.\n\n```python\ndef make_job(tmp: Path, ledger: str | None = None, extra: dict | None = None) -> Path:\n    dst = tmp / \"job\"\n    shutil.copytree(FIX / \"clean\" / \"job\", dst)\n    if ledger:\n        shutil.copyfile(FIX / ledger / \"ledger.jsonl\", dst / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\")\n    for name, rel in (extra or {}).items():\n        shutil.copyfile(FIX / rel, dst / name)\n    return dst\n```\n\nFor shadow: `make_job(tmp, extra={\"iteration-1-eval-record.json\": \"shadow/iteration-1-eval-record.json\"})` — keeps the clean ledger (freeze only) ✓.\n\nFor events/relay/hand: `make_job(tmp, ledger=\"events\")` etc.\n\nNow the record paths:\n- clean: `FIX/clean/record.custody.json`\n- hand: `FIX/hand/record-*.json`\n- events: `FIX/events/record-missing-generation.json`\n- relay: `FIX/relay/record.json`\n- shadow: `FIX/shadow/record.json`\n\nNow writing the tests. Also a test for `test_verifier_has_no_emitter_reference`.\n\nLet me also keep the old unit tests? The feedback says change only what's needed; the old tests (VerifyCliTests, VerifyUnitTests) used temp fake custody and `minimal_record`. Some of them may now fail with R1's change:\n- `test_wrong_checker_bytes_exit_2`: fake custody with wrong pin → 2 ✓ still.\n- `test_missing_block_is_schema`: fake checker exits 0, record no block → schema ✓.\n- `test_hand_times_string_is_schema`: ✓.\n- `test_unknown_top_level_key_is_schema`: ✓.\n- `test_hand_time_set_reads_hand_ledger`: unit test of hand_time_set — should still pass (unchanged).\n- `test_events_missing_generation`: unchanged.\n- `test_spend_mismatch`: unchanged.\n- `test_shadow`: unchanged.\n- `test_verifier_does_not_import_emitter`: `assertNotIn(\"import emit_custody\", text)` — passes.\n\nI'm rewriting the whole test file anyway; I can keep the unit tests and add the new fixture-driven ones. But the fake-checkout tests (`write_fake_custody`) create a checker that exits 0/1 without reading args — with the new `standard_check` running `check.py RECORD --files ... --evidence ...`, the fake checker ignores argv and exits 0/1 ✓. And the pin: `write_fake_custody(tmp, ok=True)` writes pin = sha of check.py → matches ✓. `ok=False` writes \"0\"*64 → mismatch → 2 ✓.\n\nWait: `write_fake_custody` writes the checker with `sys.exit(0 if %r else 1)` — for ok=True it exits 0 regardless of args ✓.\n\nBut `write_job` in the old tests creates a job with `inputs/custody.json` = \"{}\" and empty ledger. Then the clean-ish record `minimal_record({\"schema\": ..., \"hand_times\": []})` passes schema and hand checks; events: xp lacks generations → `got=None != expected=[]` → events failure! Wait: in the old test `test_missing_block_is_schema` etc., the record has only schema/hand_times → check_events: `got = xp.get(\"generations\")` = None; `expected` = [] → None != [] → events fail. But the test only asserts exit 4 and \"FAIL schema\" for schema cases, so extra failures don't matter. For `test_wrong_checker_bytes_exit_2`, exit 2 happens before. ✓\n\nHmm, but `test_missing_block_is_schema`: the record has no x-plumbline → check_schema fails → exit 4 before events ✓.\n\nOK, the old unit tests still pass. But I'll simplify: keep the useful unit tests, drop the redundant CLI ones that are now covered by fixtures. Actually, keeping them is harmless and reduces risk of losing coverage. But `write_fake_custody`/`write_job`/`minimal_record`/`run_cli` add length. The feedback wants new fixtures; the old temp-based tests can stay. Hmm, \"tests/test_plumbline_verify*.py covers every kind in V1-V8 with repo fixtures in new folders\" — the fixtures are the new tests. Keeping the old ones is fine.\n\nLet me keep a trimmed set: the unit tests (hand_time_set, events, spend, shadow, no-emitter-import) and drop the fake-checkout CLI tests, since the fixture tests cover schema/standard/exit-2. Actually the fake-checkout test for exit 2 is now covered by the fixture checkout with a wrong pin. Drop `write_fake_custody` entirely.\n\nWait, but the \"wrong-bytes checker copy → exit 2\" test: my `_checkout(tmp, wrong_pin=True)` writes a pin of zeros → mismatch. ✓\n\nAnd \"missing checker\" → exit 2? Could add a test with a custody dir with no checker... `_find_checker` falls back to REPO/checker/check.py which doesn't exist → None → 2. But if the repo someday has checker/check.py... unlikely. Skip that test or use a nonexistent custody dir. Let me add:\n\n```python\n    def test_missing_checker_exits_2(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as td:\n            tmp = Path(td)\n            job = make_job(tmp)\n            rv = _run(record(\"clean\", \"record.custody.json\"), job, tmp / \"nowhere\")\n            self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 2)\n```\n\n`custody` must be a dir per main's check: `if not custody.is_dir(): return finish(2, ...)`. `tmp/\"nowhere\"` isn't a dir → exit 2 (before the checker). Fine, it's an exit-2 test. Actually that's \"missing input\" not \"missing checker\". OK, keep the wrong-pin one as the checker test.\n\nNow the final structure of the test file:\n\n```python\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\nimport unittest\nfrom hashlib import sha256\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nVERIFIER = ROOT / \"kit\" / \"plumbline_verify.py\"\nFIX = ROOT / \"tests\" / \"fixtures\" / \"custody\" / \"verify\"\nsys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT / \"kit\"))\nimport plumbline_job as J  # noqa: E402\nimport plumbline_verify as V  # noqa: E402\n```\n\nHmm — importing both at module level. `plumbline_verify` imports `custody_history`. Fine.\n\nWait, one concern: `import plumbline_job` — does it define `TERMINALS` etc. without side effects? Yes.\n\nNow, ordering of `sys.path.insert` and imports: flake not enforced.\n\nLet me write the helpers and classes.\n\n```python\ndef checkout(tmp: Path, wrong_pin: bool = False) -> Path:\n    c = tmp / \"checkout\"\n    (c / \"checker\").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    (c / \"tools\").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    src = FIX / \"checkout\" / \"checker\" / \"check.py\"\n    (c / \"checker\" / \"check.py\").write_bytes(src.read_bytes())\n    pin = \"0\" * 64 if wrong_pin else sha256((c / \"checker\" / \"check.py\").read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    (c / \"tools\" / \"pin.json\").write_text(json.dumps({\"checker/check.py\": pin}) + \"\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    return c\n\n\ndef make_job(tmp: Path, ledger: str | None = None, extra: dict | None = None) -> Path:\n    dst = tmp / \"job\"\n    shutil.copytree(FIX / \"clean\" / \"job\", dst)\n    if ledger:\n        shutil.copyfile(FIX / ledger / \"ledger.jsonl\", dst / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\")\n    for name, rel in (extra or {}).items():\n        shutil.copyfile(FIX / rel, dst / name)\n    return dst\n\n\ndef record(folder: str, name: str) -> Path:\n    return FIX / folder / name\n\n\ndef run_verify(rec: Path, job: Path, custody: Path, json_out: Path | None = None):\n    cmd = [sys.executable, str(VERIFIER),\n           \"--record\", str(rec),\n           \"--files\", str(job / \"custody\" / \"files\"),\n           \"--evidence\", str(job / \"custody\" / \"evidence\"),\n           \"--ledger\", str(job / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\"),\n           \"--custody\", str(custody)]\n    if json_out is not None:\n        cmd += [\"--json\", str(json_out)]\n    return subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)\n\n\ndef kinds(rv) -> list:\n    return [ln.split()[1] for ln in rv.stdout.splitlines() if ln.startswith(\"FAIL \")]\n```\n\nNote: `str | None` requires Python 3.10+. The repo uses `from __future__ import annotations` in kit files; for tests, the venv is probably 3.10+. The iteration-1 test didn't use unions. To be safe, avoid `|` in annotations or add `from __future__ import annotations`. I'll add the future import.\n\nNow the tests.\n\nAlso: one more check on `check_events` for the `events` record — `xp.get(\"generations\")` is None and expected has 2 entries → fails. But `check_shadow` also... skipped. `finish` prints events twice (array + calls). `kinds` → [\"events\", \"events\"]. Assert \"events\" in kinds ✓.\n\nNow let me double-check `check_events`'s relay-as-approval detection for the relay record: `approvals` from `predicate.bar.approvals` = [{\"role\": \"relay\", \"by\": \"BuyerBot\", \"at\": ...}]. `a.get(\"role\") == \"relay\"` → True → fail ✓. But note: the loop breaks after the first matching approval for each relay ✓.\n\nNow — wait, there's a subtle bug: `for r in relays: for a in approvals:` — if a relay matches, fail and break. Good.\n\nNow let me write the final verifier. I'll also keep `check_shadow`'s logic but ensure `derived[\"times\"]` matches what the emitter writes. The emitter sets `xp[\"times\"] = {\"startedAt\": \"harness log\" if mk.get(\"startedAt\") else \"hand-entered\", ...}`. My verifier uses `mcalls = calls_by_seat.get(\"maker\")` → if any maker calls, \"harness log\". The emitter's `mk` is non-empty when the maker is a model with calls. Close enough (the emitter part's job is to make records pass). Since the evaluator runs the real emitter output through the verifier, a mismatch here would fail V6 on clean records. Hmm — risk: if the maker has a harness log but `calls_by_seat` fails to find it (e.g. log path resolution differs), derived says \"hand-entered\" while the emitter wrote \"harness log\" → shadow failure on a clean record. That would be bad.\n\nLet me reconsider `read_calls`: it resolves the maker log via `find_log(job, ev_dir, m.get(\"harness_log\"), \"harness-maker*.log\")` where `ev_dir` is the `--evidence` dir. The emitter uses `ev_dir = job / ci.get(\"evidence_dir\", \"custody/evidence\")`. If the verifier's `--evidence` is that same dir, they agree. `plumbline_job.run_verifier` passes `job/custody/evidence`. And the emitter's default evidence_dir is \"custody/evidence\". So they agree unless custody.json overrides `evidence_dir`. Then the verifier would miss the log → maker calls empty → `times` derived \"hand-entered\" but the emitter wrote \"harness log\" → shadow → exit 4 on a clean record! \n\nHmm. That's a real risk. How to make `check_shadow` robust for `times`? Option: don't compare `times` in shadow at all — treat it as a presentation field. But V6 lists `times` as an emitter-owned key the verifier must re-derive. Ugh.\n\nAlternative: derive `times` from the *record itself* only if it can't find logs? That's circular. Or: only flag `times` when the record's value is inconsistent with the presence of *typed* inputs (i.e., a shadow of inputs). Hmm.\n\nLet me re-read V6: \"Every emitter-owned key (`verdict`, `gaps`, `times`, `time_tolerance_s`, `hand_times`, `allowances`, `generations`, `evaluations`, `relays`, `amendments`, `spend`, `schema`) holds the value the verifier re-derives from the §5 record, ledger and logs.\" So the verifier must re-derive `times`. The derivation \"harness log\" vs \"hand-entered\" depends on whether the maker's times came from a log. The verifier can look for the maker log the same way the emitter does: from custody.json's `evidence_dir` and `maker.harness_log`. My `read_calls` uses `--evidence` as ev_dir; I should also try `job / ci.get(\"evidence_dir\", \"custody/evidence\")`.\n\nLet me improve `find_log` usage: in `read_calls`, compute `ev_dir2 = job / ci.get(\"evidence_dir\", \"custody/evidence\")` and search both `evidence` and `ev_dir2`. Simple:\n\n```python\ndef read_calls(job, ev_dir, ci):\n    dirs = [Path(ev_dir)]\n    d = (ci.get(\"evidence_dir\") if isinstance(ci, dict) else None) or \"custody/evidence\"\n    p = Path(job) / d\n    if p not in dirs:\n        dirs.append(p)\n    ...\n```\n\nAnd `find_log` should take a list of dirs. Let me refactor `find_log(job, dirs, rel, pattern)`.\n\nAlso `hand_time_set`'s log scan uses `evidence` and `job` glob — add the custody evidence_dir too. Hmm, the emitter's hand-time server-source flags come from reading the logs. For the verifier to recompute them, it must read the same logs. Let me scan: `--evidence`, `job`, and `job/ci.evidence_dir`.\n\nLet me restructure:\n\n```python\ndef evidence_dirs(job: Path, ev: Path, ci: dict) -> list[Path]:\n    out = [Path(ev)]\n    rel = (ci.get(\"evidence_dir\") if isinstance(ci, dict) else None) or \"custody/evidence\"\n    p = Path(job) / rel\n    out.append(p)\n    out.append(Path(job))\n    seen, uniq = set(), []\n    for d in out:\n        r = str(Path(d).resolve())\n        if r not in seen:\n            seen.add(r)\n            uniq.append(Path(d))\n    return uniq\n```\n\nThen `read_calls` uses `find_log(job, dirs, rel, pattern)`:\n\n```python\ndef find_log(job, dirs, rel, pattern):\n    if rel:\n        for base in dirs:\n            p = Path(base) / rel\n            if p.is_file():\n                return p\n        return None\n    hits = []\n    for base in dirs:\n        if Path(base).is_dir():\n            hits += sorted(Path(base).glob(pattern)) if pattern else []\n    # dedupe\n    ...\n    return hits[0] if len(hits) == 1 else None\n```\n\nHmm, duplicates across dirs (job dir contains custody/evidence? no, glob is non-recursive on `job/*.log`). Use a dict keyed by resolved path.\n\nAnd `hand_time_set` log scan:\n\n```python\nlogs = []\nfor base in evidence_dirs(job, evidence, ci):\n    if Path(base).is_dir():\n        logs += sorted(Path(base).glob(\"*.log\"))\nfor log in dict.fromkeys(logs):\n    ...\n```\n\nOK. This makes the verifier robust for the clean T4 job (evidence dir = job/custody/evidence, matching the emitter).\n\nBut there's another wrinkle: `times` derivation. The emitter sets \"harness log\" if `mk.get(\"startedAt\")` (a model maker with calls). My `read_calls` puts maker calls into `calls_by_seat[\"maker\"]`. If found → \"harness log\". Good.\n\nBut what about `startedAt` when the maker is a model but the log has no stamped calls? The emitter would refuse or flag. Edge case; skip.\n\nActually, maybe safer: derive `times` from the record's `x-plumbline.timeSources`? No, circular.\n\nHmm, let me look at how the emitter sets `times`:\n\n```python\nxp[\"times\"] = {\"startedAt\": \"harness log\" if mk.get(\"startedAt\") else \"hand-entered\",\n               \"producedAt\": \"harness log\" if mk.get(\"producedAt\") else \"hand-entered\"}\n```\n\n`mk` is non-empty only for a model maker with a readable log with calls. So `mk.get(\"startedAt\")` truthy iff model maker + log + calls. My `calls_by_seat[\"maker\"]` is non-empty iff the same (modulo log resolution). So `\"harness log\" if mcalls else \"hand-entered\"` matches. \n\nBut what if the maker is a model whose log exists but `H.read_log` raises in my verifier? Then mcalls empty → mismatch. I catch exceptions broadly → could silently diverge. Risk accepted; the log parsing is the same module.\n\nAlternatively, I could soften: only flag `times` when `xp[\"times\"]` contradicts `hand`/`times` derivable facts... Too clever. Keep it.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually there's a deeper problem: the evaluator's clean control C-Vc includes `job-rejected` emitted with the switch. For job-rejected, the maker is... unknown. If the maker is a model with a log, the verifier must find it. `run_verifier` passes `--evidence job/custody/evidence`, which is the default. OK.\n\nLet me also consider: `read_calls` for evaluators uses `rel = e.get(\"harness_log\") or next((x.get(\"evidence_file\") for x in ci.get(\"external\") ...))`. Matches the emitter. Good.\n\nNow another V6 risk: `spend`. The emitter's `spend_block(lines, all_calls)` uses `calls_sum = round(sum(call_cost(c) for c in calls), 6)` where `calls = mk._calls + all evaluator calls` (all calls read, filtered? `maker_history` returns `_calls` = calls filtered by `m.get(\"calls\")` and `c.at`; `evaluator_history` returns `named` dict values = all calls with `c.at`). My `compute_spend_block` sums `call_cost(c) for c in all_calls if getattr(c, \"at\", None)` — all calls with `at`. Close: the emitter's maker calls are filtered to those with `at` too (`calls = [c for c in calls if c.at]`), and `m.get(\"calls\")` filtering could differ if custody.json names a subset. Edge case. Also the emitter's `call_cost` doesn't guard bool: `isinstance(v, (int, float))` includes bool → True→1.0. Mine excludes bool. Edge case.\n\nGiven the evaluator's clean control is the T4 job (built by tools, with or_call logs), the calls all have `at` and `total_cost`. Should match. I'll align my `call_cost` with the emitter's exactly (drop the bool guard? The emitter's version returns float(v) for bools too; but `total_cost` is never a bool). Keep mine as is; equivalent for real data.\n\nHmm, but `all_calls` in my `read_calls`: I append every call from every log I read, including duplicates if the same log is found via two dirs? `evidence_dirs` dedupes dirs; `find_log` returns one file. But the fallback `if not allc and Path(ev_dir).is_dir(): for log in glob(\"*.log\")` reads all logs in `--evidence` only. Fine.\n\nWait, there's a subtlety: my `read_calls` adds calls per seat via `add(seat, log)`, and `allc` accumulates all. The emitter's `all_calls = list(mk.get(\"_calls\") or []) + evaluator calls`. Same set if the same logs are found. ✓\n\nAnother: the emitter's `spend_block` uses `lines` (the ledger) to find the spend line; mine uses `last_spend_line`. Same.\n\n`key_delta`: emitter `float(sp.get(\"key_delta_usd\") or 0)`; mine same ✓.\n\n`total = max(calls_sum, key_delta)` ✓.\n\n`source`: emitter `f\"{LEDGER_REL}:{sp['_line']}\"`; mine uses `ledger_rel` (relpath). For the default job layout they're equal. ✓\n\nOK.\n\nNow `check_spend`'s extra settle/key-reading checks: for a clean T4 job, the spend ledger has settle lines whose `call` must be among `labels` (call names from logs). The emitter's or_call writes `\"call\": <call id>` and the harness log entries have `name`. Should match if job 1's harness uses `--call` as the name. The INTERFACE says `--call` names the call. `H.read_log` gives `c.name`. Likely `name == --call`. Reasonable.\n\nAnd `key_reading.read_at >= last_settle + 180`: F1g enforces it in the ledger command, so a tool-built job satisfies it.\n\nAnd `abs(r.key_delta_usd - key_delta) > 1e-6` → the ledger line's `key_delta_usd` is copied from the reading → equal ✓.\n\nOK. Risk accepted; the emitter part handles its side.\n\nNow — the `check_spend` settle-line check: `labels = {getattr(c, \"name\", None) or str(getattr(c, \"i\", \"\")) for c in all_calls}`. If a settle line's `call` is None (some lines may lack it), skip. ✓\n\nNow let me finalize.\n\nOne more thought on R1: \"run [sys.executable, check.py, RECORD, \"--files\", FILES, \"--evidence\", EVIDENCE] once (no guessing forms); exit 0 = OK; any other exit = kind standard, exit 3.\" My implementation returns 2 only for missing checker/pin/mismatch. Good.\n\nNow let me write out the verifier file.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Verify a custody/v1 record at two layers: the standard checker, then x-plumbline.\n\nThe standard layer is `checker/check.py` from the pinned custody checkout. It knows nothing about `x-` keys\n(SPEC.md line 141): it reads the custody/v1 predicate and ignores every extension. The Plumbline layer is this\nfile: it runs the standard checker FIRST, then enforces the `x-plumbline` block the emitter writes.\n\n    plumbline_verify.py --record R --files F --evidence E --ledger L --custody <checkout> [--json OUT]\n\nThere is no switch that relaxes a check. The hand-time allowance is read from the record, never