\n      \"evaluations\": [],\n      \"relays\": [],\n      \"amendments\": [],\n      \"spend\": {\"calls_sum_usd\": 0.0, \"key_delta_usd\": 0.0, \"total_usd\": 0.0, \"tolerance_usd\": 0.0001, \"source\": null}\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCheck: `check_shadow` derived hand_times = [{\"field\":\"bar.frozenAt\",\"file\":\"inputs/custody-events.jsonl\",\"line\":1,\"source\":\"ledger-hand\"}] — hand_key compares tuples (field,file,line,source) → equal ✓. allowances not in derived → skipped ✓.\n\nExit 0 expected ✓.\n\nWait — `check_hand_times`: recomp non-empty, allowance dict, switch ✓, writer form ✓, at parses (H.parse_ts) ✓. No fails ✓.\n\nHmm, `H.parse_ts(\"2026-01-01T00:05:00Z\")` → datetime ✓.\n\n`hand/record-no-allowance.json`: same minus allowances.\n\n`hand/record-unflagged.json`: hand_times [], allowances present.\n\nNow `clean/record-flag-without-source.json`: hand_times = [{\"field\": \"bar.frozenAt\", \"file\": \"inputs/custody.json\", \"line\": 5, \"source\": \"inputs-no-ledger\"}]. Hmm, the clean job's custody.json line 5 is `\"frozenAt\": \"...\"` — let me count: line 1 `{`, 2 `\"custody_inputs\": ...`, 3 `\"bar\": {`, 4 `\"file\": ...`, 5 `\"frozenAt\": ...`. Yes line 5. Cute but irrelevant. Use line 5.\n\nNow `events/record-missing-generation.json`: the clean record without `generations`.\n\n`relay/record.json`: clean + bar.approvals + relays.\n\n`shadow/record.json`: clean + s5_record, verdict, inputs.\n\nLet me write `relay/record.json` carefully:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"_type\": \"https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1\",\n  \"subject\": [],\n  \"predicateType\": \"https://evaluatordriven.com/custody/v1\",\n  \"predicate\": {\n    \"specVersion\": \"0.9.6\",\n    \"bar\": {\n      \"approvals\": [\n        {\"role\": \"relay\", \"by\": \"BuyerBot\", \"at\": \"2026-01-01T00:02:00Z\"}\n      ]\n    },\n    \"x-plumbline\": {\n      \"schema\": \"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1\",\n      \"time_tolerance_s\": 60,\n      \"times\": {\"startedAt\": \"hand-entered\", \"producedAt\": \"hand-entered\"},\n      \"hand_times\": [],\n      \"generations\": [],\n      \"evaluations\": [],\n      \"relays\": [\n        {\"event\": \"relay\", \"kind\": \"relay\", \"quote\": \"Ship it.\", \"from\": \"BuyerBot\", \"source_chat\": \"chat-1\",\n         \"source_at\": \"2026-01-01T00:02:00Z\", \"at\": \"2026-01-01T00:03:00Z\",\n         \"writer\": \"kit/custody_ledger.py@0123456789ab\", \"source\": \"inputs/custody-events.jsonl:2\"}\n      ],\n      \"amendments\": [],\n      \"spend\": {\"calls_sum_usd\": 0.0, \"key_delta_usd\": 0.0, \"total_usd\": 0.0, \"tolerance_usd\": 0.0001, \"source\": null}\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`ledger_array` output for the relay line: the line dict minus `_line` plus `source`. My record has exactly those keys. JSON key order doesn't matter for dict equality ✓.\n\nNow, the relay ledger line must match exactly: keys event, kind, quote, from, source_chat, source_at, at, writer. ✓\n\nNow the shadow record:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"_type\": \"https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1\",\n  \"subject\": [],\n  \"predicateType\": \"https://evaluatordriven.com/custody/v1\",\n  \"predicate\": {\n    \"specVersion\": \"0.9.6\",\n    \"x-plumbline\": {\n      \"schema\": \"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1\",\n      \"time_tolerance_s\": 60,\n      \"times\": {\"startedAt\": \"hand-entered\", \"producedAt\": \"hand-entered\"},\n      \"hand_times\": [],\n      \"generations\": [],\n      \"evaluations\": [],\n      \"relays\": [],\n      \"amendments\": [],\n      \"spend\": {\"calls_sum_usd\": 0.0, \"key_delta_usd\": 0.0, \"total_usd\": 0.0, \"tolerance_usd\": 0.0001, \"source\": null},\n      \"s5_record\": {\"name\": \"iteration-1-eval-record.json\"},\n      \"verdict\": \"accept\",\n      \"inputs\": {\"verdict\": \"accept\"}\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSchema: `s5_record` object ✓, `verdict` string ✓, `inputs` object ✓.\n\ncheck_shadow: derived verdict \"reject\" → shadow ✓.\n\nHmm wait — `check_shadow`'s s5 lookup: `s5 = xp.get(\"s5_record\") or {}`; `p = job / \"iteration-1-eval-record.json\"`; `rec = load(p)`; `if \"verdict\" in rec: derived[\"verdict\"] = rec.get(\"verdict\")` ✓.\n\nNow `shadow/iteration-1-eval-record.json`: `{\"verdict\": \"reject\"}`.\n\nNow, does `check_events` read `iteration-1-eval-record.json`? No.\n\nDoes `hand_time_set`? No.\n\nOK.\n\nNow let me double check the `events` record has no `generations` key → schema passes. But wait: `check_shadow` derived has generations; xp lacks → skipped. ✓ And `check_events`: `got = xp.get(\"generations\")` → None; `expected` = 2 entries → `None != expected` → events ✓.\n\nNow the tests' assertions. For events: `self.assertIn(\"events\", kinds(rv))`. Also assert rc 4.\n\nFor relay: `self.assertIn(\"relay-as-approval\", kinds(rv))`.\n\nFor shadow: `self.assertIn(\"shadow\", kinds(rv))`.\n\nFor spend: `self.assertEqual(kinds(rv)[0], \"spend\")` — order: hand checks (none), events (none), spend, shadow. First is spend ✓.\n\nWait — for `record-spend-off` on the clean job: hand checks: hand=[] and flags=[] → no fails. events: all arrays equal ✓. spend: fail. So kinds[0] == \"spend\" ✓.\n\nFor flag-without-source: hand checks first → kinds[0] == \"flag-without-source\" ✓.\n\nFor hand-time-unflagged: kinds[0] == \"hand-time-unflagged\" ✓.\n\nFor allowance: kinds[0] == \"allowance\" ✓.\n\nFor schema: check_schema returns failures → finish(4, failures) immediately, before other checks. kinds all \"schema\" ✓.\n\nFor standard: exit 3, kinds == [\"standard\"] ✓.\n\nClean: kinds == [] and rc 0 ✓.\n\nNow the close wiring test. Let me also double check `do_close`'s gate subprocess: `subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(gate), str(packet_path)], capture_output=True, text=True)` — gate is our stub exiting 0 ✓.\n\nNow — `do_close` calls `packet_path.write_text(...)` before the gate; then `rv.returncode != 0` → revert. Ours 0 → continue.\n\nThen prints packet line. Then `if code == 0:` (code from `call(\"plumbline.py\", ...)` monkeypatched to 0).\n\n`doc = json.loads((job/\"packet.json\").read_text())` — the rewritten packet. `disp = doc.get(\"disposition\")` → None → state \"not_yet_given\". `doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS` → \"accepted\" ✓.\n\n`cargs = [\"--job-dir\", str(job)]`; allow_hand_times True → append. `cc = call(\"emit_custody.py\", cargs)` → 0. Then `run_verifier(job, a)` → our tuple.\n\nFor (4, \"schema\"): `vc != 0` → print, `log_row(job, \"close_refused\", {\"kind\": kind, \"exit\": vc}, a.run_log)` → run_log written; return 4.\n\n`log_row` reads `path = Path(override)` = run_log; not a file → `{\"runs\": []}`; appends; writes. rows = [{\"job_id\": \"job\", \"date\": ..., \"event\": \"close_refused\", \"kind\": \"schema\", \"exit\": 4}] ✓.\n\nFor (0, \"verify\"): `log_row(job, \"closed\", {...})` → rows [{\"event\": \"closed\", ...}] → return code 0 ✓.\n\nGreat.\n\nOne catch: `do_close` prints with `print(...)`; and `log_row` prints. Fine.\n\nNow — `J.do_close` also references `TERMINALS` global ✓.\n\nLet me now write the final files. I'll aim for correctness and completeness.\n\nOne more thing: the `tests/test_plumbline_verify.py` at base — wait, is it possible that the base has a file with this exact name that must keep its base result? The listing \"Tests present now under tests/\" includes it, but the allowed paths say `new tests/test_plumbline_verify*.py`, so it's not a base file (it was added by iteration 1). Fine to rewrite.\n\nNow, `unittest` discovery: the file is `tests/test_plumbline_verify.py` — is it discovered? The base tests are run presumably via `python -m unittest discover` or a list. `validate_*` files are not test_*. The test_*.py are discovered. ✓\n\nNow writing.\n\nActually — one more consideration about `_validate` and the `$defs/xPlumbline` double validation in `check_schema`:\n\n```python\nfor p, _ in _validate(record, schema, schema, \"$\"):\n    fails.append(...)\n...\nfor p, _ in _validate(xp, schema[\"$defs\"][\"xPlumbline\"], schema, \"x-plumbline\"):\n```\n\nDuplicate failures (same field twice). Harmless. But for the unknown-key test, the first `_validate(record, ...)` already catches it at `$.predicate.x-plumbline.unknown_key`. Fine.\n\nHmm, wait: `_validate` on the record descends into `predicate` → `x-plumbline` → `$defs/xPlumbline` via `$ref` → checks additionalProperties ✓.\n\nNow `iter_xplumbline(record)` yields `(\"predicate.x-plumbline\", xp)` and nested ones. For `predicate.x-plumbline` we skip. Nested x-plumbline objects: in our fixtures there are none (the emitter writes `maker.x-plumbline`, `checks[].x-plumbline`, etc., which are objects). `xNested` requires object → passes for dicts. If a nested value is a string (emitter bug), fail. OK.\n\nNow let me write everything out.\n\nAlso, I should double check `main`'s handling when `--custody` points to a dir with no checker and no pin: exit 2. In the close-wiring test we monkeypatch run_verifier, so no issue.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\nFinal check on `check_shadow`'s use of `calls_by_seat` for `times`: with the T4 job (maker model + log), `mcalls` non-empty → \"harness log\" ✓.\n\nBut hmm — what if the maker is a model but `m.get(\"calls\")` filters to a subset; still non-empty ✓.\n\nWhat if the maker is a person (no log)? The emitter's `mk = {}` → \"hand-entered\" ✓.\n\nOK.\n\nLet me also make `read_calls` robust: `ci` may be `{}` → `evidence_dirs` handles.\n\nWriting now.\n\nFor `find_log` with a list of dirs and a pattern:\n\n```python\ndef find_log(job: Path, dirs: list[Path], rel: str | None, pattern: str | None):\n    if rel:\n        for base in dirs:\n            p = Path(base) / rel\n            if p.is_file():\n                return p\n        return None\n    hits: list[Path] = []\n    seen: set[str] = set()\n    for base in dirs:\n        if not Path(base).is_dir():\n            continue\n        for p in sorted(Path(base).glob(pattern)) if pattern else []:\n            k = str(p.resolve())\n            if k not in seen:\n                seen.add(k)\n                hits.append(p)\n    return hits[0] if len(hits) == 1 else None\n```\n\nHmm — `sorted(Path(base).glob(pattern)) if pattern else []` — precedence: `for p in (sorted(...) if pattern else [])`. Write it explicitly.\n\nNow `read_calls`:\n\n```python\ndef read_calls(job: Path, ev_dir: Path, ci: dict):\n    by: dict[str, dict] = {}\n    allc: list = []\n    dirs = evidence_dirs(job, ev_dir, ci)\n\n    def add(seat: str, log):\n        if not log:\n            return\n        try:\n            calls = H.read_log(Path(log), H.Refusals(), str(seat))\n        except Exception:\n            return\n        d = by.setdefault(str(seat), {})\n        for c in calls:\n            name = getattr(c, \"name\", None) or str(getattr(c, \"i\", \"\"))\n            d[name] = c\n            allc.append(c)\n\n    if isinstance(ci, dict):\n        m = ci.get(\"maker\") or {}\n        if m.get(\"kind\") == \"model\":\n            add(\"maker\", find_log(job, dirs, m.get(\"harness_log\"), \"harness-maker*.log\"))\n        for e in ci.get(\"evaluators\") or []:\n            if e.get(\"kind\") == \"model\":\n                rel = e.get(\"harness_log\") or next(\n                    (x.get(\"evidence_file\") for x in ci.get(\"external\") or []\n                     if x.get(\"subject\") == e.get(\"id\")), None)\n                add(str(e.get(\"id\")), find_log(job, dirs, rel, None))\n    if not allc:\n        for base in dirs:\n            if Path(base).is_dir():\n                for log in sorted(Path(base).glob(\"*.log\")):\n                    add(log.stem, log)\n    return by, allc\n```\n\nHmm — the fallback globbing every *.log when no calls found could pick up unrelated logs. But it's a fallback; keep (iteration 1 had it).\n\nCareful: the fallback loops over dirs including the job dir; a log at job root would be added. Fine.\n\nNow `hand_time_set`'s log scan: use `evidence_dirs`.\n\nNow, `main`: `calls_by_seat, all_calls = read_calls(job, evidence, ci)`.\n\nLet me write the final verifier 