yer approval to custody.json too. Let me design the test to add to the ledger (simpler) since `custody_ledger.py approve` writes to both. But the test builds the job in a temp dir from code; it can append a ledger line directly or run the ledger. Let me append directly for simplicity, or run the ledger.\n\nActually, the cleanest test: build a minimal job in a temp dir. But the emitter needs a lot of files (packet, custody.json, bar, evidence logs, etc.) to not gap. Hmm, the test can copy `job-rejected` and then modify it: remove the buyer approval from `bar.approvals`, append a relay line, and remove any buyer approve line from the ledger. Then assert refusal. Then append a buyer approve line and assert no refusal.\n\nWait, but if I remove the buyer from `bar.approvals`, the `approvals` loop won't process it, and `check_approval` won't run for buyer. But `bar.approvals[1]` is referenced in other tests; here I'm making a copy so it's fine.\n\nBut removing the buyer from `bar.approvals` — how do I know which index is buyer? I can filter by `role == \"buyer\"`.\n\nLet me write the test:\n```python\ndef test_relay_only_buyer_approval_refused(self):\n    # build in a temp dir from job-rejected: drop the buyer approval, add a relay line\n    job = self._relay_only_job()\n    rv = run_emit(job, self.tmp / \"relay-only.json\", allow_hand=True)\n    self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 3, rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n    self.assertIn(\"approval\", rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n    self.assertIn(\"buyer\", rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n    self.assertFalse((self.tmp / \"relay-only.json\").exists())\n    # adding a direct buyer approve line removes the refusal\n    append buyer approve line...\n    rv2 = run_emit(job, self.tmp / \"relay-only-ok.json\", allow_hand=True)\n    self.assertEqual(rv2.returncode, 0, rv2.stdout + rv2.stderr)\n```\n\nBut this requires the emitter to run from a clean git checkout (the emitter refuses dirty trees). The test in `validate_schemas.py` uses `clean_checkout`. In `test_plumbline_verify.py`, the `CloseWiringTests` mocks `J.call` and `J.run_verifier`. Hmm.\n\nActually, running the real emitter in a unit test is heavy (it needs a clean git checkout, harness logs, etc.). The `validate_schemas.py` does it via `SNAP = clean_checkout(...)`. \n\nFor `test_plumbline_verify.py`, I could test the emitter's `relay_only_buyer` logic at the unit level (import `emit_custody` and call the function). That's simpler and doesn't need a full job. But the feedback says \"a test builds, in a temporary directory, a job whose specialist has a direct `approve` ledger line and whose buyer appears only in a `relay` line; the emitter exits with the same non-zero code as its other refusals\".\n\nSo the test should run the actual emitter. Hmm. But that requires the clean checkout dance.\n\nAlternatively, I can put the F1f test in `tests/validate_schemas.py` (which already has the `clean_checkout` infrastructure) rather than `test_plumbline_verify.py`. The allowed paths include `tests/validate_schemas.py`. And the feedback item 5 is about the emitter, which `validate_schemas.py` tests.\n\nActually, the feedback says \"Tests\" in item 7 is about the verifier tests. Item 5 doesn't say where the test goes. Let me put the F1f test in `tests/validate_schemas.py` where the emitter tests live. That's cleaner and uses the existing infrastructure.\n\nSimilarly, item 4's \"Add a test where the two fields differ\" — I can add to `validate_schemas.py` (emitter spend) or `test_plumbline_verify.py` (verifier spend). Let me add a unit test to `test_plumbline_verify.py` for the verifier's `call_cost`, and maybe a `validate_schemas.py` case for the emitter's spend. Actually, a simple unit test importing both `call_cost` functions is cleanest.\n\nItem 6: `read_log` must accept str or Path. Wrap `Path(path)`. Add a test that calls `read_log` with a str path. Put in `test_custody_ledger.py` (it imports `H`).\n\nItem 7: Tests. The verifier tests must run against a stand-in checker that exits non-zero unless `--bar` is given. Write that stand-in from code in the test file into the temp checkout. When `CUSTODY_DIR` is set, also run the clean case against its real checker.\n\nSo in `tests/test_plumbline_verify.py`, replace the `checkout` helper's checker copy with a stand-in written from code. The stand-in:\n```python\nCHECKER = '''#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport sys\nargv = sys.argv[1:]\nif \"--bar\" not in argv:\n    print(\"checker: --bar is required\", file=sys.stderr)\n    sys.exit(2)\n# read the record and do the minimal standard check the fixture expects\n...\n'''\n```\n\nBut the checker must accept valid records and reject the bad-subject record (V1 test: `record-bad-subject.json` → exit 3). The existing stand-in at `FIX / \"checkout\" / \"checker\" / \"check.py\"` presumably does a simple digest check. Since I can't modify fixtures, I need to write a stand-in from code that reproduces the behavior the tests expect:\n- Accept `clean/record.custody.json` (exit 0).\n- Reject `clean/record-bad-subject.json` (exit non-zero) — this record has an altered subject digest.\n- Require `--bar`.\n\nLet me think about what the existing stand-in does. It's at `tests/fixtures/custody/verify/checkout/checker/check.py`. I can't read it, but from the test:\n```python\ndef test_standard_failure_exits_3(self):\n    rv = run_verify(record(\"clean\", \"record-bad-subject.json\"), self.job(), self.custody())\n    self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 3, rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n    self.assertEqual(kinds(rv), [\"standard\"])\n```\nSo the stand-in rejects `record-bad-subject.json`. The difference is the subject digest.\n\nI'll write a stand-in that:\n1. Requires `--bar`.\n2. Parses `--files FILES` and checks that each subject in the record matches a file's sha256 in the files dir. If mismatch → exit 1.\n\nLet me look at the record structure. `subject` is a list of `{name, digest: {sha256}}`. The files dir is `custody/files`. So:\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport json, sys\nfrom hashlib import sha256\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef arg(name):\n    a = sys.argv[1:]\n    if name in a:\n        i = a.index(name)\n        return a[i+1] if i+1 < len(a) else None\n    return None\n\nrec = Path(sys.argv[1])\nfiles = arg(\"--files\")\nbar = arg(\"--bar\")\nif not bar:\n    print(\"checker: --bar is required\", file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(2)\ndoc = json.loads(rec.read_text())\nfor s in doc.get(\"subject\") or []:\n    name = s.get(\"name\")\n    want = (s.get(\"digest\") or {}).get(\"sha256\")\n    p = Path(files) / name if files else None\n    if not p or not p.is_file():\n        print(f\"checker: missing file {name}\", file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(1)\n    got = sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    if got != want:\n        print(f\"checker: subject digest mismatch for {name}\", file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(1)\nsys.exit(0)\n```\n\nThis accepts the clean record and rejects the bad-subject record (if the subject digest was altered). And it requires `--bar`.\n\nBut wait, does the clean record's subject match the files? The clean job has `custody/files/artifact.md` (from the `need` set in validate_schemas). And the record's subject names `artifact.md` with its sha256. So the stand-in passes. For `record-bad-subject.json`, the subject digest is altered → mismatch → exit 1. Good.\n\nNow, the `checkout` helper currently copies the fixture checker. I'll change it to write the stand-in. But I must not modify fixtures. Writing from code in the test file is allowed.\n\nLet me rewrite:\n```python\nSTAND_IN_CHECKER = '''#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport json, sys\nfrom hashlib import sha256\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef arg(name):\n    a = sys.argv[1:]\n    return a[a.index(name) + 1] if name in a and a.index(name) + 1 < len(a) else None\n\nrec = Path(sys.argv[1])\nfiles = arg(\"--files\")\nbar = arg(\"--bar\")\nif not bar:\n    print(\"checker: --bar is required\", file=sys.stderr)\n    sys.exit(2)\ndoc = json.loads(rec.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\nfor s in doc.get(\"subject\") or []:\n    name = s.get(\"name\")\n    want = (s.get(\"digest\") or {}).get(\"sha256\")\n    p = Path(files) / name if files else None\n    if p is None or not p.is_file():\n        print(f\"checker: missing file {name}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        sys.exit(1)\n    got = sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    if got != want:\n        print(f\"checker: subject digest mismatch for {name}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        sys.exit(1)\nsys.exit(0)\n'''\n```\n\nAnd `checkout`:\n```python\ndef checkout(tmp, name=\"checkout\", wrong_pin=False):\n    c = tmp / name\n    (c / \"checker\").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    (c / \"tools\").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    (c / \"checker\" / \"check.py\").write_text(STAND_IN_CHECKER, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    pin = \"0\" * 64 if wrong_pin else sha256((c / \"checker\" / \"check.py\").read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    (c / \"tools\" / \"pin.json\").write_text(json.dumps({\"checker/check.py\": pin}) + \"\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    return c\n```\n\nNow, the verifier's `standard_check` must pass `--bar`. The bar path is `bar.file` from custody.json, relative to job, default `custody/bar.md`. For the clean job, `custody/bar.md` exists (from the `need` set `bar/bar.md`? no, that's for the bundle). Let me check: the clean job's `custody/files/artifact.md` and `custody/bar.md`? The bundle `need` set includes `bar/bar.md`, so the bundle packs `bar.md` under `bar/`. The actual file is `custody/bar.md`. So the default works.\n\nBut the custody.json might have `bar.file` set to something else. Let me default to `custody/bar.md` and use `bar.file` if present.\n\nNow, for the `record-bad-subject.json` test, the verifier passes `--bar`, the stand-in accepts the bar (exists), then checks the subject digest → mismatch → exit 1 → verifier exit 3. Good.\n\nFor the wrong-checker-bytes test: `checkout(wrong_pin=True)` pins `\"0\"*64`, so `file_sha(check) != pin` → exit 2. Good. And the exit-2 message must not start with `FAIL `. Let me make it `NOT CHECKABLE:`.\n\nNow, `CUSTODY_DIR` real checker test. When `CUSTODY_DIR` is set and names a custody checkout with `checker/check.py`, run the clean case against it. But the real checker needs the real pin and a real bar. Hmm, the real checker's sha256 must equal the pin in the checkout's `tools/pin.json`. If I use `CUSTODY_DIR` directly as `--custody`, then `_find_checker` finds `CUSTODY_DIR/checker/check.py`, and `find_pin` reads `CUSTODY_DIR/tools/pin.json` (if present) or the repo's. The real checker's sha should match the repo's pin (`aaeb7e05...`). But the clean record must be valid under the real checker, which requires the real bar and files. Hmm, this is uncertain. Let me make this test conditional and skip if the real checker rejects, or just run it and check it doesn't exit 2 (checker pin OK). Actually the feedback says \"also run the clean case against its real checker/check.py\". Let me just run it and assert the result is not exit 2 (the checker ran). But that's weak. Let me assert exit 0 if the record is valid. Hmm, risk.\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"When the environment variable CUSTODY_DIR names a custody checkout, also run the clean case against its real checker/check.py.\"\n\nI'll write:\n```python\n@unittest.skipUnless(os.environ.get(\"CUSTODY_DIR\") and (Path(os.environ[\"CUSTODY_DIR\"]) / \"checker\" / \"check.py\").is_file(),\n                     \"CUSTODY_DIR not set to a custody checkout\")\ndef test_clean_against_real_checker(self):\n    rv = run_verify(record(\"clean\", \"record.custody.json\"), self.job(), Path(os.environ[\"CUSTODY_DIR\"]))\n    self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 0, rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n```\n\nBut this might fail if the clean record isn't valid under the real checker. The clean fixture is presumably built to be valid. Let me keep it but it's skipped in normal runs. Actually, `os` needs importing.\n\nHmm, wait. If the clean record has a `x-plumbline` block, the real