hecker pinned. In `validate_schemas.py`, there's no stand-in checker. So running the verifier there would fail exit 2 (no checker). Unless `CUSTODY_DIR` is set.\n\nHmm. This is a problem. The item 2 test needs a checker. In `test_plumbline_verify.py`, we have the stand-in checker. But the stand-in doesn't validate the record's predicate deeply — it only checks subject digests and `--bar`. So it would accept the emitted job-rejected record (subject digests match). Then the verifier runs the Plumbline layer, which recomputes hand times and compares. If they match, exit 0. So the item 2 test can go in `test_plumbline_verify.py` using the stand-in checker, and it needs to run the emitter.\n\nBut running the emitter requires a clean git checkout (the emitter refuses dirty trees). In `test_plumbline_verify.py`, I'd need to build a clean checkout. That's heavy but doable (copy the repo, git init, commit). Actually, the `validate_schemas.py` `clean_checkout` does exactly this.\n\nAlternatively, I can bypass the emitter's revision check by... no, I shouldn't change the emitter's behavior.\n\nHmm. Let me think about whether I can run the emitter in a way that passes the revision check. The emitter checks `git status --porcelain` for changes outside `dot-plumbline/` and the job dir. If the test runs from the real repo (which is dirty due to the test's temp files?), it might refuse. The `validate_schemas.py` solves this with a clean checkout.\n\nLet me move the clean-checkout helper into `test_plumbline_verify.py`? That's a lot of duplication. Or I can put the item-2 test in `validate_schemas.py` and use a stand-in checker there too.\n\nActually, `validate_schemas.py` already has the infrastructure for emitting. Let me add a small stand-in checker in `validate_schemas.py` for the verifier round-trip. The stand-in can be written to a temp dir and pinned. Then run `SNAP / \"kit\" / \"plumbline_verify.py\" --custody <temp-checkout>`.\n\nWait, but the verifier's `_find_checker` looks for `checker/check.py`, `check.py`, `tools/checker/check.py` under `--custody`. And `find_pin` looks for `tools/pin.json`. So I can create a temp checkout with the stand-in checker and a pin. Then run the verifier with `--custody <temp-checkout>`.\n\nLet me add to `validate_schemas.py`:\n```python\n# ---- V1/V3/T2: every record the emitter writes verifies with exit 0, and every flagged time appears in hand_times ----\ndef stand_in_checkout(name=\"verify-checkout\"):\n    d = TMP / name\n    (d / \"checker\").mkdir(parents=True)\n    (d / \"tools\").mkdir(parents=True)\n    checker = d / \"checker\" / \"check.py\"\n    checker.write_text(\n        \"import json, sys\\n\"\n        \"from hashlib import sha256\\n\"\n        \"from pathlib import Path\\n\"\n        \"def arg(n):\\n\"\n        \"    a = sys.argv[1:]\\n\"\n        \"    return a[a.index(n)+1] if n in a and a.index(n)+1 < len(a) else None\\n\"\n        \"if not arg('--bar'):\\n\"\n        \"    print('checker: --bar required', file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(2)\\n\"\n        \"rec = Path(sys.argv[1]); files = arg('--files')\\n\"\n        \"doc = json.loads(rec.read_text())\\n\"\n        \"for s in doc.get('subject') or []:\\n\"\n        \"    p = Path(files) / s['name'] if files else None\\n\"\n        \"    if not p or not p.is_file() or sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest() != (s.get('digest') or {}).get('sha256'):\\n\"\n        \"        print('checker: subject mismatch', file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(1)\\n\"\n        \"sys.exit(0)\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    (d / \"tools\" / \"pin.json\").write_text(json.dumps({\"checker/check.py\": hashlib.sha256(checker.read_bytes()).hexdigest()}) + \"\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    return d\n\n\ndef verify_record(rec, job, co, out=None):\n    cmd = [VENV_PY, str(SNAP / \"kit\" / \"plumbline_verify.py\"),\n           \"--record\", str(rec), \"--files\", str(job / \"custody\" / \"files\"),\n           \"--evidence\", str(job / \"custody\" / \"evidence\"),\n           \"--ledger\", str(job / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\"),\n           \"--custody\", str(co)]\n    if out:\n        cmd += [\"--json\", str(out)]\n    r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)\n    return r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr\n\n\nVCO = stand_in_checkout()\nrec_ok = TMP / \"roundtrip.custody.json\"\nrc, out = emit(FIX, rec_ok, allow_hand=True)\nvrc, vout = verify_record(rec_ok, FIX, VCO)\nrx = json.loads(rec_ok.read_text())[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"] if rec_ok.is_file() else {}\nres((f\"{S} · T2/V3 a job emitted with --allow-hand-times verifies with exit 0 (no flag-without-source/shadow)\",\n     rc == 0 and vrc == 0 and \"flag-without-source\" not in vout and \"shadow\" not in vout,\n     f\"emit {rc}, verify {vrc}: {vout.strip()[-140:]}\"))\nres((f\"{S} · T3 every hand time the emitter flagged is in x-plumbline.hand_times with file:line\",\n     all(e.get(\"field\") and e.get(\"file\") and e.get(\"line\") is not None for e in (rx.get(\"hand_times\") or []))\n     and bool(rx.get(\"hand_times\")), json.dumps((rx.get(\"hand_times\") or [])[:2])[:160]))\n```\n\nBut wait — `verify_record` runs the verifier from `SNAP`, which requires the emitter's `revision` check? No, the verifier doesn't check revisions. It reads the record and runs the checker. Good.\n\nBut the verifier's `job` determination: `job = ledger.parent.parent if ledger.parent.name == \"inputs\" else files`. For `FIX`, the ledger is `FIX / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\"`, so `job = FIX`. Good. Then `custody_json = FIX / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\"`. Good.\n\nNow, the verifier's `hand_time_set` recomputes. It needs to match the emitter's `hand_times`. Let me think about potential mismatches:\n1. The emitter flags evaluator calls' `.sent_at`/`.received_at` from `hand_time_entries()`. The verifier does the same via its log loop. Should match.\n2. The emitter flags `disposition.at` from `inputs-no-ledger`. The verifier does the same. Should match.\n3. The emitter might flag `passes.<id>.at` or `controls[<id>].at` from `inputs-no-ledger` if typed. Does `job-rejected` have typed pass/control `at`? From the E2 test, the base fixture does NOT (the test adds it). So no.\n4. `startedAt`/`producedAt`: the emitter marks from `inputs-no-ledger` only if no maker history. `job-rejected` has a maker log, so `mk.get(\"startedAt\")` is set. The verifier marks from `inputs-no-ledger` only if `not mcalls`. `mcalls` is truthy. So neither marks. Match.\n5. Ledger hand lines: `job-rejected`'s ledger has a pin line (tool writer). No hand lines. So neither marks. Match.\n\nWait, but the emitter's `hand` dict also includes `routing.<seat>.declaredAt` from the pin line if the writer is a hand. The pin line has a tool writer, so `writer_source` returns None → not marked. Match.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: the emitter marks `routing.<seat>.declaredAt` for `ev_log[\"pin\"]` seats. The verifier marks `routing.<seat>.declaredAt` for pin lines with `seat in seats`. Both only if `writer_source` is not None. Match.\n\nOK, but there's another subtlety. The verifier's `hand_time_set` iterates over ledger lines and for `approve` events, it checks `ci[\"bar\"][\"approvals\"]` by role. If the ledger has an `approve` line for a role not in `bar.approvals`, it's skipped. For `job-rejected`, the ledger might not have `approve` lines at all (only a pin). So no issue.\n\nBut wait — the emitter's `approvals` loop marks `bar.approvals[i].at` from `inputs-no-ledger` if there's no live approve line. For `job-rejected`, `bar.approvals` has buyer and specialist entries with typed `at`. And the ledger has no approve lines. So the emitter marks BOTH `bar.approvals[0].at` and `bar.approvals[1].at` as `inputs-no-ledger`. The verifier's `hand_time_set`:\n```python\n    for i, a in enumerate(bar.get(\"approvals\") or []):\n        if (\"approve\", a.get(\"role\")) not in seen and a.get(\"at\"):\n            mark(f\"bar.approvals[{i}].at\", inputs_rel, line_of(custody_path, a.get(\"at\"), \"at\"),\n                 \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n```\n`seen` is populated from ledger `approve` events. If no approve lines, `seen` is empty, so both are marked. Match. Good.\n\nSo the hand-time sets should match. \n\nBut wait — `line_of` vs `line_in`. The verifier uses `line_of(custody_path, value, key)`, the emitter uses `line_in(inputs_path, value, key)`. Both find the first line containing the value under the key. Should match. But `line_of` falls back to no-key search if no key match. Same as `line_in`. OK.\n\nNow, `check_shadow` also compares `hand_times`. It uses `hand_key` = (field, file, line, source). If the line numbers match, OK. They should.\n\nHmm, but the emitter's `line_in` returns `None` if not found. The verifier's `line_of` returns `None` if not found. So both None → match.\n\nOK, I'm fairly confident. But let me double-check one thing: the verifier's `hand_time_set` calls `read_calls(job, evidence, ci)` at the start:\n```python\n    calls_by_seat, _ = read_calls(job, evidence, ci)\n```\nAnd `read_calls` populates `by` from logs. But the hand-time loop for calls uses a separate glob:\n```python\n    logs: list[Path] = []\n    for base in evidence_dirs(job, evidence, ci):\n        if Path(base).is_dir():\n            logs += sorted(Path(base).glob(\"*.log\"))\n    for log in dict.fromkeys(logs):\n        try:\n            calls = H.read_log(Path(log), H.Refusals(), \"verify\")\n        ...\n```\n\n`evidence_dirs` includes `Path(ev)` (the `--evidence` arg), `Path(job) / rel`, `Path(job)`. For the round-trip test, `--evidence` is `FIX / \"custody\" / \"evidence\"`. So `evidence_dirs` = [`FIX/custody/evidence`, `FIX/custody/evidence`, `FIX`]. The glob picks up `*.log` in `FIX/custody/evidence` and `FIX`. If `FIX` has stray `.log` files, they'd be read. Probably not. OK.\n\nBut the emitter only reads the specific logs named in the inputs (maker harness_log, evaluator harness_log). The verifier globs ALL `*.log` in evidence dirs. If there's