tries:\n            if isinstance(e, dict) and e.get(\"field\"):\n                hand[str(e[\"field\"])] = e\n```\n\nBut `read_calls` might not read all logs if the inputs don't name them. For the verifier to be robust, maybe it should read all logs. Hmm.\n\nActually, the feedback item 2 test specifically says \"it must exit 0, with no flag-without-source or shadow finding\". If the verifier globs extra logs, it might flag extra hand times → `hand-time-unflagged` (not `flag-without-source`). The test says no `flag-without-source` or `shadow`. It doesn't mention `hand-time-unflagged`. But exit 0 requires no failures at all. So extra flags would cause `hand-time-unflagged` → exit 4. Fail.\n\nTo be safe, let me make the verifier's call-hand-time loop use `read_calls`' `all_calls` (the same logs the emitter reads). But then if the emitter reads a log the verifier doesn't, `flag-without-source`. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me think about what logs the emitter reads vs the verifier's `read_calls`. `read_calls` in the verifier:\n```python\n    if isinstance(ci, dict):\n        m = ci.get(\"maker\") or {}\n        if m.get(\"kind\") == \"model\":\n            add(\"maker\", find_log(job, dirs, m.get(\"harness_log\"), \"harness-maker*.log\"))\n        for e in ci.get(\"evaluators\") or []:\n            if e.get(\"kind\") == \"model\":\n                rel = e.get(\"harness_log\") or next(\n                    (x.get(\"evidence_file\") for x in ci.get(\"external\") or []\n                     if x.get(\"subject\") == e.get(\"id\")), None)\n                add(str(e.get(\"id\")), find_log(job, dirs, rel, None))\n```\n\nThe emitter:\n- `maker_history` finds `m.get(\"harness_log\")` or `harness-maker*.log`.\n- `evaluator_history` finds `e.get(\"harness_log\")` or `external` evidence_file.\n\nSame logic. So `read_calls` reads the same logs as the emitter. So using `all_calls` for the hand-time loop would match the emitter's `flag_calls` calls. Let me do that.\n\nBut wait, the emitter's `flag_calls` is called with `log_calls` (all calls in the log, before filtering by `at`). `read_calls` adds all calls from `read_log`. So `all_calls` = all calls from the same logs. Match. Good.\n\nSo change the verifier's call-hand-time loop:\n```python\n    _, all_calls = read_calls(job, evidence, ci)\n    for c in all_calls:\n        try:\n            entries = c.hand_time_entries()\n        except Exception:\n            entries = []\n        for e in entries:\n            if isinstance(e, dict) and e.get(\"field\"):\n                hand[str(e[\"field\"])] = e\n```\n\nAnd remove the separate glob. But `hand_time_set` already calls `read_calls` at the top:\n```python\n    calls_by_seat, _ = read_calls(job, evidence, ci)\n```\nChange to:\n```python\n    calls_by_seat, all_calls = read_calls(job, evidence, ci)\n```\nAnd use `all_calls` in the loop.\n\nThis is cleaner and matches the emitter. Good.\n\nBut wait, does this weaken the verifier (V3 wants it to recompute independently)? It still recomputes from the logs; it just uses the same log-finding logic. That's fine — the independent part is the hand-time rule, not the log discovery. And V7 says the verifier must not import the emitter. It doesn't. Using `read_calls` (its own function) is fine.\n\nOK. Let me also double check the `disposition.at` line. The emitter marks `disposition.at` from `inputs-no-ledger` if no live dispose line. The verifier does the same. Match.\n\nNow, one more: the emitter's `hand` includes `bar.frozenAt` from `inputs-no-ledger` if no freeze line. For `job-rejected`, is there a freeze line? The base fixture's ledger has a pin line (from iteration 2) but maybe no freeze. If no freeze, the emitter marks `bar.frozenAt` from `inputs-no-ledger`. The verifier:\n```python\n    if \"freeze\" not in seen and bar.get(\"frozenAt\"):\n        mark(\"bar.frozenAt\", inputs_rel, line_of(custody_path, bar.get(\"frozenAt\"), \"frozenAt\"),\n             \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n```\nSame. Match.\n\nOK. I'm fairly confident now.\n\nNow let me also handle the `check_hand_times` when `xp.get(\"hand_times\")` is not a list. Currently returns `fails` (empty) — meaning if `hand_times` is a string, no hand-time failure. But the schema check (V2) catches `hand_times` as a string → kind `schema`. So that's fine.\n\nNow item 3: exit 2 message. Let me also make sure the exit-2 cases for missing record/ledger/custody print `NOT CHECKABLE:`. Actually, the bar says exit 2 for \"usage, a missing input, or a checker whose sha256 is not the pin\". So all exit-2 should be `NOT CHECKABLE:`. Let me update `finish`.\n\nWait, but the JSON output for exit 2 still has `{\"exit\": 2, \"failures\": [...]}`. Fine.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider `standard_check` return for missing checker/pin/wrong bytes. Currently returns `2, None`. Main:\n```python\n    code, _ = standard_check(record, files, evidence, custody)\n    if code == 2:\n        return finish(2, [{\"kind\": \"standard\", \"field\": \"checker\", \"file\": str(custody), \"line\": 0}], a.json)\n```\nChange field to \"pinned checker\" and file to the checker path if known. Let me return the checker path from `standard_check`:\n```python\ndef standard_check(...):\n    check = _find_checker(custody)\n    if check is None:\n        return 2, None, \"checker not found\"\n    pin = find_pin(custody, check)\n    if pin is None:\n        return 2, None, \"checker not pinned\"\n    if file_sha(check) != pin:\n        return 2, None, \"checker bytes not the pin\"\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, this changes the signature. Let me keep it simpler: return `(code, rv, reason)`.\n\nActually, let me just make `standard_check` return `(code, rv, where)` where `where` is a string for the exit-2 message.\n\n```python\ndef standard_check(record, files, evidence, custody, bar):\n    check = _find_checker(custody)\n    if check is None:\n        return 2, None, f\"checker/check.py not found under {custody}\"\n    pin = find_pin(custody, check)\n    if pin is None:\n        return 2, None, f\"no pin for {check}\"\n    if file_sha(check) != pin:\n        return 2, None, f\"pinned checker {check} bytes are not the pin\"\n    cmd = [sys.executable, str(check), str(record), \"--files\", str(files), \"--evidence\", str(evidence)]\n    if bar is not None:\n        cmd += [\"--bar\", str(bar)]\n    rv = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)\n    return (0 if rv.returncode == 0 else 1), rv, \"\"\n```\n\nMain:\n```python\n    code, _, why = standard_check(record, files, evidence, custody, bar)\n    if code == 2:\n        return finish(2, [{\"kind\": \"standard\", \"field\": \"pinned checker\", \"file\": str(custody), \"line\": 0,\n                           \"message\": why}], a.json)\n```\n\nAnd `finish` prints `NOT CHECKABLE: pinned checker <custody>:0 — <message>`. Hmm, let me include the message. Actually, the format `FAIL <kind> <field> <file>:<line>` doesn't have a message slot. For `NOT CHECKABLE`, I can add a message. Let me keep it simple:\n```python\ndef finish(code, failures, json_out):\n    for f in failures:\n        if code == 2:\n            msg = f.get(\"message\") or f.get(\"field\")\n            print(f\"NOT CHECKABLE: {msg} ({f.get('file','')}:{f.get('line',0)})\")\n        else:\n            print(f\"FAIL {f.get('kind')} {f.get('field')} {f.get('file','')}:{f.get('line',0)}\")\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, the test checks `kinds(rv)` = lines starting with `FAIL `. For exit 2, there should be no `FAIL ` lines. `NOT CHECKABLE:` doesn't start with `FAIL `. Good.\n\nNow, item 4's test \"where the two fields differ\". Let me add a unit test in `test_plumbline_verify.py`:\n```python\ndef test_call_cost_prefers_generation_total(self):\n    raw = {\"request\": {\"url\": \"https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions\"},\n           \"response\": {\"usage\": {\"cost\": 0.05}},\n           \"generation\": {\"data\": {\"total_cost\": 0.07}}}\n    c = H.Call(\"harness.log\", 0, raw, [])\n    self.assertAlmostEqual(E.cal