ot None:\n        data = gen.get(\"data\") if isinstance(gen.get(\"data\"), dict) else gen\n        v = data.get(\"total_cost\")\n        if isinstance(v, (int, float)) and not isinstance(v, bool):\n            return float(v)\n    resp = raw.get(\"response\") if isinstance(raw.get(\"response\"), dict) else {}\n    usage = resp.get(\"usage\") if isinstance(resp.get(\"usage\"), dict) else {}\n    v = usage.get(\"cost\")\n    if isinstance(v, (int, float)) and not isinstance(v, bool):\n        return float(v)\n    return 0.0\n```\n\nWait, the feedback says: \"the log entry's `generation.data.total_cost` where present, not the `usage.cost` of the chat reply. Where a call's generation is absent (null) or has no `total_cost`, fall back to the chat reply's `usage.cost`\".\n\nSo exactly: if generation present and `data.total_cost` present, use it. Else use `response.usage.cost`. Don't fall back to `usage.total_cost` or `response.total_cost`.\n\nLet me write:\n```python\ndef call_cost(c) -> float:\n    raw = c.raw\n    gen = raw.get(\"generation\") if isinstance(raw.get(\"generation\"), dict) else None\n    if gen is not None:\n        data = gen.get(\"data\") if isinstance(gen.get(\"data\"), dict) else gen\n        v = data.get(\"total_cost\") if isinstance(data, dict) else None\n        if isinstance(v, (int, float)) and not isinstance(v, bool):\n            return float(v)\n    resp = raw.get(\"response\") if isinstance(raw.get(\"response\"), dict) else {}\n    usage = resp.get(\"usage\") if isinstance(resp.get(\"usage\"), dict) else {}\n    v = usage.get(\"cost\") if isinstance(usage, dict) else None\n    if isinstance(v, (int, float)) and not isinstance(v, bool):\n        return float(v)\n    return 0.0\n```\n\nSame for the verifier's `call_cost`.\n\nNow, the spend block in the emitter:\n```python\ndef spend_block(lines: list[tuple[int, dict]], calls) -> dict:\n    sp = None\n    for _, e in lines:\n        if e.get(\"event\") == \"spend\":\n            sp = e\n    calls_sum = round(sum(call_cost(c) for c in calls), 6)\n    ...\n```\n\nUses `call_cost`. Good.\n\nThe verifier's `compute_spend_block`:\n```python\ndef compute_spend_block(lines, all_calls, ledger_rel):\n    sp = last_spend_line(lines)\n    calls_sum = round(sum(call_cost(c) for c in all_calls if getattr(c, \"at\", None)), 6)\n    ...\n```\n\nUses `call_cost`. Good.\n\nWait, the verifier filters `if getattr(c, \"at\", None)`. The emitter sums over all `calls` passed in (which are `all_calls = mk._calls + ev_calls`). Hmm, does the emitter filter calls with `at`? Let me check. In the emitter's `build`:\n```python\n    all_calls = list(mk.get(\"_calls\") or [])\n    for d in ev_calls.values():\n        all_calls += list(d.values())\n```\n\nAnd `mk[\"_calls\"] = calls` where `calls = [c for c in calls if c.at]`. And `ev_calls` — from `evaluator_history`, `named` = all calls (with `at`? no, `calls = [c for c in log_calls if c.at]`). So all calls in `all_calls` have `at`. So the filter doesn't matter. But the verifier adds the filter. Since the verifier's `all_calls` comes from `read_calls` which includes all calls (even no `at`), the filter matters for the verifier. But for a successfully emitted record, all calls have `at`, so sum matches. OK.\n\nHmm wait, actually the emitter's `all_calls` includes `ev_calls` which is `eh.get(\"calls\", {})` = `named` which is built from `calls` (filtered by `at`). And `mk._calls` = filtered by `at`. So all have `at`. The verifier's `read_calls` `allc` includes all calls from `read_log` (not filtered by `at`). So the verifier's sum could include calls with no `at` (cost 0 anyway since no generation/usage?). Actually a call with no `at` might still have usage. But if it has no `at`, `read_log` added a refusal, but the verifier ignores refusals. Hmm. But again, for a successfully emitted record, all calls have `at`. So OK.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. There's a potential mismatch: the verifier's `all_calls` might include calls from logs