efusal is gone.\n\nHmm, but if `bar.approvals` has no buyer entry, the emitter's `approvals` list won't include the buyer, so how does the buyer approve line matter? It matters for my rule: \"no `approve` ledger line with role buyer\". If there IS such a line, then not relay-only → no refusal.\n\nBut then the record's `bar.approvals` would still only have specialist. That's a bit odd but acceptable for the test (the test only checks no refusal).\n\nWait, but actually, maybe the `bar.approvals` in custody.json for the F1f test should include a buyer entry that is relay-backed? Let me re-read: \"whose buyer appears only in a `relay` line\". So the buyer appears in the relay, not in `bar.approvals`. So `bar.approvals` has no buyer entry.\n\nHmm, but then \"A second test adds a direct buyer `approve` line\" — adding an approve line doesn't add to `bar.approvals` in custody.json (the ledger is separate). Unless the ledger approve line is what the emitter reads.\n\nWait — the emitter reads `ev_log[\"approve\"]` (live ledger approve lines) and cross-checks with `bar.approvals`. But it only builds `approvals` from `b.get(\"approvals\")`. So a ledger approve line for buyer that's not in `bar.approvals` is ignored for the record.\n\nSo for the second test, adding a buyer approve line: my rule sees a direct buyer approval in the ledger → no refusal. The record still lacks buyer in `bar.approvals`, but the test only checks no refusal.\n\nActually, maybe I should make the check consider BOTH sources: `ci[\"bar\"][\"approvals\"]` roles AND ledger `approve` roles. If either has buyer → direct. Good.\n\nHmm, but this is a bit awkward. Let me reconsider: maybe the F1f test job has `bar.approvals` with a buyer entry whose `at`/`by` came from the relay? No, \"no buyer approval typed into inputs/custody.json\".\n\nOK let me just go with:\n```python\ndef relay_only_buyer(ci, packet, lines, have_inputs) -> bool:\n    # a buyer approval is relay-only when a relay line exists and no direct