t(\"at\"), \"at\"),\n                 \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n```\n`seen` is populated from ledger `approve` events. If no approve lines, `seen` is empty, so both are marked. Match. Good.\n\nSo the hand-time sets should match. \n\nBut wait — `line_of` vs `line_in`. The verifier uses `line_of(custody_path, value, key)`, the emitter uses `line_in(inputs_path, value, key)`. Both find the first line containing the value under the key. Should match. But `line_of` falls back to no-key search if no key match. Same as `line_in`. OK.\n\nNow, `check_shadow` also compares `hand_times`. It uses `hand_key` = (field, file, line, source). If the line numbers match, OK. They should.\n\nHmm, but the emitter's `line_in` returns `None` if not found. The verifier's `line_of` returns `None` if not found. So both None → match.\n\nOK, I'm fairly confident. But let me double-check one thing: the verifier's `hand_time_set` calls `read_calls(job, evidence, ci)` at the start:\n```python\n    calls_by_seat, _ = read_calls(job, evidence, ci)\n```\nAnd `read_calls` populates `by` from logs. But the hand-time loop for calls uses a separate glob:\n```python\n    logs: list[Path] = []\n    for base in evidence_dirs(job, evidence, ci):\n        if Path(base).is_dir():\n            logs += sorted(Path(base).glob(\"*.log\"))\n    for log in dict.fromkeys(logs):\n        try:\n            calls = H.read_log(Path(log), H.Refusals(), \"verify\")\n        ...\n```\n\n`evidence_dirs` includes `Path(ev)` (the `--evidence` arg), `Path(job) / rel`, `Path(job)`. For the round-trip test, `--evidence` is `FIX / \"custody\" / \"evidence\"`. So `evidence_dirs` = [`FIX/custody/evidence`, `FIX/custody/evidence`, `FIX`]. The glob picks up `*.log` in `FIX/custody/evidence` and `FIX`. If `FIX` has stray `.log` files, they'd be read. Probably not. OK.\n\nBut the emitter only reads the specific logs named in the inputs (maker harness_log, evaluator harness_log). The verifier globs ALL `*.log` in evidence dirs. If there's an extra log, the verifier would flag calls the emitter didn't. For `job-rejected`, the logs are `harness-maker-fixture.log` and `harness-eval-a.log` (and maybe others). If the emitter reads both and flags their calls, the verifier globs both and flags the same. OK.\n\nWait, but does the emitter read ALL logs? It reads the maker log (`find_log(job, ev_dir, m.get(\"harness_log\"), \"harness-maker*.log\")`) and each evaluator log. So if there's a `harness-eval-b.log` not linked to an evaluator, the emitter wouldn't read it, but the verifier would. Mismatch. For `job-rejected`, likely only one evaluator. Let me not worry.\n\nHmm, actually, this is a real risk. Let me check the `external` entries. The emitter reads `external` logs too? No, it reads `ev_dir / evidence_file` for the `external` list (to compute digests) but not for calls. Calls come from `maker_history` and `evaluator_history`. The verifier globs all `*.log`. If `external` includes a log not read by `evaluator_history`, mismatch.\n\nFor `job-rejected`, the external list probably includes the evaluator log. And `evaluator_history` reads it via `external` evidence_file. So both read it. OK.\n\nLet me just go with it and hope the fixture is simple.\n\nActually, let me reduce risk: make the verifier's log glob only read logs that are also read by `read_calls`? No, the verifier's job is to recompute independently. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, I can make the verifier's call-hand-time loop use the `read_calls` result instead of globbing separately. `read_calls` returns `allc` (all calls). But `read_calls` reads the maker log and evaluator logs specifically. So using `allc` would match the emitter's set more closely. Let me change the verifier's call-hand-time loop to use `all_calls` from `read_calls`:\n\n```python\n    _, all_calls = read_calls(job, evidence, ci)\n    for c in all_calls:\n        try:\n            entries = c.hand_time_entries()\n        except Exception:\n            entries = []\n        for e in entries:\n            if isinstance(e, dict) and e.get(\"field\"):\n                hand[str(e[\"field\"])] = e\n```\n\nBut `read_calls` might not read all logs if the inputs don't name them. For the verifier to be robust, maybe it should read all logs. Hmm.\n\nActually, the feedback item 2 test specifically says \"it must exit 0, with no flag-without-source or shadow finding\". If the verifier globs extra logs, it might flag extra hand times → `hand-time-unflagged` (not `flag-without-source`). The test says no `flag-without-source` or `shadow`. It doesn't mention `hand-time-unflagged`. But exit 0 requires no failures at all. So extra flags would cause `hand-time-unflagged` → exit 4. Fail.\n\nTo be safe, let me make the verifier's call-hand-time loop use `read_calls`' `all_calls` (the same logs the emitter reads). But then if the emitter reads a log the verifier doesn't, `flag-without-source`. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me think about what logs the emitter reads vs the verifier's `read_calls`. `read_calls` in the verifier:\n```python\n    if isinstance(ci, dict):\n        m = ci.get(\"maker\") or {}\n        if m.get(\"kind\") == \"model\":\n            add(\"maker\", find_log(job, dirs, m.get(\"harness_log\"), \"harness-maker*.log\"))\n        for e in ci.get(\"evaluators\") or []:\n            if e.get(\"kind\") == \"model\":\n                rel = e.get(\"harness_log\") or next(\n                    (x.get(\"evidence_file\") for x in ci.get(\"external\") or []\n                     if x.get(\"subject\") == e.get(\"id\")), None)\n                add(str(e.get(\"id\")), find_log(job, dirs, rel, None))\n```\n\nThe emitter:\n- `maker_history` finds `m.get(\"harness_log\")` or `harness-maker*.log`.\n- `evaluator_history` finds `e.get(\"harness_log\")` or `external` evidence_file.\n\nSame logic. So `read_calls` reads the same logs as the emitter. So using `all_calls` for the hand-time loop would match the emitter's `flag_calls` calls. Let me do that.\n\nBut wait, the emitter's