l use `1767225600`.\n\nWait — but is it required to keep that line exactly? The file is in my allowed paths, and I'm returning the complete new contents. If I write `1767225600`, and the committed one is that too, no harm. If the committed one were different... but `[PHONE]` is clearly a redaction of a 10-digit number. I'll use 1767225600.\n\nActually, let me be careful: maybe the base file literally had `CREATED = [PHONE]`? No, that would be a NameError and the tests would fail at base. So it's a redaction.\n\nHmm, but the harness said \"every test that exists at the base must still pass\" — so the base must work. So it's a redaction of the epoch. Good.\n\n2. In `tests/validate_schemas.py` I used `hashlib.sha256` in the `_stand_in_checkout` — `hashlib` is imported earlier in the file (yes: `import shutil, tempfile, zipfile, copy, hashlib, time`). Good. And `run_py` is defined. Good.\n\n3. In validate_schemas, the F1f test: I modify `j` (job_copy(\"f1f-relay\")) and then reuse it. Note the `relay_only_buyer` check requires `packet.get(\"buyer\")` — the fixture packet presumably has a buyer. Let me check: `relay_only_buyer` returns False if not packet.get(\"buyer\"). The job-rejected fixture packet — I don't know its contents for sure. Hmm. In the base test file, `p[\"specialist\"] = \"Review Bot\"` etc. is set, so packet has specialist. Buyer? `H.check_approval` uses `packet.get(role)` for buyer/specialist. The fixture's approvals include a \"buyer\" role (test says `c[\"bar\"][\"approvals\"]` ... in t2b they append an approve with role buyer; and in `relay_only_buyer` I require packet.get(\"buyer\")). Risky if the fixture packet has no \"buyer\" key.\n\nHmm. The bar says: \"a buyer approval is relay-only when its only evidence is a `relay` ledger line (event `relay`) and there is no direct buyer approval, that is, no `approve` ledger line with role `buyer` ... and no buyer approval typed into inputs/custody.json.\" It doesn't mention packet.get(\"buyer\"). Let me remove the `packet.get(\"buyer\")` requirement to be safe? But then any job with a relay line and no buyer approval would be refused. Hmm, that could break other tests: e.g. the F1h test in validate_schemas appends a relay line (\"buyer likes it\") to job \"f1h\" — with a buyer approval present in custody.json? The fixture job-rejected's custody.json — does it have a buyer approval? The test `test_...` in validate_schemas: `for role, by in roles` in tool_built_job — roles from `cj[\"bar\"][\"approvals\"]`. And in the F1f-related base test `t2b-hand` appends an approve with role buyer.\n\nWe don't know if the base fixture has a buyer approval. Risky. Let's look for evidence: in validate_schemas, the \"live-match\" test appends `{\"event\": \"approve\", \"role\": \"specialist\", ...}` and checks `rl[\"bar\"][\"approvals\"][1]` — so approvals[1] is the specialist, meaning approvals[0] is likely the buyer. So the fixture's custody.json has a buyer approval. Good — so in the f1f test I remove the buyer approval from custody.json and from ledger lines, then add a relay. With `packet.get(\"buyer\")` requirement — the packet presumably names a buyer (packets require buyer). Yes, job packets require \"buyer\". The fixture packet is a real packet (validate_job_packet etc.), and the schema requires buyer. So `packet.get(\"buyer\")` should be truthy.\n\nBut careful: is the buyer approval in custody.json approvals[0] with role \"buyer\"? In the t2b test they append an approve line with role \"buyer\" and by \"Fixture Buyer\" — consistent.\n\nAlso risk: after I remove the buyer approval from custody.json and the buyer approve line from the ledger, the job still emits otherwise? Doesn't matter for the refusal test. Then I add a direct buyer approve line → refusal gone → rc2 == 0. But wait: after removing buyer from custody.json, the `check_approval` loop only iterates over remaining approvals (specialist). And gaps: \"bar.approvals\" not empty. Fine. But other things might refuse: the specialist approval in custody.json with `at` — if there's no matching ledger line, it's marked as hand time inputs-no-ledger... but we emit with allow_hand=True, so it's fine. Also I appended a specialist approve line at `when` = spec's at. That's a tool-writer line with `at` = spec at; the emitter cross-checks live vs inputs: they agree. Good.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: appending an approve line with writer \"kit/custody_ledger.py@abc123def456\" — writer_source returns None (tool), fine.\n\nAlso the relay line with `from: \"relay-bot\"` — could that trigger relay-as-approval in the emitter? The emitter doesn't check that. Fine.\n\nBut wait: adding a relay line changes `xp[\"relays\"]`, and `check_events` in the verifier compares... we're not verifying this job. Fine.\n\nAlso the F1f refusal: `rc == 3` and \"[approval]\" in out and \"buyer\" in out. My refusal message: `f\"buyer: the buyer's approval exists only as a relay ...\"` — contains \"buyer\". Good. And nothing written. Good.\n\nBut hold on — with allow_hand=True, could the emitter refuse for another reason first and still be exit 3 with [approval]? No, we check \"[approval]\" specifically.\n\nPotential problem: does the fixture job have a relay line already (the base ledger has a pin line, per comments \"the fixture's own rows (its provider pin)\")? My `relay_only_buyer` requires at least one relay line, which I add. Good.\n\nAnother risk: the fixture's custody.json approvals[0] might not be \"buyer\" — maybe both roles are buyer/specialist as in the packet. The base test `t2b-hand` appends an approve with role \"buyer\" and expects it becomes a hand time named \"bar.approvals[\" — meaning there IS an approvals entry with role buyer (the loop matches role). Yes! In `build`, `live = ev_log[\"approve\"].get(a.get(\"role\"))` — for the appended buyer line to produce a `bar.approvals[i].at` hand-time mark, there must be an approval with role \"buyer\" in custody.json. Good, confirms.\n\n4. `t2d-override` test in validate_schemas references `cj[\"bar\"][\"approvals\"]` with role specialist — fine.\n\n5. In validate_schemas, I removed nothing from the base except adding. Let me check I kept everything from the base file... I need to be careful — I rewrote the whole file from the given base plus my additions. Let me compare: the base ends with the \"E1: a real disagreement...\" block then `shutil.rmtree(TMP, ignore_errors=True)` then report. I added the V1/V3/T2 round-trip and F1f blocks before `shutil.rmtree`. Everything else preserved. I need to double check I didn't drop anything. The base had the \"dispose-mismatch\" block right before `shutil.rmtree(TMP, ignore_errors=True)`. Yes I have it.\n\n6. `tests/test_plumbline_verify.py`: I rewrote it with a stand-in checker. Concern: `test_clean_record_passes_both_layers` runs against the stand-in checker which validates subject digests against `job/custody/files`. The clean record's subject must match the clean job's files. Since the record was presumably emitted from that job, it should. But hmm — the record is a fixture `clean/record.custody.json` and the job is `clean/job`. If the record's subject digests match the job's custody/files, fine. Risk: if not, the test fails. The bar says \"clean control C-Vc ... must PASS\". Also base test had `record-bad-subject.json` failing with the real checker presumably (the stand-in at base was a copy of a fixture checker). Actually at base, the stand-in was `tests/fixtures/custody/verify/checkout/checker/check.py` — a fixture. Hmm, I replaced it with a generated one. The instruction item 7 says: \"Run the verifier tests against a stand-in checker that exits non-zero unless `--bar` is given... Write that stand-in from code in the test file into the test's temporary custody checkout (the existing helper already copies a checker there and pins its sha256).\"\n\nSo the instruction says the existing helper already copies a checker there — i.e., keep copying the fixture checker but... hmm, \"Write that stand-in from code\" — ambiguous. Safer: write the stand-in from code, as I did. But then I lose the ability to detect the bad-subject case unless my stand-in checks digests — which it does.\n\nRisk: my stand-in's subject-digest check might make `record-bad-subject.json` fail (good, expected exit 3) but might also make the clean record fail if digests don't match. Since the fixture `record-bad-subject.json` presumably differs in a subject digest from `record.custody.json`, and the clean one matches its files, this should work.\n\nHmm, but there's a risk that the clean record's subject names files not present, or that the fixture record's subject digest list is empty. If subject is empty, my stand-in passes — and then `record-bad-subject.json` would also pass, failing the test. Risky!\n\nAlternative: make the stand-in compare the record's subject digests, and if the record differs from... hmm.\n\nSafer approach: keep using the fixture checker for the existing tests (as at base) but ALSO add a check that the verifier passes `--bar`. How? Use a wrapper: copy the fixture checker bytes, but prepend a guard? Then the sha256 pin changes, and the verifier pins whatever's there (it computes the pin from tools/pin.json which the helper writes as the sha256 of the file it wrote). So: write the checker file as `GUARD + fixture_checker_source`, pin the sha256 of that. Then:\n- The fixture checker's own logic runs (so bad-subject still fails as at base).\n- The guard exits non-zero unless --bar is given.\n\nBut the fixture checker might be a python file — prepending python code works if it's python. It's `check.py` so yes python. But careful with shebang line — prepending before `#!/usr/bin/env python3` is fine since we invoke with sys.executable.\n\nBut what if the fixture checker's main logic is at module level after an `if __name__` guard? Prepending is still fine.\n\nHmm, but the fixture checker might itself require other args (like `--bar` already?). The base test passed with the fixture checker, and the base verifier did NOT pass --bar (that was the bug). Wait — the base verifier didn't pass --bar and the base tests passed with the fixture checker. So the fixture checker doesn't require --bar. Good, so prepending a guard adds the requirement.\n\nThat's the safest: preserve the fixture checker behavior (bad-subject fails), and add the --bar guard.\n\nBut the instruction says \"Write that stand-in from code in the test file\" — prepending a guard is writing part of it from code. I think combining is safest: use the fixture checker's source plus a guard prefix. Hmm, but does that satisfy the evaluator? The evaluator checks that a verifier forgetting --bar cannot pass — satisfied by the guard.\n\nActually wait: if the fixture checker is not python (could be anything), reading it as text and prepending python would break. It's named check.py in a \"checkout\" fixture dir. It's python.\n\nHmm, but there's another risk: prepending changes line numbers in the checker's tracebacks — irrelevant.\n\nAlso: does the fixture checker need `--evidence`? Probably accepts it. Fine.\n\nAlternatively — safest of all: write a stand-in that (a) requires --bar, and (b) delegates to nothing but compares subject digests, AND falls back: if the record has no subject entries, fail. But then the clean record must have subject entries. Hmm, both approaches have risk.\n\nLet me think about which risk is greater. The clean record `clean/record.custody.json` — does it have a subject? It's a custody record; the schema requires `subject`. It's derived from the clean job which has `custody/files`. So subject should be non-empty and matching. The bad-subject record is \"a record whose subject digest is altered\" (V1: \"Planted P-V1 (a record whose subject digest is altered) → exit 3\"). So the fixture pair differs in a subject digest. So a digest-checking stand-in works.\n\nI'll go with a hybrid to reduce risk: try the fixture checker source with the guard prepended. That keeps the exact base behavior for the bad-subject case and adds the --bar guard. This is the least risky since it preserves what worked at base.\n\nHmm, but the instruction explicitly: \"Write that stand-in from code in the test file into the test's temporary custody checkout (the existing helper already copies a checker there and pins its sha256).\" I read this as: write the stand-in from code. The parenthetical just notes the helper already copies+pins, so you can reuse that mechanism.\n\nDecision: write the stand-in from code with the --bar guard AND subject-digest checking. If the clean record's subject digests match the job's files, both tests pass. I'm fairly confident they do since the fixture was built that way (the clean job is \"a small job\" and the record was emitted from it, and `record-bad-subject.json` alters one).\n\nHmm, but wait: what if the clean record's subject files are under `clean/job/custody/files` with names matching? The base verifier passes `--files job/custody/files`. The record's subject entries have `name` relative to that dir. Emitted records name files relative to files_dir. So yes.\n\nRisk remains if e.g. the fixture record's subject digest is of a file whose content was later changed. Unlikely.\n\nAdditional risk: my stand-in exits 1 for a missing subject file — for `record-bad-subject.json`, the digest differs → exit 1 → verifier exit 3 with kind standard. Good.\n\nAnother consideration: `test_clean_against_real_checker` — the real checker with the clean record: the base verifier didn't pass --bar and it's not part of base tests (it's new). With the fix, we pass --bar. Real checker at CUSTODY_DIR. That test is skipped unless CUSTODY_DIR is set. Fine.\n\nNow, one more thing: in `run_verify` I pass `--custody` = the temp checkout; the verifier's `find_pin` looks at `custody/tools/pin.json` then `REPO/tools/pin.json`. Our checkout has tools/pin.json with the right sha. Good. And `_find_checker` finds `custody/checker/check.py`. Good.\n\nAlso the verifier now requires the bar file to exist: `bar_path(job, custody_json)` with default `custody/bar.md`. Does `clean/job` have `custody/bar.md`? Probably (the fixture job has custody/bar.md — validate_schemas uses `FIX / \"custody\" / \"bar.md\"` for job-rejected). For the verify fixtures, the clean job likely mirrors job-rejected. If it doesn't have custody/bar.md, the verifier returns exit 2 and all tests break. Risky!\n\nHmm. To reduce risk: if `bar.file` is absent and the default bar file doesn't exist, fall back to... the bar is needed by the real checker. But the stand-in only needs *some* --bar value. Option: if no bar file exists, still pass the path (the real checker would fail, but that's correct behavior: exit 2 is \"cannot run\" per bar). Hmm, but bar says exit 2 for \"a missing input\". A missing bar is a missing input.\n\nBut if the verify fixture's clean job lacks custody/bar.md, all the tests fail with exit 2. Let me consider: the fixtures were created in iteration 2 by me (the Maker) — I created `tests/fixtures/custody/verify/clean/job`. The prompt says fixtures added in iteration 2 exist. I don't have their contents. The clean job was described in the base test docstring: \"`clean/job` is a small job (a tool-stamped freeze in the ledger and a matching inputs/custody.json)\". No mention of a bar file. Hmm.\n\nThat's a real risk. Mitigation: make the bar optional — if the bar file is missing, run the checker without `--bar`... no, that defeats the purpose.\n\nBetter mitigation: in the tests, create the bar file in the copied job if missing. The tests copy the job to a temp dir (`make_job`), so I can write `custody/bar.md` there if absent. That makes the tests robust. And for the real-checker test it also helps.\n\nBut also the emitted-record round-trip test in validate_schemas uses job-rejected, which definitely has custody/bar.md.\n\nI'll add to `make_job`: ensure `job/custody/bar.md` exists (write a small bar if missing). Hmm, but if the record's bar digest refers to a specific file... the verifier doesn't check bar digests; the real checker would. For the stand-in, any file works.\n\nHmm, but careful: if the clean job already has custody/bar.md, don't overwrite (its digest might be in the record and the real checker checks it).\n\nSo: `if not (dst / \"custody\" / \"bar.md\").is_file(): write a placeholder`. Wait — but for the real-checker test, a placeholder bar would fail the real checker's bar digest check. Only if the job lacks the real bar. If the job has its own bar, we keep it.\n\nHmm, but also the real checker might require more (evidence files etc.). The real-checker test is optional (skipped without CUSTODY_DIR). Accept.\n\nAlternatively, make the verifier's bar handling: pass `--bar` only if the bar file exists, else pass nothing? Then the stand-in would refuse → exit 2 → tests fail. No.\n\nOK: plan — verifier requires the bar file (exit 2 if missing), and the test helper ensures a bar file exists in the copied job. That's consistent and safe.\n\nActually wait. There's another consideration: `bar_path` reads `bar.file` from custody.json. If the clean job's custody.json names a bar file that exists, great. Default `custody/bar.md`.\n\nLet me write `make_job` to ensure the bar exists:\n\n```python\ndef make_job(tmp, ledger=None, extra=None):\n    dst = tmp / \"job\"\n    shutil.copytree(FIX / \"clean\" / \"job\", dst)\n    ...\n    # the verifier passes the job's bar to the pinned checker; make sure one exists\n    b = dst / \"custody\" / \"bar.md\"\n    if not b.is_file():\n        b.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        b.write_text(\"# bar\\n\\nfixture bar\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    return dst\n```\n\nBut if custody.json says bar.file = something else that doesn't exist, still broken. Hmm. Then the verifier would exit 2 for the missing bar. To be safer, in make_job: read custody.json, get bar.file, and if that file doesn't exist, create it with placeholder content. Let's do that — create whatever file `bar.file` names.\n\nBut careful: creating a file inside `custody/` changes the job tree; `test_run_writes_nothing` snapshots before/after the verify run, which is fine since we create before.\n\nAlso `test_run_writes_nothing` copies the record into tmp and verifies. Fine.\n\nOK, and one more: the verifier's `hand_time_set` now marks typed passes/controls `at` as hand times always. For the clean job (no typed `at` in passes/controls?) the record's hand_times must match. The clean record `clean/record.custody.json` has empty hand_times presumably. If the clean job's custody.json has a typed `passes.<id>.at`, the verifier would now require it in hand_times → mismatch → test_clean fails with hand-time-unflagged.\n\nHmm! That's a real risk. The clean job is \"a small job (a tool-stamped freeze in the ledger and a matching inputs/custody.json)\". Does its custody.json have passes with `at`? The fixture's passes are referenced in the emitter's tests... The clean verify job is small; it probably has no passes at all (no §5 record). If it has passes with `at` and the record lists them in hand_times — the record was hand-written, so probably hand_times is empty for the clean case (\"clean\" = no hand times).\n\nRisk: if clean job has typed ats, the test breaks. But the clean record's hand_times should have been written to match. Since at base the verifier did NOT flag typed pass ats when a call was linked, and my change now flags them always — if the clean job has a pass with `at` and a linked call, then the record (clean) has empty hand_times → now fails.\n\nHmm. To mitigate in the test: for `test_clean_record_passes_both_layers`, I can't easily change the fixture record. But I could delete typed `at` values from the copied job's custody.json for passes/controls — no, that changes what the record says.\n\nAlternatively, add the corresponding entries to the record? That's fixture editing via test code — allowed (test builds it in a temp dir).\n\nSimplest robust mitigation: in `make_job`, for the clean case... no, it's used for all cases.\n\nAlternative: accept the risk. The clean job is described as small: \"a tool-stamped freeze in the ledger and a matching inputs/custody.json\". Given the planted cases are about allowance/events/relay/shadow, the clean job likely has no passes/controls with ats. Also the base `test_clean_record_passes_both_layers` passed at base with my iteration-2 verifier which computed hand times with the call-linked exception. If the clean job had a typed pass at with a linked call, the base verifier wouldn't flag it and the record's hand_times (empty) matched. My change would now flag it → fail.\n\nHmm, this is exactly item 2's risk. The item says: \"Test: copy tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected into a temporary directory, emit it with --allow-hand-times, and run the verifier on that record; it must exit 0\". That's the required test. It doesn't say the clean fixture test must still pass... but it must (G2).\n\nTo be safe, I can make the verifier's rule apply exactly as the emitter's: the emitter marks `passes.<id>.at` always when `f.get(\"at\")` is present. So for job-rejected, the emitter marks it (passes.pass-a.at has a typed at in the fixture — yes! validate_schemas's E2 test sets `c[\"passes\"][\"pass-a\"][\"at\"]`, but that's setting it; the base fixture may or may not have it).\n\nWait, E2 test: `c[\"passes\"][\"pass-a\"][\"at\"] = \"2026-10-20T12:00:02Z\"` — sets it explicitly, implying the base fixture may not have it. Hmm, and the emitter's `mark` for passes happens `if f.get(\"at\")`. So if the fixture lacks it, no hand time.\n\nFor the clean verify job — unknown. I'll take the risk but reduce it: in `make_job`, after copying, if the clean job's custody.json has `passes`/`controls` with `at` AND the corresponding record (which I don't control per-case)... too complex.\n\nAlternative safer approach: make the verifier's rule match the emitter'