No, dirty tree refuses regardless.\n\nWait — actually, is the tree dirty in the harness? The harness commits my files, so the working tree is clean at test time (they run tests after committing). In validate_schemas they made a clean checkout because tests may run from a dirty tree. Hmm, \"the emitter refuses to run from a dirty tree (it records its own commit), so the cases below run it from a throwaway clean commit of this working tree\".\n\nIf the test suite runs in a clean checkout (the harness commits everything then runs tests), running the emitter directly from the repo works. But if there are untracked files (e.g., __pycache__ — gitignored? \"untracked-but-not-ignored\")... `revision()` uses `git status --porcelain=v1 -z --untracked-files=all` and excludes dot-plumbline/ and the job dir. __pycache__ might be untracked and not ignored → dirty. Hmm, in validate_schemas they copy files via `ls-files -co --exclude-standard`, which excludes ignored files but includes untracked non-ignored. So if the repo is clean at test time, that copy is clean too.\n\nFor safety in test_plumbline_verify.py, I'll create a small helper that copies just `kit/` into a temp dir and git-inits it — the emitter only needs kit/ files (emit_custody.py, custody_history.py). It imports custody_history from its own dir. And `revision()` runs git in HERE (kit dir) → `rev-parse --show-toplevel` in the temp dir. That works: a temp git repo containing kit/ (and maybe nothing else). The emitter doesn't need schemas.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\ndef clean_emitter_tree(tmp: Path) -> Path:\n    d = tmp / \"emitter-tree\"\n    (d / \"kit\").mkdir(parents=True)\n    for name in (\"emit_custody.py\", \"custody_history.py\", \"custody_ledger.py\"):\n        shutil.copy2(ROOT / \"kit\" / name, d / \"kit\" / name)\n    git = [\"git\", \"-C\", str(d), \"-c\", \"user.name=test\", \"-c\", \"user.email=test@localhost\", \"-c\", \"commit.gpgsign=false\"]\n    subprocess.run([\"git\", \"-C\", str(d), \"init\", \"-q\"], check=True, capture_output=True)\n    subprocess.run([\"git\", \"-C\", str(d), \"add\", \"-A\"], check=True, capture_output=True)\n    subprocess.run([\"git\", \"-C\", str(d), \"commit\", \"-qm\", \"clean\"], check=True, capture_output=True)\n    return d\n```\n\nWait, `git -c` options must come before the subcommand: `git -C dir -c k=v commit`. In validate_schemas: `git = [\"git\", \"-C\", str(dest), \"-c\", \"user.name=test\", ...]` then `git + [\"commit\", \"-qm\", ...]`. Good.\n\nThen emit:\n\n```python\ndef emit_job(job, out, tree, allow_hand=True):\n    args = [sys.executable, str(tree / \"kit\" / \"emit_custody.py\"), \"--job-dir\", str(job), \"--out\", str(out)]\n    if allow_hand: args.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n    return subprocess.run(args, capture_output=True, text=True)\n```\n\nAlso, the emitter's `revision()` excludes the job dir if it's inside the tree — our job is in tmp, not inside the tree. And the tree contains only kit/ files, clean. Good.\n\nHmm, but one catch: `sys.executable` — the tests run under the venv python; fine.\n\nNow the item-3 test:\n\n```python\nclass HandTimeTypedTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp: tmp\n    def job(self): copy job-rejected\n    def test_typed_started_at_and_produced_at_are_hand_times(self):\n        job = copy of job-rejected\n        out = tmp/\"rec.json\"\n        rv = emit_job(job, out, tree, allow_hand=True)\n        assert rc 0\n        xp = record x-plumbline\n        ht = {e[\"field\"]: e for e in xp[\"hand_times\"]}\n        for f in (\"startedAt\", \"producedAt\"):\n            assert in ht; ht[f][\"file\"] == \"inputs/custody.json\"; isinstance(ht[f][\"line\"], int); ht[f][\"source\"] == \"inputs-no-ledger\"\n        # the verifier recomputes the same\n        hand = V.hand_time_set(job, job/\"inputs\"/\"custody-events.jsonl\", job/\"inputs\"/\"custody.json\", rec, job/\"custody\"/\"evidence\")\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, `hand_time_set(job, ledger_path, custody_path, record, evidence)`. I can call it directly and check the entries.\n\nThen run the verifier as a subprocess with a stand-in checkout, expect 0 and no flag-without-source/shadow.\n\nCareful: the verifier's `hand_time_set` reads harness logs from evidence dirs. It should produce the same set as the record's. Then exit 0.\n\nBut: the verifier's check_events/check_spend/check_shadow must also pass for job-rejected. validate_schemas already proves the roundtrip passes with exit 0. Good.\n\nFor item 2's test I need the same emit machinery.\n\nNow also item 1's test for the record-no-allowance: uses the verify fixtures; no emitter.\n\nLet me now write the code.\n\nAlso: `V.hand_time_set` signature — it takes (job, ledger_path, custody_path, record, evidence). The `record` param is unused actually (I see it's in the signature but not used). Fine.\n\nNow let's write `check_hand_times` with record_path:\n\n```python\ndef check_hand_times(hand: list[dict], record: dict, record_path: Path | None = None) -> list[dict]:\n    fails = []\n    rp = str(record_path) if record_path else \"<record>\"\n    ...\n    for k in sorted(recomp - got):\n        fails.append({\"kind\": \"hand-time-unflagged\", \"field\": k[0], \"file\": k[1] or rp, \"line\": k[2] if isinstance(k[2], int) else 0})\n```\n\nHmm, `k[2]` may be None. Use a helper `_loc(k, rp)`.\n\nWait: careful about sorting tuples containing None vs int — `sorted(recomp - got)` where tuples have (str, str, int|None, str). Comparing None with int raises TypeError if two tuples differ only at position 2 with None vs int. Existing code already does this... it's pre-existing risk. Since both sides use hand_key consistently, probably fine. But to be safe, I could normalize line to int in hand_key: `line = int(...) if ... else 0`. Hmm, changing hand_key affects matching: if the record has line None and recomputed 0 → mismatch. Keep as is (don't touch), but make sorting robust? Let me normalize `None` to -1 in the sort key only:\n\n```python\ndef _hkey_sort(k):\n    return (str(k[0]), str(k[1]), -1 if k[2] is None else int(k[2]) if isinstance(k[2], (int, float)) else -1, str(k[3]))\n```\n\nHmm, simpler: keep sorted() as-is; it worked before. Actually if it worked before with the fixtures, it works now. Leave it. But the new hand entries (startedAt) have int lines. Fine.\n\nHmm, actually there is a risk: `sorted(recomp - got)` with mixed None/int. In job-rejected roundtrip, both sets are identical so no sorting issue (empty sets). For P-V3 plants, the differing tuples... e.g., a hand ledger line missing from the flags: recomputed has line int, got empty. Sorting a single-element set is fine. Two elements with one None... unlikely. Leave.\n\nNow `check_schema(record, schema_path, record_path)`:\n\n```python\n    if not isinstance(xp, dict):\n        fails.append({\"kind\": \"schema\", \"field\": \"predicate.x-plumbline\", \"file\": str(record_path), \"line\": 0})\n        return fails\n    ...\n    if xp.get(\"schema\") != SCHEMA_VERSION:\n        fails.append({\"kind\": \"schema\", \"field\": \"x-plumbline.schema\", \"file\": str(record_path), \"line\": 0})\n```\n\nAnd other schema findings keep schema_path.\n\nNow `finish`:\n\n```python\ndef _floc(f, fallback):\n    file = f.get(\"file\")\n    if not isinstance(file, str) or not file.strip():\n        file = fallback\n    line = f.get(\"line\")\n    if not isinstance(line, int) or isinstance(line, bool) or line < 0:\n        line = 0\n    return file, line\n\n\ndef finish(code, failures, json_out):\n    fb = str(RECORD_PATH) if RECORD_PATH else \"<record>\"\n    for f in failures:\n        file, line = _floc(f, fb)\n        if code == 2:\n            msg = f.get(\"message\") or f.get(\"field\")\n            print(f\"NOT CHECKABLE: {msg} ({file}:{line})\")\n        else:\n            print(f\"FAIL {f.get('kind')} {f.get('field')} {file}:{line}\")\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, but the json output: should it also carry the fixed file/line? The acceptance only checks stdout lines. But consistency is better: write failures with the normalized file/line. Let me build a normalized list.\n\nWait, careful with `--json`: existing test `test_json_out_lists_failures` checks `doc[\"failures\"][0][\"kind\"] == \"allowance\"`. Fine.\n\nBut the item-1 test checks stdout lines. I'll normalize in the json too (harmless).\n\nHmm, one concern: `f.get('field')` could be None for some failure? All have field.\n\nAlso RECORD_PATH global: set at the start of main after parsing args: `global RECORD_PATH; RECORD_PATH = record`. But `finish` is called for missing record too — at that point RECORD_PATH isn't set. Use the local fallback: pass record path? Simpler: set RECORD_PATH right after parsing args, before the existence checks. `record = Path(a.record).expanduser()` then `RECORD_PATH = record`.\n\nModule-level:\n\n```python\nRECORD_PATH: Path | None = None\n```\n\nand in main: `global RECORD_PATH`.\n\nNow the line for allowance findings: 0. The acceptance wants `<digits>` — 0 is digits. But could I do better and point at the record's line where `allowances` would be? Not necessary.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: \"For record-no-allowance.json the line is `FAIL allowance x-plumbline.allowances.hand_times <the --record path>:<digits>`\". So file must be exactly the --record path. `str(Path(a.record).expanduser())` — the test passes an absolute path so no expansion changes. Good.\n\nNow let's also double check the `check_hand_times` field for allowance findings: unchanged fields: `x-plumbline.allowances.hand_times`, `.switch`, `.writer`, `.at`. Only file/line change.\n\nNow item 2: remove the elif. Also, should `check_spend` still fail when there's no spend line but calls_sum > 0? No — removed.\n\nLet me double-check the rest of check_spend with no spend line and a record spend block: `sp_line = None` → skip. Good.\n\nNow let's write the emitter change.\n\n```python\n    # A typed startedAt/producedAt is a hand time whether or not the maker has a logged call (V3): the tool's\n    # time is the log's, and a time typed into custody.json has no tool behind it.\n    if ci.get(\"startedAt\"):\n        mark(\"startedAt\", rel_inputs, line_in(inputs_path, ci.get(\"startedAt\"), \"startedAt\"), \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n    if ci.get(\"producedAt\"):\n        mark(\"producedAt\", rel_inputs, line_in(inputs_path, ci.get(\"producedAt\"), \"producedAt\"), \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n```\n\nCareful: `rel_inputs` when inputs_path is outside the job — uses str(inputs_path). Fine.\n\nNow the verifier's hand_time_set:\n\n```python\n    # A typed startedAt/producedAt is a hand time whether or not the maker has a logged call (V3): the tool's\n    # time is the log's, and a time typed into custody.json has no tool behind it.\n    if ci.get(\"startedAt\"):\n        mark(\"startedAt\", inputs_rel, line_of(custody_path, ci.get(\"startedAt\"), \"startedAt\"), \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n    if ci.get(\"producedAt\"):\n        mark(\"producedAt\", inputs_rel, line_of(custody_path, ci.get(\"producedAt\"), \"producedAt\"), \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n```\n\n`mcalls` variable no longer needed for that; but it's still used? Let me check: `mcalls = calls_by_seat.get(\"maker\") or {}` used only in that block. Remove it (or leave unused). Remove.\n\nNow, hmm: `check_shadow`'s derived[\"times\"] uses `mcalls = calls_by_seat.get(\"maker\") or {}`. Keep.\n\nNow let me also double-check the emitter's `xp[\"times\"]`: `{\"startedAt\": \"harness log\" if mk.get(\"startedAt\") else \"hand-entered\", ...}` — unchanged per instructions.\n\nNow — potential mismatch: job-rejected has a model maker with a log, so mk.get(\"startedAt\") is set → times \"harness log\"; verifier derived: mcalls non-empty → \"harness log\". Match.\n\nNow let's think about whether `hand_time_set`'s typed startedAt could mismatch the emitter's for the T4 tool-built job: no typed startedAt → no entry. Good.\n\nNow, the `time_sources` function: unaffected.\n\nOne more: `xp[\"sources\"][f]` for startedAt/producedAt from mk — unaffected.\n\nNow the tests. Let me write the full new test file, keeping all existing tests and adding new ones.\n\nI need imports: `shutil`, `subprocess`, `sys`, `tempfile`, `unittest`, `json`, `os`, `Path`, `sha256`. Already imported.\n\nNew helper functions at module level:\n\n```python\nJOB_REJECTED = ROOT / \"tests\" / \"fixtures\" / \"custody\" / \"job-rejected\"\n\n\ndef clean_emitter_tree(tmp: Path) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"A throwaway clean git checkout of kit/ (the emitter records its own revision and refuses a dirty tree).\"\"\"\n    d = tmp / \"emitter-tree\"\n    (d / \"kit\").mkdir(parents=True)\n    for name in (\"emit_custody.py\", \"custody_history.py\", \"custody_ledger.py\"):\n        shutil.copy2(ROOT / \"kit\" / name, d / \"kit\" / name)\n    base = [\"git\", \"-C\", str(d), \"-c\", \"user.name=test\", \"-c\", \"user.email=test@localhost\", \"-c\", \"commit.gpgsign=false\"]\n    subprocess.run(base + [\"init\", \"-q\"], capture_output=True, check=True)\n    subprocess.run(base + [\"add\", \"-A\"], capture_output=True, check=True)\n    subprocess.run(base + [\"commit\", \"-qm\", \"clean checkout for the emitter\"], capture_output=True, check=True)\n    return d\n\n\ndef run_emit(job: Path, out: Path, tree: Path, allow_hand: bool = True):\n    cmd = [sys.executable, str(tree / \"kit\" / \"emit_custody.py\"), \"--job-dir\", str(job), \"--out\", str(out)]\n    if allow_hand:\n        cmd.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n    return subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)\n\n\ndef copy_job(tmp: Path, name: str = \"job\") -> Path:\n    dst = tmp / name\n    shutil.copytree(JOB_REJECTED, dst)\n    return dst\n```\n\nCareful: `git -C` before `init` works (the dir exists).\n\nNow the new test classes.\n\n```python\nclass FailureLineTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    \"\"\"V intro: every failure line names kind, field and a non-empty FILE:LINE.\"\"\"\n\n    def setUp/tearDown\n\n    def _folders(self):\n        return sorted(p for p in FIX.iterdir() if p.is_dir())\n\n    def test_every_fail_line_names_a_file(self):\n        for folder in self._folders():\n            ledger = folder / \"ledger.jsonl\"\n            records = sorted(folder.glob(\"record*.json\"))\n            for rec in records:\n                with self.subTest(folder=folder.name, record=rec.name):\n                    tmp = self.tmp / f\"{folder.name}-{rec.name}\"\n                    tmp.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n                    extra = {p.name: str(p.relative_to(FIX)) for p in folder.iterdir()\n                             if p.is_file() and p.name != \"ledger.jsonl\" and not p.name.startswith(\"record\")}\n                    job = make_job(tmp, f\"{folder.name}/ledger.jsonl\" if ledger.is_file() else None, extra)\n                    rv = run_verify(rec, job, checkout(tmp))\n                    self.assertNotIn(rv.returncode, (2,), rv.stdout + rv.stderr)  # hmm\n                    for ln in rv.stdout.splitlines():\n                        if not ln.startswith(\"FAIL \"):\n                            continue\n                        tok = ln.split()[-1]\n                        file, sep, line = tok.rpartition(\":\")\n                        self.assertTrue(sep and file and line.isdigit(), (rec.name, ln))\n```\n\nHmm, `make_job(tmp, ledger, extra)` — `extra` maps name→rel path relative to FIX, and copies `FIX / rel` to `dst / name`. In my loop, `p.relative_to(FIX)` gives e.g. \"shadow/iteration-1-eval-record.json\". Good.\n\nBut careful: `checkout(tmp)` creates a checkout dir inside tmp — and `make_job` creates tmp/\"job\". If I create `tmp` per record, fine.\n\nAlso: some records might cause exit 2 (NOT CHECKABLE) if the bar is missing — ensure_bar handles it. And the record path exists.\n\nBut wait: `run_verify` passes `--custody checkout`. And `checkout()` writes the stand-in checker. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: for a record in the `events` folder, the verifier might exit 3 (standard) if the record's subject digests don't match the files dir — but all these records share the same subject presumably. If exit 3, output is `FAIL standard record <record>:0` — last token fine. Good, the test only checks format, not exit code.\n\nShould I assert something about exit codes? The acceptance only requires format checks plus the specific allowance case. Keep it lenient: no assertion on rc except that the run doesn't crash (returncode in (0,2,3,4)).\n\nHmm, if the verifier raises a traceback, rc would be 1 and stdout empty. Let me assert `rv.returncode in (0, 3, 4)` and no \"Traceback\" in stderr. But could a record legitimately be exit 2? Only if the checker pin is wrong or the bar is missing — not our case. So assert in (0,3,4).\n\nCareful: `record-v0.json` etc. → exit 4. Fine.\n\nNow the specific allowance test:\n\n```python\n    def test_allowance_finding_names_the_record(self):\n        job = make_job(self.tmp, \"hand/ledger.jsonl\")\n        rec = record(\"hand\", \"record-no-allowance.json\")\n        rv = run_verify(rec, job, checkout(self.tmp))\n        self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 4, rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n        line = next((ln for ln in rv.stdout.splitlines()\n                     if ln.startswith(\"FAIL allowance x-plumbline.allowances.hand_times \")), None)\n        self.assertIsNotNone(line, rv.stdout)\n        file, sep, ln_no = line.split()[-1].rpartition(\":\")\n        self.assertEqual(file, str(rec))\n        self.assertTrue(sep and ln_no.isdigit())\n```\n\nWait: make_job with ledger \"hand/ledger.jsonl\" — `shutil.copyfile(FIX / ledger / \"ledger.jsonl\", ...)`. Good.\n\nAlso `test_schema_failures` existing checks exit 4 and \"schema\" in kinds — still true.\n\nAdd a check that schema findings name the record:\n\nMaybe fold into the format test (already covered since clean/record-no-block.json is in the clean folder and will be checked for non-empty file).\n\nNow item 2 test:\n\n```python\nclass SpendWithoutSpendLineTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp/tearDown\n\n    def test_no_spend_line_with_a_cost_passes(self):\n        tree = clean_emitter_tree(self.tmp)\n        job = copy_job(self.tmp)\n        log = job / \"custody\" / \"evidence\" / \"harness-maker-fixture.log\"\n        self.assertTrue(log.is_file())\n        doc = json.loads(log.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n        doc.setdefault(\"generation\", {}).setdefault(\"data\", {})[\"total_cost\"] = 0.0012\n        log.write_text(json.dumps(doc), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        # no spend line\n        ledger = job / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\"\n        if ledger.is_file():\n            rows = [ln for ln in ledger.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines() if ln.strip()]\n            self.assertFalse(any(json.loads(r).get(\"event\") == \"spend\" for r in rows))\n        out = self.tmp / \"rec.json\"\n        rv = run_emit(job, out, tree)\n        self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 0, rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n        rec = json.loads(out.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n        self.assertEqual(rec[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"][\"spend\"],\n                         {\"calls_sum_usd\": 0.0012, \"key_delta_usd\": 0.0, \"total_usd\": 0.0012,\n                          \"tolerance_usd\": 0.0001, \"source\": None})\n        v = run_verify(out, job, checkout(self.tmp))\n        self.assertEqual(v.returncode, 0, v.stdout + v.stderr)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"FAIL \", v.stdout)\n```\n\nCareful: `doc.setdefault(\"generation\", {})` — if \"generation\" exists as dict with \"data\". job-rejected's maker log has generation (the record's endpoint requires it). But maybe the structure is `{\"data\": {...}}` directly. Use:\n\n```python\ngen = doc.get(\"generation\")\nif isinstance(gen, dict) and isinstance(gen.get(\"data\"), dict):\n    gen[\"data\"][\"total_cost\"] = 0.0012\nelse:\n    doc[\"generation\"] = {\"data\": {\"total_cost\": 0.0012, ...}}\n```\n\nHmm, but if we replace the generation entirely we lose provider_name → the emitter would refuse (endpoint not evidenced). So it must already have data.provider_name. Let me assert the structure:\n\n```python\nself.assertIsInstance(doc.get(\"generation\"), dict)\ngen = doc[\"generation\"]\ndata = gen.get(\"data\") if isinstance(gen.get(\"data\"), dict) else gen\ndata[\"total_cost\"] = 0.0012\n```\n\nThat handles both shapes.\n\nAlso: the log may be a list of calls or a single object. validate_schemas does `json.loads(lg.read_text())` and `x[\"response\"]` → single object. But to be safe handle both:\n\n```python\ndef _set_total_cost(path, value):\n    text = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    try:\n        doc = json.loads(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        raise AssertionError(\"maker log is not JSON\")\n    items = doc if isinstance(doc, list) else [doc]\n    for c in items:\n        gen = c.get(\"generation\")\n        if isinstance(gen, dict):\n            data = gen.get(\"data\") if isinstance(gen.get(\"data\"), dict) else gen\n            if isinstance(data, dict):\n                data[\"total_cost\"] = value\n    path.write_text(json.dumps(doc), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n```\n\nHmm, if the log is JSON Lines (multiple lines each a JSON object), `json.loads(text)` fails. validate_schemas's `stamp_log` handles both. Let me write a robust helper:\n\n```python\ndef _load_calls(path):\n    text = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    try:\n        doc = json.loads(text)\n        return (doc, True) if isinstance(doc, list) else ([doc], False), text\n    except ValueError:\n        return [json.loads(ln) for ln in text.splitlines() if ln.strip()], False\n```\n\nAnd write back preserving shape.\n\nBut careful: rewriting the log with `json.dumps` changes byte content — the record's `external` evidence digests are computed at emit time from the modified file, so fine.\n\nAlso careful: the log may be read by the verifier later; same file. Fine.\n\nAlso, other calls in the eval log might have `usage.cost` — if so, calls_sum wouldn't be 0.0012 and my assertion would fail. The Orchestrator measured 0.0012, so eval calls contribute 0.\n\nHmm, but wait: does the *maker* log have only one call? If it has two calls each with total_cost set to 0.0012, the sum would be 0.0024. The Orchestrator said \"set the maker log entry's generation.data.total_cost to 0.0012\" and observed calls_sum 0.0012, so one call. My helper sets it on all calls in the maker log — if there were two, sum 0.0024. To be safe, set only on the first call? The Orchestrator's phrasing \"the maker log entry\" suggests one entry. I'll set on all entries (matching \"entry\" singular) — if there's one, same.\n\nHmm, risky either way. Actually, better: compute the expected calls_sum from the modified logs using the same rule as the emitter? That would make the test tautological-ish but still validates the verifier-vs-emitter agreement, which is the point (the record must pass the verifier). But the acceptance explicitly wants the block `calls_sum_usd: 0.0012`. If there are two maker calls, my assertion fails and the test fails. I'll set on all and assert 0.0012 — trusting the measurement.\n\nHmm, actually — safer: assert the block equals the expected dict only if it matches the measured shape; else assert internal consistency. No — keep it strict per acceptance. The Orchestrator measured on my fixture, so one call.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider: maybe the maker log is a JSON Lines file with one line (validate_schemas: `x = json.loads(lg.read_text())` succeeded, so it's a single JSON object, not JSON Lines — a single object parse works for one-line JSONL too!). Right: `json.loads(text)` on a one-line JSONL file works. And on a two-line file fails. So the maker log is a single object (one call). Good.\n\nSimilarly `harness-eval-a.log` — validate_schemas does `json.loads(lg.read_text())` only on the maker log. The eval log has ≥3 calls (from the E3 test: `out.count(\"harness-eval-a.log#\") >= 3`), so it's a JSON array or JSONL.\n\nFor the eval log calls: do they have usage.cost? The E3 test shows they lack sent_at/received_at. If they had usage.cost, calls_sum would include it. The Orchestrator measured 0.0012 total, so no.\n\nOK.\n\nNow the item-3 tests:\n\n```python\nclass TypedStartedProducedTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp/tearDown\n\n    def _job(self):\n        job = copy_job(self.tmp)\n        return job\n\n    def test_typed_started_at_and_produced_at_are_hand_times(self):\n        tree = clean_emitter_tree(self.tmp)\n        job = copy_job(self.tmp)\n        out = self.tmp / \"rec.json\"\n        rv = run_emit(job, out, tree, allow_hand=True)\n        self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 0, rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n        rec = json.loads(out.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n        xp = rec[\"predicate\"][\"x-plumbline\"]\n        ht = {e[\"field\"]: e for e in xp[\"hand_times\"]}\n        for field in (\"startedAt\", \"producedAt\"):\n            self.assertIn(field, ht)\n            self.assertEqual(ht[field][\"file\"], \"inputs/custody.json\")\n            self.assertIsInstance(ht[field][\"line\"], int)\n            self.assertGreater(ht[field][\"line\"], 0)\n            self.assertEqual(ht[field][\"source\"], \"inputs-no-ledger\")\n        # the verifier's own recomputation lists the same two entries\n        ci = json.loads((job / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\").read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n        self.assertTrue(ci.get(\"startedAt\") and ci.get(\"producedAt\"))\n        hand = V.hand_time_set(job, job / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\",\n                               job / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\", rec, job / \"custody\" / \"evidence\")\n        vh = {e[\"field\"]: e for e in hand}\n        for field in (\"startedAt\", \"producedAt\"):\n            self.assertIn(field, vh)\n            self.assertEqual(V.hand_key(vh[field]), V.hand_key(ht[field]))\n        v = run_verify(out, job, checkout(self.tmp))\n        self.assertEqual(v.returncode, 0, v.stdout + v.stderr)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"flag-without-source\", v.stdout)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"shadow\", v.stdout)\n\n    def test_without_the_switch_the_refusal_names_them(self):\n        tree = clean_emitter_tree(self.tmp)\n        job = copy_job(self.tmp)\n        out = self.tmp / \"rec.json\"\n        rv = run_emit(job, out, tree, allow_hand=False)\n        self.assertEqual(rv.returncode, 3, rv.stdout + rv.stderr)\n        self.assertIn(\"REFUSED (history)\", rv.stdout)\n        self.assertIn(\"[hand-time] startedAt\", rv.stdout)\n        self.assertIn(\"[hand-time] producedAt\", rv.stdout)\n        self.assertFalse(out.exists())\n```\n\nWait — the refusal lines are printed to stdout (main prints to stdout via print, not stderr). Yes: `print(f\"REFUSED (history): ...\")` → stdout. Good.\n\nCareful with the `hand_key` comparison: `V.hand_key` returns (field, file, line, source). Both should be (\"startedAt\", \"inputs/custody.json\", N, \"inputs-no-ledger\"). \n\nNow, does `V.hand_time_set` need `record`? It ignores it. Pass rec.\n\nAlso careful: `V.hand_time_set` reads `evidence_dirs(job, evidence, ci)` including the job dir → globs \"*.log\" in job dir; harmless.\n\nOne more: `hand_time_set` marks `passes.pass-a.at` too (typed in job-rejected). That's in the record. Good.\n\nNow, potential issue: in `hand_time_set`, the typed startedAt mark happens after the ledger loop; no ledger line for startedAt exists. Fine.\n\nNow let me also double check `test_run_writes_nothing` etc. remain valid.\n\nAlso existing test `test_verifier_does_not_reference_the_emitter` asserts `\"emit_custody\" not in text`. My new verifier code must not contain the string \"emit_custody\". Careful in comments! I'll avoid mentioning it. Note the module docstring says \"This file must not import the record emitter (V7)\" — no literal. Keep it that way. I must not write \"kit/emit_custody.py\" anywhere in the verifier.\n\nHmm, the current docstring doesn't contain it. Good.\n\nNow, also: the test asserts `self.assertNotIn(\"import emit\", text)` — fine.\n\nNow let me also handle: in the new test module, `V.hand_key` exists. Yes.\n\nNext: does anything else in validate_schemas break? Let me scan for verifier/spend/allowance expectations:\n\n- `res(... V1/V3/T2 a job emitted with --allow-hand-times verifies with exit 0 (no flag-without-source, no shadow) ...)` — after my changes both sides include startedAt/producedAt → still 0. \n\nBut hold on! The record emitted at 4c2444c3 for job-rejected — in validate_schemas, the roundtrip emits fresh, so the record will include the new fields. Good.\n\n- The `E2` test: `json.loads(o.read_text())[\"predicate\"][\"checks\"][0][\"at\"] == \"2026-10-20T12:00:03Z\"` — unaffected.\n\n- The T3 test: `res(... T3 with --allow-hand-times the record is written and hand_times lists the hand fields with file:line ... len(ht) >= 1 ...)` — now includes startedAt/producedAt; still passes.\n\n- `T2c a time typed into custody.json with no ledger line is named (disposition.at, its source)` — still.\n\nNow, does the emitter's hand-time refusal count change? `res((f\"{S} · T2 hand times are refused by default: exit 3, [hand-time], REFUSED (history), nothing written\", ...))` — fine.\n\nAnother: `E3` test counts `out.count(\"harness-eval-a.log#\") >= 3` — unchanged.\n\nOK. And `validate_schemas` needs no edits. I won't return it (allowed but unnecessary). The instruction says \"Return the files you change\". Fine.\n\nHmm, wait. One thing to check in validate_schemas: the `tool_built_job` removes `startedAt`/`producedAt` from custody.json, so exit 0 with empty hand_times. Still true.\n\nBut: does `tool_built_job`'s emitter run produce a record whose verifier... not verified there. Fine.\n\nNow let me write out the complete new `kit/plumbline_verify.py`.\n\nI'll carefully reproduce the file with edits.\n\nAlso one more consideration for item 1: \"Also make `finish` (line 832) never print an empty file field.\" Done.\n\nLet me also check the `check_events` findings: `{\"kind\": \"events\",