                "text": "The bug: my `make_job` signature is `make_job(tmp, ledger=None, extra=None)` where `ledger` is a folder-relative path like `\"hand/ledger.jsonl\"` — no wait, in the existing tests, `self.job(ledger=\"hand\")` passes the folder name, and `make_job` does `FIX / ledger / \"ledger.jsonl\"`. So ledger is a folder name, not a path. My `_case` passes `f\"{folder.name}/ledger.jsonl\"` which is wrong. Fix: pass `folder.name`.\n\nAlso the \"extra\" dict comprehension: `p.relative_to(FIX).as_posix()` for files in the folder (not ledger.jsonl and not record*.json). That's fine, since make_job does `shutil.copyfile(FIX / rel, dst / name)`. But careful — for the `shadow` folder, the extra file is `iteration-1-eval-record.json`, which is what `test_shadowed_inputs` does. Fine.\n\nWait — but for the `clean` folder, the extra files list may include e.g. `bar.md`? Let's check: it's fine to copy extra files into the job root; but names could collide with the job's own files. Hmm. For safety, only copy extras that don't already exist in the copied job? Actually `make_job` will just overwrite. For clean folder, extra files could be `record-*.json` (excluded) and `ledger.jsonl` (excluded). What else might exist? Unknown. Let's be conservative: filter extras to those not already present in the destination job, or just skip extras entirely except for the shadow folder's iteration record. Actually the important thing for the FAIL-line test is that the verifier runs and produces sensible output; extras aren't essential except that shadow case needs the iteration record to exist? Actually the shadow record case has `x-plumbline.s5_record`? Let's just pass extras only for the shadow folder — no, simpler: keep the extra logic but guard: only copy if destination doesn't already have that file. That avoids clobbering the job.\n\nHmm, but the shadow test needs `iteration-1-eval-record.json` at job root, which doesn't exist in the clean job, so guard works.\n\nLet me also double check: does `make_job` handle `extra` values as `FIX / rel`? Yes: `shutil.copyfile(FIX / rel, dst / name)`.\n\nAlso note: the ledger param in make_job does `FIX / ledger / \"ledger.jsonl\"`. So passing `folder.name` gives `FIX / \"hand\" / \"ledger.jsonl\"`. Good.\n\nAdditionally, `checkout(tmp)` in `_case` uses `tmp` which is a subdirectory — fine.\n\nAnother consideration: `_case` uses `tmp.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` then `make_job(tmp, ...)` which does `shutil.copytree(FIX / \"clean\" / \"job\", dst)` where dst = tmp/\"job\". Fine.\n\nAlso, the `test_allowance_finding_names_the_record` passes `\"hand/ledger.jsonl\"` — wrong too. Fix to `\"hand\"`.\n\nLet me also verify the run of the full test: \"Ran 25 tests ... FAILED (errors=6, skipped=1)\". 6 errors all from this. Fix and rerun.\n\nLet me also double check the `extra` mapping in `_case`: for `clean` folder, files include `record-bad-subject.json` etc. (excluded by `not p.name.startswith(\"record\")`). What else might be in clean/? Possibly nothing. For `events/`, `relay/`, `hand/`, `shadow/` folders — shadow has `record.json` (excluded) and `iteration-1-eval-record.json`. OK.\n\nBut wait: for shadow folder, the ledger? `shadow/` may have no ledger.jsonl. Then `make_job(tmp, None, extra)` uses the clean job's ledger. That's what test_shadowed_inputs does effectively (job=self.job(extra=...)). Good.\n\nOne more: for the `events` folder with ledger, my `_case` will use the events ledger. Good.\n\nNow let's also consider: might a FAIL line be emitted by the standard layer with file = record path? Yes `{\"kind\":\"standard\",\"field\":\"record\",\"file\":str(record),\"line\":0}` — non-empty. Good.\n\nCould any other failure have an empty file? Let's grep in plumbline_verify.py: check_schema returns schema-file paths (non-empty), record path for missing block/version. check_events: ledger path. check_spend: ledger path / lp / rp — `str(lp)` could be non-empty always. check_shadow: ledger path. check_hand_times: now record path fallback. finish() normalises. Good.\n\nAlso, for exit-2, lines start with \"NOT CHECKABLE\" — not FAIL, so test unaffected.\n\nNow also the guard in the test: `self.assertIn(rv.returncode, (0, 3, 4))`. For the clean job with a `record-no-block.json` etc, exit 4. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: running every record against the clean job might produce a `writes` failure or something odd? The snapshot compares before/after — verifier writes nothing so fine.\n\nAnother potential issue: for the `relay` folder's record.json run against the relay ledger job — that's what existing test does. Fine.\n\nNow, do I need to also keep the previously-returned fixes to kit files? Yes — return all three files again in full. The kit files were fine presumably (the test failure was only in the test). But the operator says \"Nothing you returned was kept\", so I return all three.\n\nLet me re-check whether there are other latent failures in my new tests that didn't get a chance to run because of errors. `Ran 25 tests` — the file has: VerifierKindTests (19 tests incl. skip), FailureLineTests (2), SpendWithoutSpendLineTests (1), TypedStartedProducedTests (2), CloseWiringTests (2) = 26? Let's count VerifierKindTests methods: clean_record_passes, standard_failure, wrong_checker, schema_failures, allowance_missing, allowance_present, hand_time_unflagged, flag_without_source, events_missing_generation, relay_as_approval, spend_off_by_a_cent, shadowed_inputs, json_out, run_writes_nothing, verifier_does_not_reference, call_cost, checker_needs_bar, clean_against_real_checker(skip) = 18. +2+1+2+2 = 25. OK, all ran. 6 errors, 1 skipped, rest passed (18 passed? 25-6-1=18). Good — so only the `_case` bug. Wait, `test_every_fail_line_names_a_file` produced 6 errors (6 subTest failures for folders with ledger files: hand×2 records, events×1, relay×1... plus maybe more). Anyway.\n\nHmm, but did `test_allowance_finding_names_the_record` pass? It used `make_job(self.tmp, \"hand/ledger.jsonl\")` — that would also raise NotADirectoryError. It's counted among the 6 errors. Yes.\n\nAlso `SpendWithoutSpendLineTests` and `TypedStartedProducedTests` passed — good, meaning the emitter/verifier changes work.\n\nWait — but did they run against a checkout() built in tmp? Yes. And they passed. \n\nNow fix the test file. Also, let me reconsider whether `make_job`'s extra handling could clobber. I'll add a guard: only copy if the destination doesn't already exist.\n\nActually careful: for shadow folder, the extra is `iteration-1-eval-record.json` — doesn't exist in clean job, so it copies. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: `test_shadowed_inputs` (existing) passes `extra={\"iteration-1-eval-record.json\": \"shadow/iteration-1-eval-record.json\"}` — with a guard it still works since dst doesn't have it.\n\nLet me write make_job:\n\n```python\ndef make_job(tmp: Path, ledger: str | None = None, extra: dict | None = None) -> Path:\n    dst = tmp / \"job\"\n    shutil.copytree(FIX / \"clean\" / \"job\", dst)\n    if ledger:\n        shutil.copyfile(FIX / ledger / \"ledger.jsonl\", dst / \"inputs\" / \"custody-events.jsonl\")\n    for name, rel in (extra or {}).items():\n        src = FIX / rel\n        if src.is_file() and not (dst / name).exists():\n            shutil.copyfile(src, dst / name)\n    ensure_bar(dst)\n    return dst\n```\n\nHmm, but changing existing behaviour slightly (guard) — the existing test test_shadowed_inputs expects the file copied; it isn't in the clean job, so fine.\n\nActually, wait: `make_job` is also used by existing tests with `extra=None`. Fine.\n\nBut hold on — do I want to change `make_job` at all? The existing tests use it. Adding a guard is harmless. But is the guard necessary? For `_case`, extras come from the record folder. If a folder contained e.g. a file named `packet.json`, copying would clobber. Unlikely but the guard is safe. I'll keep the guard but also restrict extras to files that look like job-level additions... Simplest: guard only.\n\nNow `_case`:\n\n```python\ndef _case(self, folder: Path, rec: Path):\n    tmp = self.tmp / f\"{folder.name}-{rec.stem}\"\n    tmp.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    ledger = folder / \"ledger.jsonl\"\n    extra = {p.name: p.relative_to(FIX).as_posix() for p in folder.iterdir()\n             if p.is_file() and p.name != \"ledger.jsonl\" and not p.name.startswith(\"record\")}\n    job = make_job(tmp, folder.name if ledger.is_file() else None, extra)\n    return run_verify(rec, job, checkout(tmp))\n```\n\nGood.\n\nAnd `test_allowance_finding_names_the_record`: `job = make_job(self.tmp, \"hand\")`.\n\nOne more check: `record.stem` for names like `record.custody.json` → `record.custody`, and `record-bad-subject.json` → `record-bad-subject`. Unique per folder. Fine.\n\nAlso I should double check the FAIL-line test's assertion `self.assertIn(rv.returncode, (0, 3, 4))` — could exit 2 occur? If the checkout is fine and bar exists (ensure_bar), no. Good.\n\nNow also verify: does `ensure_bar` get called for `_case`? make_job calls it. Yes.\n\nAnother risk: in `_case` I copy extra files into the job, e.g. for folder `clean`, are there files like `bar.md`? If so they'd be copied to job root, harmless.\n\nLet me now double-check the remaining item-2 and item-3 test logic once more since they passed. Fine.\n\nOne more thought on `FailureLineTests.test_every_fail_line_names_a_file`: for records in `clean/` that are `record-no-block.json` etc., the run uses clean ledger and clean job → exit 4 schema failures with file = schema path or record path. Good.\n\nNow, also `seen > 0` assertion: yes, failures exist.\n\nLet me write out the final files. kit/plumbline_verify.py and kit/emit_custody.py unchanged from what I returned (they passed). Return all three.\n\nActually wait — one more consideration on item 1: `finish` uses `RECORD_PATH` global; but if `main` is called via `V.main([...])` in-process, global gets set. Fine.\n\nAlso in `finish`, exit 2 messages use `f['file']` after normalisation — the fallback is RECORD_PATH for empty files. Fine.\n\nLet me make sure the test file's `_case` returns the CompletedProcess and check `rv.stdout` doesn't contain \"Traceback\" — verifier catches exceptions? If the verifier raises, stderr has traceback. The test asserts no Traceback in stderr. Should be fine.\n\nWrite the reply.",
